\subsection{Review Decision}

A [[ReviewDecision]] captures the review state of a single paper, including
its status and associated tags.

It also remembers which searches returned the paper ([[found_by]]).
A session accumulates searches, possibly several on one provider, and
the [[provider]] field alone cannot tell them apart.  [[prov found-via]]
used to fill that gap by picking one search for the provider, and so
named a query that had never returned the paper
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/128}).  Only the moment a
result is turned into decisions knows which search a paper came from,
so that is where the link is recorded (\cref{sec:review-found-by}).  A
link is the [[(query, provider, filters)]] key of the search's
[[SearchRecord]], the key that identifies a record in the session
(\cref{sec:search-record-key}), so it always resolves to the stored
query, filters and limit.  Its third part is the [[filters_key]] of the
record's filters, the empty string for an unfiltered search.  Links
saved before filters were part of the key have only the query and the
provider; they load with [[None]] in the third part unless the session
holds exactly one record with that query and provider
(\cref{sec:session-persistence}).  The list stays empty when
nothing was recorded: decisions saved before the link existed, and
papers added by snowballing, which no search returned.

An old link without filters says only that \emph{some} search of its
query and provider returned the paper (\cref{sec:session-persistence}).
Once the paper also has a resolved link for that query and provider, the
old one adds nothing a reader can use: found-via would print the
resolved command and, beside it, a \enquote{not reproducible} note about
the same query.  So a resolved link supersedes the unresolved links of
its query and provider ([[without_superseded_links]]), whenever a link
is recorded and when a file is read.

A link also knows the paper's place in the answer of its search
([[rank]], counted from one;
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/302}), so that a first
tier of the records can be screened and the rest counted
(\cref{sec:screening-tier}).  The place is taken from the provider's
own list before any merging or deduplication
(\cref{sec:answer-places} in the [[scholar]] module).  It is no part of
the link's identity: the link still names the record, and two links to
one record are one link whatever their places.  That decides the
representation.  A [[SearchLink]] is the key tuple itself, with the
place as an attribute beside it: it compares and hashes as the key, so
every place that looks a link up, unites links in a set, maps them
through a dictionary of replaced keys or compares them with a plain
tuple works as before, and a list copied with the decision
([[list(prev.found_by)]]) carries the places along without anyone
remembering them.  A separate dictionary from key to place would have
had to be copied, repointed and united by hand everywhere the list is.
The price is that a key rebuilt from its parts is a plain tuple
without a place; that happens only where a link is moved to
\emph{another} record (a legacy or merged record replaced by a newer
one), and there the place is rightly lost, since it was the place in
the other record's answer.

When one record is linked twice, by a re-run of its search that found
the paper elsewhere in the list or by two copies of a decision being
united, the smaller place is kept ([[smaller_rank]]): the paper did
stand that high in an answer of that search, and keeping the larger
would let the order of two saves decide the tier.  The kept place can
therefore come from an older run than the record now kept for the
search ([[record_rank]]): a re-run that found the paper
lower replaces the record but not the place.  That is the right
reading for a tier, which asks whether the search ever put the paper
near the top; a paper the provider ranked high once is one a screener
should see, and the newer run's lower place says only that the
ranking moved.  A link without a
place ([[None]]: made before places were kept, by a snowball, or moved
to another record) never replaces a known one.

A tuple subclass is also rebuilt by code that knows only tuples:
[[dataclasses.asdict]] calls the type with one iterable of the
elements.  So the constructor takes the three parts or one iterable of
them, and the place only by name; such a rebuilt link has no place,
which is what a plain tuple would have.  Copying and pickling pass the
place by name too ([[__getnewargs_ex__]]).

<<review data structures>>=
class SearchLink(tuple):
    """A `found_by` link, `(query, provider, filters key)`, and a place.

    Compares and hashes as the plain key tuple; `rank` is the paper's
    1-based place in the search's answer, `None` when unknown. Built
    from the three parts, or from one iterable of them.
    """

    def __new__(cls, *parts, rank: int | None = None):
        if len(parts) == 1:
            parts = tuple(parts[0])
        if len(parts) != 3:
            raise TypeError("a link has a query, a provider and a key")
        link = super().__new__(cls, parts)
        link.rank = rank
        return link

    def __getnewargs_ex__(self):
        return tuple(self), {"rank": self.rank}


def link_rank(link) -> int | None:
    """The place a `found_by` link records, `None` for a plain key."""
    return getattr(link, "rank", None)


def smaller_rank(a: int | None, b: int | None) -> int | None:
    """The smaller of two places, a known one over `None`."""
    if a is None:
        return b
    if b is None:
        return a
    return min(a, b)


def united_links(links) -> list[SearchLink]:
    """`links` without repeats, in first-seen order, smallest place kept."""
    held: dict[tuple, int | None] = {}
    for link in links:
        key = tuple(link)
        held[key] = smaller_rank(held.get(key), link_rank(link))
    return [SearchLink(*key, rank=rank) for key, rank in held.items()]


def without_superseded_links(
    links: list[tuple[str, str, str | None]],
) -> list[tuple[str, str, str | None]]:
    """Drop each unresolved link whose query and provider have a resolved one."""
    resolved = {(q, p) for q, p, key in links if key is not None}
    return [
        link for link in links
        if link[2] is not None or link[:2] not in resolved
    ]


@dataclass
class ReviewDecision:
    """
    A review decision for a single paper.

    Tracks whether the paper was kept or discarded during review,
    along with tags (themes for kept, motivations for discarded).

    Attributes:
        paper: The Paper being reviewed.
        provider: Which search provider returned this paper.
        status: Current decision status (pending/kept/discarded).
        tags: List of tags (themes for kept, motivations for discarded).
        source: Where the decision came from (human or LLM).
        is_example: True if this paper is a training example for LLM.
        llm_confidence: Confidence score from LLM (0.0-1.0), if applicable.
        llm_model, llm_decided_at, llm_prompt_version, llm_reason,
            llm_abstract_truncated: what `scholar llm classify` recorded
            about its decision (see `LLM_AUDIT_FIELDS`); `None` when
            unknown, as for decisions made before they were recorded.
        agent_reason, agent_by, agent_decided_at, agent_batch: the
            reason an AI agent gave with `sessions decide --source agent
            --reason`, who gave it, when, and the batch of work it came
            from (see `AGENT_AUDIT_FIELDS`); `None` when not recorded.
        human_decided_at, human_decided_via, human_by: when, where
            (`tui` or `decide`) and by whom (as given) a human made or
            confirmed the decision (see `HUMAN_AUDIT_FIELDS`); `None`
            when no record of the review was kept, as for decisions
            made before it was, or by code that set the source itself.
        found_by: The `(query, provider, filters key)` keys of the search
            records whose results contained the paper, in the order
            recorded; empty when not recorded (sessions saved before the
            link existed, snowballing). The filters key is `None` for an
            old link that could not be tied to one record. A link may be
            a `SearchLink`, which also holds the paper's place in that
            search's answer (`link_rank`).
        same_id_copies: Other works a search returned under this
            paper's DOI-less id, with years too far apart to be the same
            work (`{"year", "venue", "provider"}` each); see
            `is_same_id_namesake`.
        relabelled: Each time the source was corrected without changing
            the decision (`{"from", "to", "at", "note", "by",
            "is_example"}`), oldest first; see `relabel_source`.
        resets: Each time the decision was set back to pending, with
            the decision it had (`{"at", "status", "tags", "source",
            "llm_confidence", "is_example", "trace", "note", "by"}`,
            `trace` only when the classifier left one), oldest first;
            see `reset_decision`.
        abstract_set: Each time the paper's abstract was given by hand
            (`{"at", "from", "by", "chars", "text", "previous"}`,
            `previous` only when an abstract was replaced), oldest
            first; see `set_abstract`.
        abstract_lookup: What `scholar enrich` found the last time it
            asked for the paper's abstract (`{"at", "outcome", "asked",
            "failed"}`, see `ABSTRACT_LOOKUP_OUTCOMES`); `None` when no
            lookup was recorded.
        abstract_fetched: Each time `scholar enrich` stored an abstract
            a service supplied (`{"at", "from", "chars", "digest",
            "replaced"}`, `replaced` only when it replaced a doubtful
            one), oldest first; see `note_fetched_abstract`.
        screenings: Verdicts of other screeners beside the decision, and
            snapshots of the decision's own verdict taken when the first
            of them was recorded (dicts keyed by `SCREENING_KEYS`), in
            the order recorded; see `record_screening`. They never
            change the decision.
        baseline_key: The decision's key in its session's baseline,
            given when the session was loaded or saved (not saved
            itself); `None` for a decision made since. See
            `pair_with_file`.
    """
    paper: Paper
    provider: str
    status: DecisionStatus = DecisionStatus.PENDING
    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    source: ReviewSource = ReviewSource.HUMAN
    is_example: bool = False
    llm_confidence: float | None = None
    <<LLM audit fields of a decision>>
    <<agent audit fields of a decision>>
    <<human record fields of a decision>>
    found_by: list[tuple[str, str, str | None]] = field(default_factory=list)
    same_id_copies: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    relabelled: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    resets: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    abstract_set: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    abstract_lookup: dict | None = None
    abstract_fetched: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    screenings: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)
    baseline_key: str | None = field(default=None, compare=False, repr=False)

    def __post_init__(self):
        """Coerce None list fields to empty lists."""
        if self.tags is None:
            self.tags = []
        if self.found_by is None:
            self.found_by = []
        if self.same_id_copies is None:
            self.same_id_copies = []
        if self.relabelled is None:
            self.relabelled = []
        if self.resets is None:
            self.resets = []
        if self.abstract_set is None:
            self.abstract_set = []
        if self.abstract_fetched is None:
            self.abstract_fetched = []
        if self.screenings is None:
            self.screenings = []

    def add_relabel(self, record: dict) -> None:
        """Note a correction of the source (`relabel_source`), once.

        The records stay in the order of their `at` times; records of
        the same time keep the order they were added in.
        """
        if record not in self.relabelled:
            self.relabelled.append(record)
            self.relabelled.sort(key=lambda r: str(r.get("at") or ""))

    def add_reset(self, record: dict) -> None:
        """Note a reset to pending (`reset_decision`), once.

        Kept in the order of the `at` times, as `add_relabel` keeps its
        records.
        """
        if record not in self.resets:
            self.resets.append(record)
            self.resets.sort(key=lambda r: str(r.get("at") or ""))

    def add_abstract_record(self, record: dict) -> None:
        """Note an abstract given by hand (`set_abstract`), once.

        Kept in the order of the `at` times, as `add_relabel` keeps its
        records.
        """
        if record not in self.abstract_set:
            self.abstract_set.append(record)
            self.abstract_set.sort(key=lambda r: str(r.get("at") or ""))

    def add_fetched_abstract(self, record: dict) -> None:
        """Note an abstract a service supplied (`note_fetched_abstract`),
        once.

        Kept in the order of the `at` times, as `add_relabel` keeps its
        records.
        """
        if record not in self.abstract_fetched:
            self.abstract_fetched.append(record)
            self.abstract_fetched.sort(key=lambda r: str(r.get("at") or ""))

    def add_screening(self, record: dict) -> None:
        """Note a screening (`record_screening`), once by its `id`.

        Two versions of one `id` become one (`merged_screening`: the
        later relabel's correction, a set-aside mark from either); an
        entry without an id is compared whole. Kept in the order of the
        `recorded_at` times, as `add_relabel` keeps its records.
        """
        key = record.get("id") if isinstance(record, dict) else None
        for index, held in enumerate(self.screenings):
            held_key = held.get("id") if isinstance(held, dict) else None
            if key is None and held == record:
                return
            if key is not None and held_key == key:
                self.screenings[index] = merged_screening(held, record)
                return
        self.screenings.append(record)
        self.screenings.sort(key=_screening_order)

    def add_same_id_copy(self, copy: dict) -> None:
        """Note another work returned under this paper's id, once."""
        if copy not in self.same_id_copies:
            self.same_id_copies.append(copy)

    def note_same_id_copy(self, paper: Paper, provider: str) -> None:
        """Note `paper` as another work returned under this paper's id."""
        self.add_same_id_copy(
            {"year": paper.year, "venue": paper.venue, "provider": provider}
        )

    def record_search(
        self, query: str, provider: str, key: str | None,
        rank: int | None = None,
    ) -> None:
        """Note that the search `(query, provider, key)` returned this paper.

        `key` is the `filters_key` of the search's filters, or `None` for
        an old link that names no filters. `rank` is the paper's place in
        the search's answer, if known; a link held already keeps the
        smaller place.
        """
        link = (query, provider, key)
        for index, held in enumerate(self.found_by):
            if held == link:
                kept = smaller_rank(link_rank(held), rank)
                if kept != link_rank(held):
                    self.found_by[index] = SearchLink(*link, rank=kept)
                return
        self.found_by = without_superseded_links(
            self.found_by + [SearchLink(*link, rank=rank)]
        )

    def record_link(self, link: tuple) -> None:
        """Note the `found_by` link `link`, with its place if it has one."""
        self.record_search(*link, rank=link_rank(link))

    @property
    def is_decided(self) -> bool:
        """Check if a decision has been made."""
        return self.status != DecisionStatus.PENDING
    
    @property
    def is_valid(self) -> bool:
        """
        Check if the decision is valid.
        
        Discarded papers require at least one tag (motivation).
        Kept and pending papers are always valid.
        """
        if self.status == DecisionStatus.DISCARDED:
            return len(self.tags) > 0
        return True
    
    def add_tag(self, tag: str) -> None:
        """Add a tag if not already present."""
        if tag and tag not in self.tags:
            self.tags.append(tag)
    
    def remove_tag(self, tag: str) -> None:
        """Remove a tag if present."""
        if tag in self.tags:
            self.tags.remove(tag)
    
    def has_tag(self, tag: str) -> bool:
        """Check if this decision has a specific tag."""
        return tag in self.tags
    
    def clear_tags(self) -> None:
        """Remove all tags."""
        self.tags.clear()
    
    # Backward compatibility for code using 'motivation' as single string
    @property
    def motivation(self) -> str:
        """Get first tag as motivation (backward compatibility)."""
        return self.tags[0] if self.tags else ""
    
    @motivation.setter
    def motivation(self, value: str) -> None:
        """Set motivation as single tag (backward compatibility)."""
        self.tags = [value] if value else []
