\subsection{What an agent recorded}
\label{sec:agent-audit-fields}

An AI agent that screens with [[sessions decide --source agent]] makes
the same kind of call as the classifier, from the same title and
abstract, but used to leave only its verdict, its tags and perhaps a
confidence (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/249}).  A
reviewer who doubts a row of the audit table can read the classifier's
reason ([[llm_reason]], \cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}) but had nothing
to read for the agent's, and earlier screens that had exactly this gap
let a title-only rule go unnoticed.  So a decision also keeps the
agent's own trace:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[agent_reason]] ] the reason the agent gave, in its own words,
  which is what a reviewer checks the verdict against;
\item[ [[agent_by]] ] who gave it, as the agent named itself (a name or
  a role), or [[None]] when it did not say---Scholar never guesses a
  login, since a guessed name in an audit record is worse than none;
\item[ [[agent_decided_at]] ] when, in UTC (ISO 8601), since a screen
  of thousands of records runs over days and a reason written under
  one version of the review's rules should be read against those rules;
\item[ [[agent_batch]] ] the batch of work the reason came from, as
  the reasons file or [[--batch]] named it (a screen sheet's batch id,
  \cref{sec:sheet-batches} in the CLI module), or [[None]].  A
  screener's batch is the unit in which defaulted rows happen
  (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/292}): with the id
  stored, a bad batch can be found again, checked as a whole
  (\cref{sec:stored-batches}) and reset as a unit
  ([[sessions reset --batch]]).  Nothing else in the record stands for
  it: a run of [[sessions decide]] is split by verdict and may merge
  several batches.
\end{description}
They are kept apart from the classifier's fields rather than written
into [[llm_reason]]: the report's LLM screening section counts the
classifier's calls by those fields (\cref{sec:report-llm-screening}),
and an agent's reason there would be taken for a model's.  Like the
classifier's fields they default to [[None]], meaning \emph{no reason
was recorded}, and are never filled in afterwards.

<<agent audit fields of a decision>>=
agent_reason: str | None = None
agent_by: str | None = None
agent_decided_at: str | None = None
agent_batch: str | None = None
@

They travel together for the same reason as the classifier's fields,
so they are named once too.  The load, the concurrent merge and
[[decision_state]] read them through [[agent_audit_of]]; an append of
new search results keeps the stored decision whole
([[dataclasses.replace]], \cref{sec:review-found-by}), so it needs
nothing of its own.  The save writes them only when they are set
([[agent_audit_data]]): most decisions have none, and a file without
the keys is the file an older Scholar wrote.

An older Scholar reads a decision by the keys it knows, so it loads a
file with these fields without complaint; it does not know them, so it
drops them when it next saves the session, as it drops anything else
it does not know.  Every installation that saves a session must
therefore be updated before agents record reasons.

<<review data structures>>=
AGENT_AUDIT_FIELDS: tuple[str, ...] = (
    "agent_reason",
    "agent_by",
    "agent_decided_at",
    "agent_batch",
)


def agent_audit_of(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """Return the agent's recorded trace of `decision`, by name."""
    return {name: getattr(decision, name) for name in AGENT_AUDIT_FIELDS}


def agent_audit_data(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """The agent trace of `decision` as saved: only the fields set."""
    return {
        name: value for name, value in agent_audit_of(decision).items()
        if value is not None
    }
