A reason explains one verdict.  A later machine decision is a new
verdict, so it must not keep the old reason beside it: the fields are
registered with [[clear_machine_audit]] (\cref{sec:review-source}), and
the decision that carries a new reason sets them again
(\cref{sec:sessions-decide} in the CLI module).  A human decision does
not clear them, as it does not clear the classifier's: they are the
record of what the agent said, which a reviewer's confirmation or
correction is compared with.

<<review data structures>>=
MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS = MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS + AGENT_AUDIT_FIELDS
@

The reason is free text written by an agent, and the session file keeps
it for every record, about 30\,000 of them in the screens that raised
the issue.  [[MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS]] bounds it.  The classifier's
[[MAX_REASON_CHARS]] (500) caps a model's output, which Scholar asks
for and truncates; an agent writes its reason for a reader and may need
to quote the sentence of the abstract that decided it and name the rule
it applied, so the bound is four times that, about 300 words.  At the
bound, 30\,000 reasons add some 60\,MB to a session file; real reasons
are a sentence or two.  A longer reason is refused, never truncated: a
cut reason would be stored as if the agent had said only that much.

<<review data structures>>=
MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS = 2000
@

Let's check the round trip as for the classifier's fields: the fields
survive a save and a load, are left out of the file when unset, load
as unknown from a file without them, survive an append and a stale
concurrent save, and are cleared by a replacing machine decision.  A
decision that carries an agent's reason is not unscreened, so it is
never folded away as an unscreened copy (\cref{sec:session-copies}).

