\subsection{What a human's review recorded}
\label{sec:human-review-record}

A classifier's decision carries the classifier's record
(\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}) and an agent's carries the agent's
(\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}), but a human's decision carried
nothing: no time, no channel, no name.  [[human]] was the one source
that needed no evidence, and it is also the default of the dataclass,
so any code that built a decision without naming a source recorded a
human.  Issue~\#257 (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/257})
reports two sessions where scripts writing through the Python API, and
into the JSON directly, gave more than a thousand decisions the source
[[human]] that no human made.  The report's \enquote{Decided: human}
could not be told apart from \enquote{decisions of unknown origin}, and
the audit table cites a human's keep as the strongest kind there is.

A guard on the file cannot close that gap: a script that calls
[[save_session]] writes a valid file, whatever it claims.  What can be
done is to record positive evidence where a human actually decides, so
that a report can say how many of its \enquote{human} decisions carry
it.  A decision therefore keeps a record of the human's review:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[human_decided_at]] ] when the human decided or confirmed it,
  in UTC (ISO 8601);
\item[ [[human_decided_via]] ] where: [[tui]] for the review screens,
  [[decide]] for [[sessions decide --source human]];
\item[ [[human_by]] ] who, as given with [[sessions decide --by]], or
  [[None]]: as for an agent's name (\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}),
  Scholar never guesses a login.
\end{description}
The record belongs to the decision that stands.  It is set for a
human's own decision ([[human]]) and for a human's confirmation or
correction of a machine's ([[llm_reviewed]], [[agent_reviewed]]), and a
later decision by a human replaces it, time included.  The default
source is not changed here: [[human]] is also the source of every
undecided paper, and the readers of the source rely on that
(\cref{sec:reset-decision}).  What changes is that a decision without a
record can be told from one with a record.

<<human record fields of a decision>>=
human_decided_at: str | None = None
human_decided_via: str | None = None
human_by: str | None = None
@

A decision \emph{without} a record is not thereby a machine's.  Every
decision made before this record existed has none, the author's own
included, so reports say \enquote{without a record of the review},
never \enquote{not human}.  The record is evidence that a decision went
through one of the two places where a human decides, not proof of who
sat at the keyboard: a script can still set the fields, as it can set
the source.  It closes the accidental case, which is the one the issue
found.

The fields are named once and travel like the agent's: the load, the
concurrent merge and [[decision_state]] read them through
[[human_record_of]] (the comparison of two copies of a work does not:
[[decision_verdict]], \cref{sec:session-integrity}), an append of search results keeps the stored
decision whole ([[dataclasses.replace]], \cref{sec:review-found-by}),
and the save writes them only when set ([[human_record_data]]), so the
file of a session nobody reviewed since is unchanged.

<<review data structures>>=
HUMAN_AUDIT_FIELDS: tuple[str, ...] = (
    "human_decided_at",
    "human_decided_via",
    "human_by",
)


def human_record_of(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """Return the record of a human's review of `decision`, by name."""
    return {name: getattr(decision, name) for name in HUMAN_AUDIT_FIELDS}


def human_record_data(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """The human review record of `decision` as saved: only the fields set."""
    return {
        name: value for name, value in human_record_of(decision).items()
        if value is not None
    }
@

They are deliberately \emph{not} registered in [[MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS]].
That registry holds a machine's trace of its own verdict, which a
replacing machine decision clears ([[clear_machine_audit]]) and a reset
keeps in its history as the decision's [[trace]]
(\cref{sec:reset-decision}); a human's record is neither.  Who clears
it, and when, is a rule of each writer (below), not of the registry.

The record is set in exactly two places, the TUI and [[sessions decide
--source human]], and both go through [[record_human_decision]], so the
two cannot drift apart in what they write.  The function refuses a
decision that does not stand as a human's: a pending paper has not been
decided, and a machine source would be a human record on a machine's
verdict.  The time defaults to [[_utcnow]], the module's clock seam
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}); [[sessions decide]] passes one time for
its whole run, as it does for an agent's reason.  The name is stored as
given, and [[None]] replaces an earlier name: the record describes the
decision that stands, not an earlier one.

<<review data structures>>=
HUMAN_DECISION_CHANNELS: tuple[str, ...] = ("tui", "decide")
REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN: tuple[ReviewSource, ...] = (
    ReviewSource.HUMAN,
    ReviewSource.LLM_REVIEWED,
    ReviewSource.AGENT_REVIEWED,
)


def record_human_decision(
    decision: ReviewDecision,
    via: str,
    by: str | None = None,
    at: str | None = None,
) -> None:
    """Record that a human made or confirmed `decision` just now.

    `via` is where (`tui` or `decide`), `by` who (as given, never
    guessed) and `at` when (UTC ISO; now if not given). Call it after
    the decision is recorded: its status must be kept or discarded
    and its source one of `REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN`.

    Raises:
        ValueError: For another channel, a pending paper or a decision
            whose source is a machine's or unknown.
    """
    if via not in HUMAN_DECISION_CHANNELS:
        raise ValueError(
            f"unknown channel {via!r}; use one of "
            f"{', '.join(HUMAN_DECISION_CHANNELS)}"
        )
    if not decision.is_decided:
        raise ValueError("a pending paper has no decision to record")
    if decision.source not in REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN:
        raise ValueError(
            f"a {decision.source.value} decision is not a human's"
        )
    decision.human_decided_at = (
        at if at is not None else _utcnow().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    )
    decision.human_decided_via = via
    decision.human_by = by


def clear_human_record(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """Forget the human review record of `decision`; return what was set."""
    old = human_record_data(decision)
    for name in HUMAN_AUDIT_FIELDS:
        setattr(decision, name, None)
    return old
@

Every other writer of a decision has a rule of its own, so that a
record never describes a decision it does not belong to:
\begin{description}
\item[A machine decision] ([[sessions decide --source agent|llm]],
  which needs [[--force]] to replace a human's or a reviewed decision)
  clears the record (\cref{sec:sessions-decide} in the CLI module):
  the human's decision no longer stands.
\item[A relabel] ([[relabel_source]], \cref{sec:relabel-source}) moves
  the source to [[agent]] or [[llm]], so the decision is no longer a
  human's; the record goes with the label being corrected, and its old
  values are kept in the relabel record.
\item[A reset] ([[reset_decision]], \cref{sec:reset-decision}) accepts
  only unreviewed decisions, which carry no record, so it has nothing
  to clear; a human's later decision of the reset paper sets a new one.
\item[The API] (code that builds or changes a [[ReviewDecision]]
  itself) sets nothing: that is the point.
\end{description}

An older Scholar reads a decision by the keys it knows, so it loads a
file with these fields and drops them when it next saves the session,
as it drops the agent's (\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}).  This was
checked on November 20, 2026, with an installation built from commit
4ba7a23: it showed such a session and, after a [[sessions decide]] on
another paper, saved it without the three fields, the decision itself
intact.  Every installation that saves a session must be updated
before the records mean anything.

\paragraph{Counting the records.}
Reports state, for each source that claims a human, how many of its
decisions carry a record: \enquote{human: 1200 (0 with a record of the
review)}.  [[human_record_counts]] counts the decided papers with a
record per such source; [[decision_source_rows]] puts the count beside
the source's row of [[decision_source_items]] (\cref{sec:review-source}),
as a third element, the note.  The unreviewed sources get no note, since
no human claims them.  Two readers want different things.
[[sessions show]] is the working view, so it always gives the note
([[always_note]]); a report gives it only when some decision that
claims a human lacks a record, so a fully recorded session (every new
one) reads as before, and an old one says plainly what it cannot show.
The audit table and its caption are not changed: which decision a row
cites is still decided by the source (\cref{sec:audit-table}).

<<review data structures>>=
REVIEW_RECORD_NOTE = "({} with a record of the review)"


def human_record_counts(decisions) -> dict[ReviewSource, int]:
    """Count the decided papers with a human review record, per source.

    Only the sources that claim a human (`REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN`) are
    counted, each always present.
    """
    counts = dict.fromkeys(REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN, 0)
    for decision in decisions:
        if (decision.is_decided and decision.source in REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN
                and decision.human_decided_at is not None):
            counts[decision.source] += 1
    return counts


def decision_source_rows(
    decisions, always_note: bool = False,
) -> list[tuple[str, int, str]]:
    """Return `(label, count, note)` for each row of `decision_source_items`.

    The note says how many of a human source's decisions carry a record
    of the review, e.g. "(0 with a record of the review)". It is given
    for each source that claims a human and decided something, when
    `always_note` is set or any such decision lacks a record; otherwise
    it is "".
    """
    counts = decision_source_counts(decisions)
    recorded = human_record_counts(decisions)
    note_them = always_note or any(
        counts[source] > recorded[source] for source in REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN
    )
    sources = {source_label(source): source for source in counts}
    rows: list[tuple[str, int, str]] = []
    for label, count in decision_source_items(decisions):
        source = sources[label]
        note = (
            REVIEW_RECORD_NOTE.format(recorded[source])
            if note_them and count and source in REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN
            else ""
        )
        rows.append((label, count, note))
    return rows


def source_row_text(count: int, note: str) -> str:
    """The count of a source row with its note, if any."""
    return f"{count} {note}" if note else str(count)
@

Every session saved before this record existed has none, so for a while
nearly every report will say \enquote{(0 with a record of the
review)}, and a reader cannot guess what that means.  Wherever a report
gives the note, it therefore also says once what the record is and why
a decision may lack one ([[review_record_sentence]]): the LaTeX
report after its summary, its abstract, and the summary of [[llm
synthesize]].  The sentence names no version and no date, which would
age, only the two ways a decision comes to lack a record; and when
every decision has one, it is left out with the note.

<<review data structures>>=
REVIEW_RECORD_EXPLANATION = (
    "Scholar records when and through which interface a human decided; "
    "decisions without such a record were made before Scholar kept "
    "them, or were written by code that set the source itself."
)


def review_record_sentence(decisions) -> str:
    """The sentence explaining the review records, or "".

    Given when a report notes the records (`decision_source_rows`
    without `always_note`), that is when a decision that claims a
    human lacks one.
    """
    if any(note for _, _, note in decision_source_rows(decisions)):
        return REVIEW_RECORD_EXPLANATION
    return ""
@

Let's check the record: each of the two channels is tested where it
lives (the CLI and TUI modules); here we check that the setter writes
the three fields and refuses what is not a human's decision, that code
building a human decision itself gets no record, that the fields
survive a save, a load, an append and both directions of a concurrent
save, that a relabel moves them into its record, that a reset of an
unreviewed decision leaves nothing behind, and the counts.

