\subsection{Correcting who decided}
\label{sec:relabel-source}

A decision's source can be wrong while the decision itself is right.
Before [[sessions decide]] demanded [[--source]] off a terminal
(issue~\#162), an AI agent that left the option out was recorded as
[[human]], and an agent that confirmed an LLM's call the same way
turned it into [[llm_reviewed]].  Issue~\#216 reports two sessions
full of such decisions
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/216}), and reports cite
them as the author's.  The only repair [[sessions decide]] offers
is to decide again with [[--source agent --force]], and that replaces
the decision: it clears the classifier's record
([[clear_machine_audit]], \cref{sec:review-source}) and the confidence,
which are exactly what an audit of those rows would read.  So a
correction of the source is an operation of its own,
[[relabel_source]], which changes the source and leaves the decision
as it is.

Which sources may it give?  Only the unreviewed ones, [[agent]] and
[[llm]].  The three others each say that a human decided or confirmed
the decision: [[human]] by definition, and [[llm_reviewed]] and
[[agent_reviewed]] because they are what a human's review turns a
machine decision into (\cref{sec:review-source}).  A command that
writes one of them asserts a human judgement that nobody made in that
command, and a human who wants to confirm a row can do so where it is
recorded as a review, in the TUI or with [[sessions decide --source
human]].  The issue suggested mapping [[llm_reviewed]] to
[[agent_reviewed]] when the reviewer was an agent.  That would be wrong
by the meaning of the values: [[agent_reviewed]] says \enquote{an agent
proposed it and a human confirmed it}, while these rows say \enquote{an
LLM proposed it and an agent confirmed it}, which no human has looked
at.  What an agent records after reading the model's call is the
agent's own verdict, so the target is [[agent]].  The model's part is
not lost: the classifier's fields ([[llm_model]], [[llm_reason]], \dots,
\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}) and its confidence stay, and they now
describe the proposal the agent checked.  Once a human confirms such a
row in the TUI it becomes [[agent_reviewed]], which is then true.

A pending paper is never relabelled.  Its source is the default
[[human]], which says nothing because nobody decided anything, and
turning it into [[agent]] would invent an agent decision.  For the same
reason the source may not stay what it was: a relabel that changes
nothing would only add a misleading record.

Everything else stays as it is: status, tags, confidence, the
classifier's fields, [[found_by]] and the provider.  The one exception
is [[is_example]], which means \enquote{a human changed the machine's
verdict}: [[mark_as_reviewed]] sets it when a reviewer's decision
differs from the machine's, and every place that records a fresh
machine decision clears it ([[apply_llm_decisions]], a machine
[[sessions decide]]).  On a relabelled decision the flag would be
wrong.  The decision is an unreviewed machine decision again, so no
human has changed anything; yet the flag would count it as a training
example in [[llm status]], in [[ReviewSession.example_papers]] and in
the TUI's examples filter, sort it ahead of confirmed examples when
unreviewed ones are let in ([[--include-llm-examples]]), and, once a
human confirms the row unchanged, make [[machine_review_rows]] and the
CSV report the agreement as a change.  So the relabel clears the flag
and keeps its old value in the record, where an auditor can still see
that a reviewer, human or not, once changed the machine's call.
The human review record (\cref{sec:human-review-record}) goes the same
way, for the same reason: it says that a human made or confirmed the
decision, which the corrected source now denies.  The relabel clears
it and keeps the fields that were set under [[human]] in its record;
a decision without such a record gets no [[human]] key, so its relabel
record is the one written before.

Each relabel is recorded on the decision, in [[relabelled]], as
[[{"from", "to", "at", "note", "by", "is_example"}]]: the source before
and after, the UTC time, the caller's note, the caller's name for who
made or asked for the correction (both [[None]] when not given;
Scholar does not guess a user name), and the [[is_example]] flag the
decision had before.  It is a list because a source can be corrected
more than once, and each correction is an event an auditor may need to
see; nothing ever removes one.  The list is kept in time order, so that
records united from two processes (below) still read oldest first;
records of the same time keep the order they were added in.  The time
is taken from [[_utcnow]], the module's clock seam
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}).

<<review data structures>>=
RELABEL_TARGETS: tuple[ReviewSource, ...] = tuple(
    source for source in ReviewSource if source.is_unreviewed
)


def relabel_source(
    decision: ReviewDecision,
    target: ReviewSource,
    at: str,
    note: str | None = None,
    by: str | None = None,
) -> dict:
    """Set the source of `decision` to `target`, keeping the decision.

    Only the unreviewed sources (`RELABEL_TARGETS`) can be given, since
    the others claim a human's confirmation, and only to a decided paper
    whose source differs. `is_example` is cleared, since no human has
    changed the now unreviewed decision, and so is the human review
    record (`HUMAN_AUDIT_FIELDS`). The change is recorded in
    `decision.relabelled` as `{"from", "to", "at", "note", "by",
    "is_example"}` (the old flag), plus `"human"` with the record's
    fields that were set, if any, and returned.

    Raises:
        ValueError: For any other target, a pending paper, or a target
            equal to the current source.
    """
    if target not in RELABEL_TARGETS:
        raise ValueError(
            f"{target.value} claims a human's confirmation; only "
            f"{', '.join(s.value for s in RELABEL_TARGETS)} can be given"
        )
    if not decision.is_decided:
        raise ValueError("a pending paper has no decision to relabel")
    if decision.source is target:
        raise ValueError(f"the source is already {target.value}")
    record = {
        "from": decision.source.value, "to": target.value,
        "at": at, "note": note, "by": by,
        "is_example": decision.is_example,
    }
    old_verdict = _snapshot_key(decision_snapshot(decision, at))
    human = clear_human_record(decision)
    if human:
        record["human"] = human
    decision.source = target
    decision.is_example = False
    <<correct the snapshots of the relabelled verdict>>
    decision.add_relabel(record)
    return record
@

[[RELABEL_TARGETS]] is derived from [[is_unreviewed]] rather than
listing the members, for the reason given with the properties: a
source added later is a target exactly when it waits for a human.
Unknown stored values (\cref{sec:review-source}) are unreviewed too,
but they are not members the enum iterates, so none of them can be a
target: a relabel should name a source Scholar knows.  As
\emph{from}, an unknown value is fine: that is how a decision whose
source is [[manual]] gets a known one.

The record travels like the other lists a decision keeps about its
history.  It is saved only when non-empty, as [[same_id_copies]] is,
so the file of a session nobody relabelled is unchanged, and a file
without the key loads it as empty.  An append of new search results
copies it with the decision (\cref{sec:review-create-strict}).  A save
that merges another process's changes unites the two lists
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}): a relabel is a fact about the decision,
like a search that returned the paper.  When both processes relabelled
the same decision, the source follows the usual rule (this process's
decision is kept, with a warning), but both records stay, so the
history shows what each did.  It is also part of
[[decision_state]] (and of [[decision_verdict]], which compares copies),
so that two copies of a work with different
histories are not taken for equal decisions when copies are folded.
A Scholar from before this field loads such a file, since it reads only
the keys it knows, but drops the list when it next saves the session
(checked on November 19, 2026, by loading and saving a session with
the key under commit 528d933); the source itself survives, since
[[agent]] and [[llm]] are known to every version since
issue~\#162.

Let's check that a relabel changes the source and leaves the
decision as it is (the example flag moves into the record), that
it refuses every target that claims a human's confirmation, and that
the record survives a save, a load, an append and a concurrent save.

