\subsection{Setting a decision back to pending}
\label{sec:reset-decision}

A screen can turn out flawed after the fact.  Issue~\#248
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/248}) reports two
sessions screened partly by title-only rules and by an [[llm classify]]
run made before the classifier's record was kept
(\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}); the author wants to classify them again,
with a recorded trace, and then have an agent check the calls.  But
[[llm classify]] classifies pending papers only, and nothing set a
decided paper back to pending.  Re-running the searches into a new
session would change the hit counts and search dates the report states
and spend provider quota; editing the file by hand bypasses
[[save_session]], which the session-integrity work
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}) exists to prevent.  [[reset_decision]]
is the supported way: it sets one decision back to pending and keeps
what it was in the decision's history.

Which decisions may be reset?  Only those still waiting for a human:
the unreviewed machine sources [[agent]] and [[llm]], and stored
sources Scholar does not know, which count as unreviewed
(\cref{sec:review-source}).  A [[human]], [[llm_reviewed]] or
[[agent_reviewed]] decision is a human's decision or confirmation, and
undoing it is the human's to do, where it is recorded as theirs: in
the TUI, or with [[sessions decide --source human]].  A decision
wrongly recorded as a human's is first corrected with [[sessions
relabel]] (\cref{sec:relabel-source}), which makes the correction
itself part of the record.  A pending paper has nothing to reset.

The reset clears everything that belongs to the decision: the status
becomes pending; the tags go, since a pending paper's tags would read
as themes or motivations and are not a decision any more; the
confidence goes; every field registered in [[MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS]]
goes ([[clear_machine_audit]], \cref{sec:review-source}); and
[[is_example]] is cleared, since a pending paper is no training
example.  The source becomes [[human]], the default of a paper nobody
has decided.  Every reader of the source takes a pending [[human]]
paper for an unscreened one, while a pending [[llm]] paper would still
be marked \enquote{LLM, unreviewed} in [[sessions show]] and the
report, because [[unreviewed_mark]] does not look at the status.
Everything else is a fact about the paper or the searches, not about
the decision, and stays: the paper and its metadata, the provider,
[[found_by]], [[same_id_copies]], [[relabelled]], the screenings (set
aside, and counted in the reset record, \cref{sec:screenings}), and of course the
session's search records, snowball rounds, LLM samples and research
context.

Each reset is recorded on the decision, in [[resets]], as
[[{"at", "status", "tags", "source", "llm_confidence", "is_example",
"trace", "note", "by"}]]: the UTC time; the status, tags, source,
confidence and example flag the decision had; the classifier's trace,
that is the fields of [[MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS]] that were set, by name
(the key is left out when none was); and the caller's note and name
for who asked ([[None]] when not given, as in a relabel record).  The
trace is read from the registry rather than from a list of names here,
so a machine audit field registered later, say an agent's reason, is
kept in the history as soon as the reset clears it, without a change
here.  A redone screen is then auditable: the old call, with its model
and reason, stands beside the new one.  The list is kept in time
order, like [[relabelled]], and nothing removes a record.

<<review data structures>>=
def reset_decision(
    decision: ReviewDecision,
    at: str,
    note: str | None = None,
    by: str | None = None,
) -> dict:
    """Set `decision` back to pending, keeping what it was in its history.

    Only an unreviewed decision (source `agent`, `llm` or an unknown
    stored value) can be reset. Status, tags, confidence, the machine
    audit fields (`MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS`) and `is_example` are cleared
    and the source becomes `human`, the default of an undecided paper.
    The old values are recorded in `decision.resets` as `{"at",
    "status", "tags", "source", "llm_confidence", "is_example",
    "trace", "note", "by"}` (`trace` holds the audit fields that were
    set, and is left out when none was) and returned.

    Raises:
        ValueError: For a pending paper, or a decision a human made or
            confirmed (`human`, `llm_reviewed`, `agent_reviewed`).
    """
    if not decision.is_decided:
        raise ValueError("a pending paper has no decision to reset")
    if not decision.source.is_unreviewed:
        raise ValueError(
            f"a {decision.source.value} decision was made or confirmed "
            "by a human; only a human can change it"
        )
    trace = {
        name: getattr(decision, name, None)
        for name in MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS
        if getattr(decision, name, None) is not None
    }
    record = {
        "at": at,
        "status": decision.status.value,
        "tags": list(decision.tags),
        "source": decision.source.value,
        "llm_confidence": decision.llm_confidence,
        "is_example": decision.is_example,
        **({"trace": trace} if trace else {}),
        "note": note,
        "by": by,
    }
    <<count the screenings a reset sets aside>>
    decision.status = DecisionStatus.PENDING
    decision.tags = []
    decision.llm_confidence = None
    clear_machine_audit(decision)
    decision.is_example = False
    decision.source = ReviewSource.HUMAN
    decision.add_reset(record)
    return record
@

The history travels like the relabel records (\cref{sec:relabel-source}):
saved only when non-empty, so the file of a session nobody reset is
unchanged; loaded as empty from a file without the key; copied with the
decision when search results are appended
(\cref{sec:review-create-strict}); united with the file's by a save
that merges another process's changes (\cref{sec:session-integrity}),
after this process's decision state is taken, so that the union does
not look like a change made here; and part of [[decision_state]] and
[[decision_verdict]], so
that two copies with different histories are not taken for equal
decisions.  A reset paper is also not \emph{unscreened}
([[is_unscreened]], \cref{sec:session-copies}), since folding it into
a copy would drop its history.  A Scholar from before this field loads
a session with it, as a pending paper, and drops the list when it next
saves the session (checked on November 19, 2026, by loading and saving
a session with the key under commit bc8ec8c).

What other parts of Scholar make of a reset paper follows from its
being pending.  The audit table and the report list it as pending
([[audit_reason]]).  A sampled decision reset after the draw is gone
from the sample: [[llm sample --report]] counts it apart, never as
reviewed, changed or unreviewed, and the TUI does not queue it
([[reset_since_draw]], \cref{sec:llm-sample} in the LLM review module),
since whatever the paper is decided next is a call the sample never
drew.  [[first_made_by_llm]] does not count it,
since a pending paper is no decision, whoever made the one it had
(\cref{sec:llm-sample} in the LLM review module).  And [[llm classify]]
classifies it like any other pending paper.  One consequence for a
session reset as a whole: the classifier is told to choose from the
tags of the session's decided papers (plus those given with [[--tag]]),
so after every decision is reset the vocabulary has to be given again
with [[llm classify --tag]].

Let's check that a reset keeps the old decision in its history and
clears the decision itself, that a registered audit field lands in the
trace, that a human's decision or confirmation is refused, and that
the history survives a save, a load, an append and a concurrent save
by a process that loaded the session before the reset.

