\subsection{Abstracts given by hand}
\label{sec:set-abstract}

When [[scholar enrich]] finds no abstract for a record, a screener may
look the DOI up by hand, on Crossref, OpenAlex or PubMed, and find one
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/258}).  Before
[[sessions set-abstract]] (in the CLI module) the text could only go
into the decision's reason, so the record still counted as lacking an
abstract, the next screener or classifier did not see the text the
decision rested on, and nothing but that reason said where the text had
come from.  The command stores the text as the paper's abstract and
records, on the decision, where it was found.

The abstract itself is a field of the paper like any other, so
everything that reads abstracts (the classifier, the TUI, the reports)
sees it without a change.  The record of its origin is on the
decision, in [[abstract_set]], not on the [[Paper]]: a new paper field
would need its own rule in the three-way merge of paper metadata
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}) and in every merge of two copies of a
paper, while a list on the decision travels like the reset history
(\cref{sec:reset-decision}).  An older Scholar then keeps the abstract,
which is an ordinary paper field to it, and drops only the record.
Each record is [[{"at", "from", "by", "chars", "text", "previous"}]]:
the UTC time; where the text was found, as the caller wrote it
(\enquote{Crossref, 2026-11-20}); who gave it ([[None]] when not
given, never guessed, as in a reset record); the length and the text
itself as stored; and the abstract it replaced, only when one was
replaced.  The record holds the text, not only its length, because the
paper's abstract can be replaced afterwards (below), and then the text
a decision rested on would otherwise be in no file at all; with it, a
reader can also tell exactly whether the abstract the paper holds now
is the one the record describes.  Abstracts are given by hand for a
few records of a session, so the copies cost little space.

The text is cleaned as provider text is at ingest
([[clean_metadata_text]], \cref{sec:clean-provider-text} in the
utilities module): a text copied from a publisher's page or an API
response carries the same entities, inline tags and mojibake.  White
space is collapsed to single spaces, since a text copied from a PDF
breaks its lines wherever the column ended.  A text that is empty after
cleaning is refused: it would set nothing but a record.  There is no
upper limit, since abstracts vary in length, but the command warns
above [[LONG_ABSTRACT_CHARS]] characters, where the text is more likely
a whole page than an abstract.

<<review data structures>>=
LONG_ABSTRACT_CHARS = 10_000


def clean_abstract_text(text: str) -> str:
    """Clean an abstract given by hand as provider text is cleaned.

    Entities are decoded, inline markup stripped and mojibake repaired
    (`clean_metadata_text`); white space is collapsed to single spaces.
    """
    return " ".join(clean_metadata_text(text).split())
@

A paper can be held by more than one decision of a session: copies of
one work decided apart under one id (\cref{sec:session-copies}), which
share one [[Paper]] object once the session is loaded.  The abstract is
a fact about the paper, so [[set_abstract]] takes every decision that
holds it and gives each paper the text and each decision the record.
A paper that already has an abstract is refused unless [[replace]] is
given: the stored text may be what a decision rested on, and replacing
it silently would make that decision read as if it rested on the new
one.  With [[replace]] the old text is kept in the record.

<<review data structures>>=
def set_abstract(
    decisions: list[ReviewDecision],
    text: str,
    origin: str,
    at: str,
    by: str | None = None,
    replace: bool = False,
) -> dict:
    """Give the paper of `decisions` the abstract `text`, found at `origin`.

    `decisions` are the decisions holding one paper (copies of one work
    under one id). The text is cleaned (`clean_abstract_text`); every
    decision gets the record `{"at", "from", "by", "chars", "text",
    "previous"}` in `abstract_set` (`text` is the stored text;
    `previous`, the replaced abstract, only when one was replaced),
    which is returned.

    Raises:
        ValueError: For an empty text or origin, or a paper that has an
            abstract when `replace` is false.
    """
    cleaned = clean_abstract_text(text)
    if not cleaned:
        raise ValueError("the abstract is empty after cleaning")
    if not origin.strip():
        raise ValueError("say where the abstract was found")
    previous = next(
        (d.paper.abstract for d in decisions if d.paper.abstract), None
    )
    if previous and not replace:
        raise ValueError("the paper already has an abstract")
    record = {
        "at": at,
        "from": origin.strip(),
        "by": by,
        "chars": len(cleaned),
        "text": cleaned,
        **({"previous": previous} if previous else {}),
    }
    for decision in decisions:
        decision.paper.abstract = cleaned
        decision.add_abstract_record(dict(record))
    return record
@

A record says that the abstract the paper holds came from where it
says, so it must not outlive the text unnoticed.  Enrichment from
providers does not replace a stored abstract: [[enrich]] asks for an
abstract only when the paper lacks one ([[needs_enrichment]] in the
enrichment module), and its merge with a provider's copy keeps the
stored values ([[Paper.merge_with]]).  Two things can replace it.  One
is a fold of two copies ([[sessions reconcile]], and a save that
settles a copy another process folded, \cref{sec:session-copies}): the
survivor takes the DOI copy's abstract, filled from the other copy
only when it has none ([[merge_version_of]]).  So the records always go to
the survivor, with their texts: in the usual case, a DOI-less copy
given the abstract by hand and its DOI twin without one, the text
becomes the survivor's abstract and the record describes it.  When
the twin had an abstract of its own, that one is kept, as a fold keeps
it for any other filled-in field; the hand text then survives in the
record, which [[hand_abstract_replaced]] (below) marks as replaced, and
a warning names the copy and where the text was from.

<<review data structures>>=
def abstract_records_after_fold(
    paper: Paper, copies: list[tuple[str, str | None, list[dict]]]
) -> list[dict]:
    """The abstract records of folded copies, united, oldest first.

    `copies` gives each copy's paper id, the abstract it had before the
    fold and its records. A copy whose abstract `paper` does not hold
    is named in a warning; its records are kept, with their texts.
    """
    united: list[dict] = []
    for paper_id, abstract, records in copies:
        if not records:
            continue
        united.extend(r for r in records if r not in united)
        if not abstract or abstract != paper.abstract:
            logger.warning(
                "%s: the abstract added by hand (from %s) was not kept; "
                "the copy it was folded with has an abstract of its own "
                "(the text stays in the record)",
                paper_id, records[-1].get("from"),
            )
    return sorted(united, key=lambda r: str(r.get("at") or ""))
@

The other is a preprint adoption (\cref{preprint-module}): when
enrichment finds no PDF and replaces the entry with a preprint, the
preprint's own abstract wins ([[_adopt]]), since the entry then stands
for the preprint.  The record stays, since it is still true that an
abstract was given then, from there, but it no longer describes the
text.  [[hand_abstract_replaced]] tells the displays so, by comparing
the record's text with the paper's abstract.  A record without a text
(written while the record held only the length, during the review of
this change) is compared by its length.  The adoption is left as it
is, since which version's abstract an adopted entry should carry is a
question for the preprint fallback, not for this record; the hand text
stays in the record either way.

<<review data structures>>=
def hand_abstract_replaced(decision: ReviewDecision) -> bool:
    """Whether the abstract last given by hand has since been replaced.

    False when no abstract was given by hand; otherwise true when the
    paper's abstract is not the last record's text (a record without a
    text: not of the length it noted).
    """
    if not decision.abstract_set:
        return False
    last = decision.abstract_set[-1]
    if "text" in last:
        return last["text"] != decision.paper.abstract
    return last.get("chars") != len(decision.paper.abstract or "")
@

