The record travels like the reset history: saved only when non-empty,
so the file of a session nobody gave an abstract is unchanged; loaded
as empty from a file without the key; copied with the decision when
search results are appended (\cref{sec:review-create-strict}), where
the stored abstract also stays, since the stored paper's values win
([[stored_paper_with]]); and united with the file's by a save that
merges another process's changes (\cref{sec:session-integrity}).

Unlike the reset history, it is \emph{not} part of [[decision_state]].
A reset changes the decision; giving an abstract does not.  The merge
takes the whole decision from the file when this process's decision
state is still the baseline's, and keeps this process's when it
differs.  If the record were part of that state, a [[set-abstract]]
run that loaded the session before another process saved a human's
decision would hold a changed state, and its save would put the old
pending decision back over the human's.  Left out, the record is
united on its own and the decision follows the usual rule.  For the
same reason a hand-given abstract does not make a pending copy
screened ([[is_unscreened]]): it can still be folded into its twin,
its record going along as described above.

The abstract itself is a paper field and follows the paper-metadata
merge: a field this process changed keeps its value, a field it left
as loaded takes the file's.  Two races need more than that, because
the rule does not know that one of the two values was given by hand.
\begin{description}
\item[A stale enrichment] A process loads the session, [[set-abstract]]
  saves a hand text, and the first process fills the paper's abstract
  from a provider (it had none when loaded) and saves.  Both changed
  the field, so the usual rule keeps the later save's provider text,
  while the record is united: the record would vouch for a text the
  paper does not hold, and the hand text would be in no file.  So when
  the file holds a hand record this process lacks, and the file's
  abstract is that record's text, the file's abstract is taken,
  whatever this process did to the field.  The user gave the text on
  purpose, after the provider's had been looked for; an automatic fill
  must not undo that.  If this process gave a hand text of its own as
  well, both were given on purpose, and the usual rule decides.
\item[A stale [[set-abstract]] ] The reverse: [[set-abstract]] loads a
  paper without an abstract, another process fills one and saves, and
  [[set-abstract]] saves its text.  Its text is kept (the user gave it
  on purpose, so a provider's text does not displace it), but it
  replaced an abstract without [[--replace]], which the command would
  have refused.  So the replaced abstract is written into the record's
  [[previous]], as [[--replace]] would have kept it: when the file's
  abstract differs from the one this process loaded and from ours, and
  this process added a hand record, the newest of its records gets the
  file's abstract as [[previous]].  A [[None]] on disk is a gap, not an
  abstract, and replaces nothing.
\end{description}
The records this process and the file hold apart ([[mine_new]],
[[their_new]]) are taken before the file's records are united into
ours.

<<settle the abstract against the file's hand records>>=
