\subsection{Abstract lookups by enrichment}
\label{sec:abstract-lookup}

A record without an abstract can mean that no source holds one, or
that nobody asked.  [[scholar enrich]] tells the two apart for the
papers of one run ([[LookupOutcome]], \cref{sec:enrich-lookup-outcomes}
in the enrichment module), but used to keep nothing of it, so a
screener deciding a record weeks later could not tell either; records
were screened on titles alone before anyone had run the enrichment
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/293}).  The decision
therefore keeps, in [[abstract_lookup]], what [[scholar enrich]] found
the last time it asked for the paper's abstract:
[[{"at", "outcome", "asked", "failed"}]], the UTC time of the run, one
of [[ABSTRACT_LOOKUP_OUTCOMES]], the sources asked (left out when none
was) and the failed ones with their reasons (left out when none
failed).  The outcomes are those of the run's tally
(\cref{sec:enrich-lookup-outcomes}):
\begin{description}
\item[ [[found]] ] the run filled the abstract;
\item[ [[none-found]] ] every source asked answered, and none had an
  abstract;
\item[ [[failed]] ] at least one lookup failed, so the abstract may be
  there for the asking;
\item[ [[no-doi]] ] nothing could be asked, the paper has no DOI;
\item[ [[none-asked]] ] the paper has a DOI, but every source declined
  or none was available.
\end{description}
The enrichment module once said that the outcome belongs to the run,
since a stored \enquote{failed} goes stale when the next run is
answered.  The record carries its time for that reason and holds only
the latest attempt: a later run replaces it, and a reader sees when the
answer was given.  It is one dictionary, not a history like
[[abstract_set]], because only the latest attempt says anything about
the record as it stands.

It is set by [[scholar enrich]] only, for the papers it asked an
abstract for.  Like [[abstract_set]] (\cref{sec:set-abstract}) it lives
on the decision, not on the [[Paper]], so that the three-way merge of
paper metadata needs no rule for it, and for the same reason it is not
part of [[decision_state]] or of [[is_unscreened]]: an enrichment run
that loaded the session before a human decided a paper must not, by
holding a changed record, put the old pending decision back over the
human's in its save.  It is saved only when set, loaded as [[None]] from
a file without it, copied with the decision on an append, and in a
concurrent save the newer of the two records wins
([[later_abstract_lookup]]), since both describe the same paper and
the later attempt replaces the earlier.  Sessions written before it
existed have no record: that says nothing about whether a lookup was
made, and the counts below call it \enquote{no record of a lookup},
never \enquote{never looked up}.

<<review data structures>>=
ABSTRACT_LOOKUP_OUTCOMES: tuple[str, ...] = (
    "found", "none-found", "failed", "no-doi", "none-asked",
)


def later_abstract_lookup(
    ours: dict | None, theirs: dict | None
) -> dict | None:
    """The later of two abstract lookup records; `ours` on a tie."""
    if not isinstance(theirs, dict):
        return ours
    if not isinstance(ours, dict):
        return theirs
    if str(theirs.get("at") or "") > str(ours.get("at") or ""):
        return theirs
    return ours
@

A fold of two copies (\cref{sec:session-copies}) keeps the DOI copy's
record: the survivor takes the DOI copy's paper, and the DOI-less
copy's record can only say [[no-doi]], which is no longer true of it.

\paragraph{Counting decisions made without an abstract.}
The counts are made over a set of decisions, one per paper: the
records a command has just decided or screened, or every decided paper
of a session.  A paper without an abstract (none, or only white space)
is sorted by its record: [[none-found]], [[failed]], not asked
([[no-doi]] and [[none-asked]]) or no record.  A record of a
[[found]] abstract on a paper that has none (the abstract was replaced
since, by a preprint's adoption, say) does not describe the paper as it
stands, so it counts as no record, as does an outcome this version
does not know.

A stored abstract may also not be one: a conflict-of-interest
statement or a publisher's boilerplate kept as the abstract
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/287}).  A decision on
such a record rests on its title as much as one on a record without an
abstract, so a paper whose abstract [[abstract_doubt]]
(\cref{sec:fetched-abstract}) doubts is counted too, apart from the
missing ones.  [[abstract_doubt]] is used rather than the text check
itself, since it applies the rule that a text given by hand is never
judged.

\paragraph{Two records of enrichment.}
[[abstract_fetched]] (\cref{sec:fetched-abstract}) and
[[abstract_lookup]] answer different questions and sit side by side.
[[abstract_fetched]] says where the stored abstract came from: one
entry each time [[scholar enrich]] stored a service's abstract, with a
digest that tells whether it still describes the text, and so nothing
when enrichment stored nothing.  [[abstract_lookup]] says when
[[scholar enrich]] last asked for the abstract and what came of it,
also, and mostly, when nothing was stored.  A doubtful abstract the run
judged was asked about like a missing one: replaced by a source's real
abstract, the paper gets both a [[found]] lookup and an
[[abstract_fetched]] entry; kept because no source had one, it gets the
lookup outcome of the sources asked ([[none-found]], [[failed]], ...)
and no entry, since nothing was stored.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class AbstractGap:
    """How many of some decided papers lack an abstract, and why.

    `papers` counts the papers, `without` those without an abstract,
    split by their lookup record into `none_found`, `failed`,
    `not_asked` and `no_record`; `doubtful` counts papers whose
    abstract `abstract_doubt` doubts.
    """

    papers: int = 0
    without: int = 0
    none_found: int = 0
    failed: int = 0
    not_asked: int = 0
    no_record: int = 0
    doubtful: int = 0


def has_abstract(paper: Paper) -> bool:
    """Whether `paper` holds an abstract that is not only white space."""
    return bool((paper.abstract or "").strip())


def abstract_gap(decisions) -> AbstractGap:
    """Count the papers of `decisions` without an abstract, by lookup."""
    counts = dict.fromkeys(
        ("without", "none_found", "failed", "not_asked", "no_record",
         "doubtful"), 0,
    )
    seen: dict[str, ReviewDecision] = {}
    for decision in decisions:
        seen.setdefault(get_paper_id(decision.paper), decision)
    for decision in seen.values():
        if has_abstract(decision.paper):
            counts["doubtful"] += abstract_doubt(decision) is not None
            continue
        counts["without"] += 1
        record = decision.abstract_lookup
        outcome = record.get("outcome") if isinstance(record, dict) else None
        if outcome == "none-found":
            counts["none_found"] += 1
        elif outcome == "failed":
            counts["failed"] += 1
        elif outcome in ("no-doi", "none-asked"):
            counts["not_asked"] += 1
        else:
            counts["no_record"] += 1
    return AbstractGap(papers=len(seen), **counts)
@

A doubtful abstract (\cref{sec:fetched-abstract}) is counted apart from
a missing one, through [[abstract_doubt]], so that a text given by hand
is never judged, however much it looks like something else:

