\subsection{Screenings: verdicts beside the decision}
\label{sec:screenings}

A session holds one decision per paper, and a review that screens its
records twice, a classifier's screen checked by an agent or by a second
human, say, has nowhere to put the second verdict
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/253}).  A machine
[[sessions decide]] replaces the first decision and clears its trace,
and a human's turns it into a reviewed one ([[mark_as_reviewed]]), so
after either the first verdict is gone.  Without both verdicts nothing
can list where two screeners disagree or say how often they agree.

So a decision keeps a list of \emph{screenings} beside it,
[[screenings]]: each is one screener's verdict of the paper (kept or
discarded, with tags, confidence and reason), recorded by a screening
command and never by deciding.  The decision's status, tags and source
stay the outcome that counts: nothing reads the list to decide what is
kept, cited or counted as decided.  This section holds the record and
its rules; [[sessions screen]] (in the CLI module) records agents',
humans' and relayed classifiers' verdicts.  How agreement is computed
from the list, how conflicts are listed and how the report states them
are later parts of the issue.

\paragraph{One record.}
Every record has the same keys, all present when saved and [[None]]
when unknown, so a reader never has to guess what a missing key meant:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[id]] ] a random hex string ([[uuid4]]); the record's identity
  when two processes' lists are united (below).
\item[ [[origin]] ] [[screen]] for a verdict a screening command
  recorded, [[decision]] for a snapshot of the decision's own verdict
  (below).
\item[ [[kind]], [[name]] ] the screener: [[llm]], [[agent]] or
  [[human]], and its name as given (the model for a classifier), or
  [[None]]; Scholar does not guess a name, as for an agent's reason
  (\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}).
\item[ [[confirmed_from]], [[confirmed_name]] ] for a snapshot of a
  human's confirmation of a machine's call, which kind of machine
  ([[llm]], [[agent]]) proposed it and its name; [[None]] otherwise.
\item[ [[status]], [[tags]] ] the verdict, [[kept]] or [[discarded]]
  (pending is no verdict), and its tags as given.  The category is not
  stored apart: it is [[audit_category]] of the tags when read, as
  everywhere else, so one rule decides it.
\item[ [[confidence]], [[reason]] ] as the screener gave them.  A
  reason is stored whole, under the agent's limit
  ([[MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS]]).
\item[ [[at]], [[recorded_at]] ] when the verdict was made, when known,
  and when Scholar stored it (both UTC, ISO 8601).
\item[ [[prompt_version]], [[abstract_truncated]] ] a classifier's
  record (\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}), for classifier verdicts only.
\item[ [[blind]], [[blind_basis]] ] whether the screener worked without
  seeing the other verdicts ([[True]], [[False]], or [[None]] for
  unknown), and on what ground Scholar says so: [[prompt]] (Scholar
  built the classifier's prompt), [[tui]] (a blind review screen) or
  [[declared]] (the caller said so).  For an agent, which can read the
  session file, it can only be declared.
\item[ [[sample_id]] ] the review sample (\cref{sec:llm-sample-record})
  the screen was made for, or [[None]].
\item[ [[batch]] ] the batch of work the verdict came from, as the
  verdicts file or [[--batch]] named it (a screen sheet's batch id,
  \cref{sec:sheet-batches} in the CLI module), or [[None]]; for a
  snapshot, the [[agent_batch]] of an agent's decision
  (\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}).  The batch check groups stored
  verdicts by it (\cref{sec:stored-batches}).
\item[ [[via]] ] where it was recorded: [[screen]], [[classify]],
  [[tui]], or [[snapshot]] for a snapshot.
\end{description}
[[_screening_record]] builds a record in that key order and refuses a
key that is not one of them, so a writer added later cannot slip a
field past the list.

<<review imports>>=
import uuid
<<review data structures>>=
SCREENING_KEYS: tuple[str, ...] = (
    "id", "origin", "kind", "name", "confirmed_from", "confirmed_name",
    "status", "tags", "confidence", "reason", "at", "recorded_at",
    "prompt_version", "abstract_truncated", "blind", "blind_basis",
    "sample_id", "batch", "via",
)
SCREENING_KINDS: tuple[str, ...] = ("llm", "agent", "human")
SCREENING_STATUSES: tuple[str, ...] = ("kept", "discarded")
SCREENING_BLIND_BASES: tuple[str, ...] = ("prompt", "tui", "declared")
SCREENING_VIAS: tuple[str, ...] = ("screen", "classify", "tui", "snapshot")


def _screening_record(**values) -> dict:
    """A screening with every key of `SCREENING_KEYS`, in that order."""
    unknown = set(values) - set(SCREENING_KEYS)
    if unknown:
        raise ValueError(f"not a screening key: {', '.join(sorted(unknown))}")
    return {key: values.get(key) for key in SCREENING_KEYS}
@

A screening command builds its verdicts with [[new_screening]].  It
checks what a caller could get wrong and a reader would trust: the
kind, the status, the channel and the ground of blindness are each one
of a few values, a confidence lies between 0 and~1 like [[-c]] of
[[sessions decide]], and a reason is not longer than an agent's.  A
verdict made through a command has no time of its own but the run's,
so [[at]] defaults to [[recorded_at]], as an agent's reason takes the
time of its [[sessions decide]] run.

<<review data structures>>=
def new_screening(
    *,
    kind: str,
    status: str,
    tags: list[str],
    recorded_at: str,
    name: str | None = None,
    confidence: float | None = None,
    reason: str | None = None,
    at: str | None = None,
    prompt_version: str | None = None,
    abstract_truncated: bool | None = None,
    blind: bool | None = None,
    blind_basis: str | None = None,
    sample_id: str | None = None,
    via: str = "screen",
    batch: str | None = None,
) -> dict:
    """A screener's verdict, as a screening command records it.

    `at` (when the verdict was made) defaults to `recorded_at`.

    Raises:
        ValueError: For a kind, status, channel or ground of blindness
            that is not one of `SCREENING_KINDS`, `SCREENING_STATUSES`,
            `SCREENING_VIAS` (`snapshot` excepted) and
            `SCREENING_BLIND_BASES`, a confidence outside [0, 1] or a
            reason longer than `MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS`.
    """
    if kind not in SCREENING_KINDS:
        raise ValueError(f"unknown screener kind {kind!r}")
    if status not in SCREENING_STATUSES:
        raise ValueError(f"a screening is kept or discarded, not {status!r}")
    if via not in SCREENING_VIAS or via == "snapshot":
        raise ValueError(f"unknown channel {via!r}")
    if blind_basis is not None and blind_basis not in SCREENING_BLIND_BASES:
        raise ValueError(f"unknown ground of blindness {blind_basis!r}")
    if confidence is not None and not 0.0 <= confidence <= 1.0:
        raise ValueError(f"confidence {confidence} is not between 0 and 1")
    if reason is not None and len(reason) > MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS:
        raise ValueError(
            f"reason of {len(reason)} characters, over the limit of "
            f"{MAX_AGENT_REASON_CHARS}"
        )
    return _screening_record(
        id=uuid.uuid4().hex, origin="screen", kind=kind, name=name,
        status=status, tags=list(tags), confidence=confidence,
        reason=reason, at=at if at is not None else recorded_at,
        recorded_at=recorded_at, prompt_version=prompt_version,
        abstract_truncated=abstract_truncated, blind=blind,
        blind_basis=blind_basis, sample_id=sample_id, batch=batch, via=via,
    )
@

\paragraph{The decision's verdict as the first screen.}
A second verdict is compared with the first, and the first is the
decision's.  But the decision does not stay what the first screener
said: a human's review overwrites the machine's status when it
disagrees ([[mark_as_reviewed]]), so comparing a second screen with
the live decision after an adjudication would compare the second
screener with an outcome that screener helped bring about, and the
two would look more in agreement than they were.  So when a screening
is recorded for a decided paper, Scholar first copies the decision's
current verdict into the list as a screening of its own,
[[origin: "decision"]], [[via: "snapshot"]].  A later change of the
decision leaves the record of what the first screener said.

Who that verdict is from follows the source.  An unreviewed classifier
or agent decision is that machine's verdict: kind [[llm]] or
[[agent]], named by [[llm_model]] or [[agent_by]], with its
confidence, its reason and its time, for a classifier its prompt
version and truncation flag, and for an agent its batch.  A decision
a human made, confirmed or changed ([[human]], [[llm_reviewed]],
[[agent_reviewed]]: the sources of [[REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN]]) is the
human's verdict: when the human changed the machine's call, the status is the human's, and when
they confirmed it, they made it theirs.  So its kind is [[human]],
named by [[human_by]] and timed by [[human_decided_at]] of the human's
record (\cref{sec:human-review-record}), while [[confirmed_from]] and
[[confirmed_name]] say which machine proposed it, so that a report can
call it the human-confirmed decision.  The machine's confidence and
reason are not copied into it: they describe the machine's call, not
the human's, and they are still on the decision.  A decision whose
stored source Scholar does not know (\cref{sec:review-source}) gives a
snapshot of kind [[None]], which no comparison will pair.

<<review data structures>>=
SNAPSHOT_FIELDS: tuple[str, ...] = (
    "kind", "name", "confirmed_from", "confirmed_name", "confidence",
    "reason", "at", "prompt_version", "abstract_truncated", "batch",
)


def verdict_screener(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """Who made the decision's verdict, as a snapshot records it.

    The keys of `SNAPSHOT_FIELDS`, `None` where unknown or not the
    screener's own (a human's confirmation carries no machine
    confidence or reason).
    """
    fields = dict.fromkeys(SNAPSHOT_FIELDS)
    source = decision.source
    if source in REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN:
        confirmed = {ReviewSource.LLM_REVIEWED: "llm",
                     ReviewSource.AGENT_REVIEWED: "agent"}.get(source)
        fields.update(
            kind="human", name=decision.human_by,
            confirmed_from=confirmed,
            confirmed_name={"llm": decision.llm_model,
                            "agent": decision.agent_by}.get(confirmed),
            at=decision.human_decided_at,
        )
    elif source is ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED:
        fields.update(
            kind="llm", name=decision.llm_model,
            confidence=decision.llm_confidence,
            reason=decision.llm_reason, at=decision.llm_decided_at,
            prompt_version=decision.llm_prompt_version,
            abstract_truncated=decision.llm_abstract_truncated,
        )
    elif source is ReviewSource.AGENT_UNREVIEWED:
        fields.update(
            kind="agent", name=decision.agent_by,
            confidence=decision.llm_confidence,
            reason=decision.agent_reason, at=decision.agent_decided_at,
            batch=decision.agent_batch,
        )
    return fields


def decision_snapshot(decision: ReviewDecision, recorded_at: str) -> dict:
    """The decision's current verdict as a screening (`origin: decision`)."""
    return _screening_record(
        id=uuid.uuid4().hex, origin="decision",
        status=decision.status.value, tags=list(decision.tags),
        recorded_at=recorded_at, via="snapshot",
        **verdict_screener(decision),
    )
@

One snapshot serves every screening of the same verdict.  Before
taking one, [[record_screening]] looks for a snapshot already counted
(below) with the same status, tags, screener and time, and takes none
if there is one: a third screener compared with the decision meets the
same first verdict as the second did.  A changed decision (a machine
[[sessions decide]], a human's adjudication) differs in one of these,
so the next screening snapshots the new verdict, and the old snapshot
stays as the record of what was there before.  Nothing snapshots a
decision when it changes: most sessions never record a screening, and
copying every machine decision into the list from now on would about
double the size of every session for a feature it may never use, while
old decisions would still need the snapshot.  A pending paper has no
verdict, so its screening is recorded without a snapshot.

<<review data structures>>=
def _snapshot_key(record: dict) -> tuple:
    """What makes two snapshots one verdict: status, tags, screener, time."""
    return (
        record.get("status"), tuple(record.get("tags") or ()),
        record.get("kind"), record.get("name"),
        record.get("confirmed_from"), record.get("confirmed_name"),
        record.get("at"),
    )


def record_screening(decision: ReviewDecision, record: dict) -> dict | None:
    """Add the screening `record` to `decision`, after its verdict.

    A decided paper's current verdict is added first as a snapshot
    (`decision_snapshot`), unless an equal one is already counted
    (`counted_screenings`). The decision itself is not changed.
    Returns the snapshot taken, or None.
    """
    snapshot = None
    if decision.is_decided:
        candidate = decision_snapshot(decision, record["recorded_at"])
        if not any(
            held.get("origin") == "decision"
            and _snapshot_key(held) == _snapshot_key(candidate)
            for held in counted_screenings(decision)
        ):
            decision.add_screening(candidate)
            snapshot = candidate
    decision.add_screening(record)
    return snapshot
@

\paragraph{Which records count.}
Three kinds of entries are kept but not counted.  An entry without an
[[id]], or without a status that is [[kept]] or [[discarded]], comes
from a hand edit or a writer that broke the format; it is kept as it
is, so that a save never loses what was there, skipped by every reader,
and warned about once per file and process
([[warn_about_malformed_screenings]], as for unknown sources).  And a
reset (\cref{sec:reset-decision}) sets aside every screening recorded
before it: the reset says the decision was flawed and will be made
again, and the snapshot of that decision, and the verdicts compared
with it, describe a screen that no longer stands.  They stay in the
list as history; [[counted_screenings]] keeps only those recorded after
the last reset, comparing the times as times, as [[reset_since_draw]]
does for samples (\cref{sec:llm-sample} in the LLM review module).  A
screening recorded in the very second of the reset counts as set
aside: the stored times have no finer grain to tell the order.  The
third kind is a screening made at the same time as a reset by another
process, which the time cannot tell from one made after it; the merge
marks it with [[set_aside_at]] (below), and a marked record is not
counted either.

<<review data structures>>=
def is_valid_screening(record) -> bool:
    """Whether `record` is a screening a reader can count."""
    return (
        isinstance(record, dict) and bool(record.get("id"))
        and record.get("status") in SCREENING_STATUSES
    )


def _iso_after(at: str, since: str) -> bool:
    """Whether the time `at` is later than `since` (both ISO 8601)."""
    try:
        return datetime.fromisoformat(at) > datetime.fromisoformat(since)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return at > since


def counted_screenings(decision: ReviewDecision) -> list[dict]:
    """The valid screenings of `decision` recorded since its last reset."""
    valid = [
        r for r in decision.screenings
        if is_valid_screening(r) and not r.get("set_aside_at")
    ]
    if not decision.resets:
        return valid
    last = str(decision.resets[-1].get("at") or "")
    return [
        r for r in valid
        if _iso_after(str(r.get("recorded_at") or ""), last)
    ]


def _screening_order(record) -> str:
    """Sort key of the list: the time the screening was recorded."""
    if not isinstance(record, dict):
        return ""
    return str(record.get("recorded_at") or "")
<<review data structures>>=
_WARNED_MALFORMED_SCREENINGS: set[str] = set()


def warn_about_malformed_screenings(session_path: Path, session) -> None:
    """Warn once that `session` holds screenings no reader can count."""
    count = sum(
        1 for d in session.decisions for r in d.screenings
        if not is_valid_screening(r)
    )
    if not count or str(session_path) in _WARNED_MALFORMED_SCREENINGS:
        return
    _WARNED_MALFORMED_SCREENINGS.add(str(session_path))
    logger.warning(
        "%s: %d screening(s) without an id or a kept/discarded status; "
        "they are kept as they are and skipped",
        session_path.name, count,
    )
@

\paragraph{Who a screener is.}
A comparison of screeners has to know which records are one
screener's, and of each screener's records of a paper only the latest
counts.  For an agent or a human the kind and the name tell screeners
apart, and [[sessions screen]] refuses a screener who would screen a
decision under the decision's own identity, since that record would
replace the first verdict.  A classifier needs one thing more.
[[llm classify --second-screen]] (in the CLI module) runs a model as a
second screener, and the model that made the decision may be the one
run again: a review may have only one model at hand.  That run is a
screen of its own, a new request with a prompt that holds no verdict of
the paper, and its answer need not be the first one: Vembye et al.\
found that the screening decisions of GPT models, like those of
humans, \enquote{are not 100\% consistent across screenings}
(Psychological Methods, 2025, accepted manuscript p.~39).  Recorded
under kind [[llm]] and the model's name alone, it would share the
identity of the snapshot of the decision it screens, and the
latest-record rule would hide the first verdict, and with it any
disagreement.  Refusing the run, as [[sessions screen]] refuses its
own case, would lose a screen the review asked for; a second screen by
the same model is recorded, and the reports say that it was the same
model.

So a screener is the kind, the name and whether the record is a
classifier's second screen that Scholar ran ([[via]] is
[[classify]]): [[screener_identity]].  The name stays the model's id,
because that is what it is, and a report comparing the names can say
that both screens came from one model.  The channel tells the two
apart because only [[llm classify --second-screen]] records
[[classify]], and a snapshot of a decision is always [[snapshot]].
The rule holds for every classifier second screen, not only for a
decision the same model made.  Whether the model made it can change
afterwards (a human's confirmation makes the next snapshot the
human's) while the second screen stays what it was; and so one model's
second screens are one screener over all the papers it screened,
whichever model made each decision.  Two other ways were rejected: a
key of its own, which every writer of every record would carry for
what one writer needs; and the prompt version, which two runs under
the same procedure share, and which is no part of a screener anyway (a
re-prompted model is the same screener).  A human or an agent never
records [[classify]], so for them the identity is the kind and the name,
as before.

<<review data structures>>=
def screener_identity(record: dict) -> tuple:
    """Who made a screening, as comparisons tell screeners apart.

    The kind, the name, and whether the record is a classifier's second
    screen run by `llm classify --second-screen` (`via` "classify"),
    which keeps a model's second screen apart from the snapshot of a
    decision the same model made.
    """
    return (record.get("kind"), record.get("name"),
            record.get("via") == "classify")
@

Let's check it on the case that needs it: a classifier's discard,
screened again by the same model through the second screen.  The
snapshot and the second screen both count, carry the same name and are
two screeners; a verdict relayed with [[sessions screen --as llm]]
under that name would be the snapshot's screener, which is why that
command refuses it.

<<test functions>>=
class TestScreenerIdentity:
  """A model's second screen is not the snapshot of its decision (#253)."""

  def test_same_model_second_screen_is_a_screener_of_its_own(self):
    decision = ReviewDecision(
      paper=Paper(title="Twice Screened", authors=["Ada Lovelace"],
                  doi="10.5555/twice"),
      provider="openalex", status=DecisionStatus.DISCARDED,
      tags=["off-topic-false-hit"], source=ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED,
      llm_model="model-a", llm_decided_at="2026-11-01T09:00:00+00:00",
    )
    second = new_screening(
      kind="llm", name="model-a", status="kept",
      tags=["adjacent-subtopic"], recorded_at="2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00",
      blind=True, blind_basis="prompt", via="classify",
    )
    snapshot = record_screening(decision, second)
    counted = counted_screenings(decision)
    assert counted == [snapshot, second]
    assert snapshot["name"] == second["name"] == "model-a"
    assert screener_identity(snapshot) == ("llm", "model-a", False)
    assert screener_identity(second) == ("llm", "model-a", True)
    assert len({screener_identity(r) for r in counted}) == 2
    relayed = new_screening(
      kind="llm", name="model-a", status="kept", tags=[],
      recorded_at="2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00",
    )
    assert screener_identity(relayed) == screener_identity(snapshot)
    agent = new_screening(kind="agent", name="agent-2", status="kept",
                          tags=[], recorded_at="2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00")
    assert screener_identity(agent) == ("agent", "agent-2", False)
@

\paragraph{How the list travels.}
Like the reset history (\cref{sec:reset-decision}), the list is saved
only when non-empty, so the file of a session nobody screened twice is
unchanged; loaded as empty from a file without the key; copied with the
decision when search results are appended
(\cref{sec:review-create-strict}); and united with the file's, by
[[id]], by a save that merges another process's changes
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}).  A fold of two copies of one work
(\cref{sec:session-copies}) gives the survivor the other copy's
screenings, so a fold never loses one.  Both go through
[[unite_screenings]], which applies the snapshot rule once more: two
processes that screened the same paper at once each took a snapshot of
the same verdict, under two ids, and of two snapshots of one verdict
only one is kept.  Every other record is kept whatever it holds.

<<review data structures>>=
def unite_screenings(decision: ReviewDecision, records: list) -> None:
    """Add the screenings `records` to `decision`'s, each once.

    A snapshot of a verdict that `decision` already holds a counted
    snapshot of, under another id, is left out.
    """
    for record in records:
        if is_valid_screening(record) and record.get("origin") == "decision":
            held = counted_screenings(decision)
            if (record["id"] not in {r["id"] for r in held}
                    and any(r.get("origin") == "decision"
                            and _snapshot_key(r) == _snapshot_key(record)
                            for r in held)):
                continue
        decision.add_screening(record)
@

Unlike the reset history, the list is part of neither
[[decision_state]] nor [[decision_verdict]], although the issue
sketched it as part of the state.  Both reasons come from the reviews
of the two records added to the decision just before it.
\begin{description}
\item[Not in the merge's state] The merge takes the whole decision from
  the file when this process's state is still the baseline's, and keeps
  this process's when it differs.  A screening does not change the
  decision, but in the state it would look as if it did: a [[sessions
  screen]] run that loaded the session before another process saved a
  human's adjudication would hold a changed state, and its save would
  put the old decision back over the human's.  That is what the review
  of the hand-given abstracts found for their records
  (\cref{sec:set-abstract}).  Out of the state, the screenings are
  united on their own and the decision follows the usual rule.  The
  snapshot such a run took stays, beside the human's later decision,
  as the verdict that run compared with.
\item[Not in the copy comparison] Copies of one work are compared by
  [[decision_verdict]] ([[_fold_direction]], [[same_id_pairs]]), and
  anything in it that differs between two copies keeps them apart: the
  review of the human's record found that copies decided alike at two
  moments no longer folded (\cref{sec:human-review-record}).  A
  screening recorded on one copy would do the same, and more: the
  copies would be listed as decided apart, and [[sessions decide]]
  would refuse to decide them without [[--all-copies]], though nobody
  decided anything.  Left out, the fold unites the lists instead
  (above).
\end{description}
A pending copy with screenings is not \emph{unscreened}
([[is_unscreened]]), so neither [[sessions reconcile]] nor a save that
settles a folded copy ([[apply_tombstones]]) drops it: a second
screener's verdict of a paper is a judgement, whatever the decision
says.  Nothing registers the list in [[MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS]] either,
so a machine [[sessions decide]], with or without [[--force]], leaves
the screenings alone; the next screening snapshots the new decision.

A record's identity is its [[id]], and two things change a stored
record: a relabel's correction of a snapshot (below), which stamps it
with [[relabelled_at]], and the merge's mark [[set_aside_at]] on a
screening made at the same time as a reset (next).  So when two lists
hold one [[id]] with different contents, [[add_screening]] makes one
record of the two ([[merged_screening]]): the version with the later
relabel stamp, carrying a set-aside mark if either version has one.
A process that loaded the session before the correction or the mark
then cannot put the old version back, whichever saves last.

<<review data structures>>=
def merged_screening(held: dict, record: dict) -> dict:
    """One record of two versions of a screening with the same `id`.

    The version with the later `relabelled_at` (`held` on a tie), with
    the earliest `set_aside_at` either version has.
    """
    newer = held
    if (str(record.get("relabelled_at") or "")
            > str(held.get("relabelled_at") or "")):
        newer = record
    asides = [r["set_aside_at"] for r in (held, record)
              if r.get("set_aside_at")]
    if not asides or newer.get("set_aside_at") == min(asides):
        return newer
    return {**newer, "set_aside_at": min(asides)}
@

\paragraph{Races with a reset or a relabel.}
A screening is made against the decision the screener saw, and the
snapshot records it.  When another process resets or relabels that
decision between the screener's load and its save, the two saves are
merged and each change stands, but the screening and the snapshot then
describe a decision that is no longer there.  The window is short for
[[sessions screen]], which holds the session for milliseconds, but a
review that holds it for minutes (a blind review in the TUI, say) makes
it the normal case.  The time rule of [[counted_screenings]] cannot
settle it: the screener's [[recorded_at]] may well be later than the
reset's [[at]], since both ran at once.

The merge can, because it sees what each side added since both loaded
the session ([[ConcurrentHistory]]): the screenings, resets and
relabels in one side's list and not in the other's.  Anything this
process loaded is in the file too (every save unites the lists), so
what only one side holds is what that side added meanwhile.  A
screening one side added while the other side added a reset was made
against the decision before the reset, so it is marked
[[set_aside_at]], with the reset's time, and no longer counted.  A
snapshot one side added while the other side relabelled the decision
claims the old source, so it is corrected as the relabel corrects its
own snapshots ([[correct_relabelled_snapshots]]), from the verdict the
decision had before each relabel ([[relabelled_verdict_keys]]).  This
runs after the decision itself is merged, in whichever process saves
second, so it does not matter which of the two that is.  When both
processes changed the decision itself, ours is kept with a warning
(below), and a relabel only the file holds may then match no snapshot;
that is left with the warning.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class ConcurrentHistory:
    """What each of two versions of a decision added since both loaded it.

    `mine_*` are in this process's version only, `their_*` in the
    file's only: screening ids, reset records and relabel records.
    """

    mine_screenings: set[str]
    their_screenings: set[str]
    mine_resets: list[dict]
    their_resets: list[dict]
    mine_relabels: list[dict]
    their_relabels: list[dict]

    @classmethod
    def of(cls, ours: ReviewDecision,
           theirs: ReviewDecision) -> "ConcurrentHistory":
        """Compare two versions of one decision, before they are united."""
        def ids(decision):
            return {r["id"] for r in decision.screenings
                    if is_valid_screening(r)}

        return cls(
            mine_screenings=ids(ours) - ids(theirs),
            their_screenings=ids(theirs) - ids(ours),
            mine_resets=[r for r in ours.resets if r not in theirs.resets],
            their_resets=[r for r in theirs.resets if r not in ours.resets],
            mine_relabels=[r for r in ours.relabelled
                           if r not in theirs.relabelled],
            their_relabels=[r for r in theirs.relabelled
                            if r not in ours.relabelled],
        )


def relabelled_verdict_keys(
    decision: ReviewDecision, relabels: list[dict]
) -> list[tuple]:
    """The snapshot keys `decision`'s verdict had before each relabel.

    Undoes `relabels`, newest first: each gives back its old source and
    the human's record it cleared.
    """
    keys = []
    state = decision
    for record in sorted(relabels, key=lambda r: str(r.get("at") or ""),
                         reverse=True):
        human = record.get("human") or {}
        state = replace(
            state, source=ReviewSource(str(record.get("from"))),
            **{name: human.get(name) for name in HUMAN_AUDIT_FIELDS},
        )
        keys.append(_snapshot_key(decision_snapshot(state, "")))
    return keys


def settle_concurrent_screenings(
    decision: ReviewDecision, history: ConcurrentHistory
) -> None:
    """Settle one side's screenings against the other side's resets and
    relabels, made at the same time (`ConcurrentHistory`)."""
    for screenings, resets, relabels in (
        (history.their_screenings, history.mine_resets,
         history.mine_relabels),
        (history.mine_screenings, history.their_resets,
         history.their_relabels),
    ):
        if not screenings:
            continue
        if relabels:
            correct_relabelled_snapshots(
                decision, screenings,
                relabelled_verdict_keys(decision, relabels),
                max(str(r.get("at") or "") for r in relabels),
            )
        if resets:
            at = max(str(r.get("at") or "") for r in resets)
            for index, held in enumerate(decision.screenings):
                if (is_valid_screening(held) and held["id"] in screenings
                        and not held.get("set_aside_at")):
                    decision.screenings[index] = {
                        **held, "set_aside_at": at,
                    }
@

An older Scholar reads a decision by the keys it knows, so it loads a
file with screenings and drops the list when it next saves the
session, as with [[relabelled]] (\cref{sec:relabel-source}).
This was checked on November 20, 2026, with the installed pipx build
(version 1.29, built from the main checkout before the human's record
of \cref{sec:human-review-record} existed): it showed a session with
screenings and, after a [[sessions decide]] on another paper, saved it
without the list, the decisions themselves intact.  Every installation
that saves a session must be updated before the screenings are relied
on.

The list costs space.  In the indented JSON of a session file a
screening without a reason takes about 670 bytes and a snapshot about
690 (measured on invented records on November 20, 2026); a reason
comes on top, stored whole, and a classifier's snapshot copies the
classifier's reason too.  A second screen of 30\,000 decided records
with 300-character reasons, one snapshot and one screening each, thus
adds about 50~MB to the file, more when the snapshots copy classifiers'
reasons.  Reasons are not cut to save it: a reason
cut short is no longer the screener's.

\paragraph{The relabel and the reset.}
A relabel (\cref{sec:relabel-source}) corrects who made the decision
and leaves the verdict as it is.  The screenings recorded by a command
stand as they were: they are other screeners' verdicts.  But a snapshot
of this decision taken under the old source claims the same wrong
origin as the source did, so it is corrected with it: every counted
snapshot whose verdict and screener are those the decision had before
the relabel takes the screener the relabelled decision gives
([[verdict_screener]]), stamped with the relabel's time.  The values
it replaces are kept in the snapshot, under [[before_relabel]]: the
label was wrong, but what the snapshot claimed (a classifier's model,
reason and prompt version, say, when an LLM call is relabelled as an
agent's) is part of the record, as the relabel record keeps the old
source.  A snapshot relabelled twice keeps what it claimed first.  The
old kind is also noted in the relabel record as [[screenings_kind]].
Leaving the snapshot would keep, as a human's verdict, one the relabel
has just declared an agent's.  A relabel of a decision without a
snapshot writes the record it wrote before.

<<correct the snapshots of the relabelled verdict>>=
counted = {r["id"] for r in counted_screenings(decision)}
if correct_relabelled_snapshots(decision, counted, [old_verdict], at):
    record["screenings_kind"] = old_verdict[2]
<<review data structures>>=
def correct_relabelled_snapshots(
    decision: ReviewDecision, ids: set[str], old_keys: list[tuple], at: str
) -> int:
    """Give the snapshots of a relabelled verdict the decision's screener.

    The snapshots among `ids` whose `_snapshot_key` is one of
    `old_keys` take `verdict_screener(decision)`, stamped with
    `relabelled_at`; the values they claimed before are kept under
    `before_relabel` (the first relabel's). Returns how many changed.
    """
    new_screener = verdict_screener(decision)
    corrected = 0
    for index, held in enumerate(decision.screenings):
        if (is_valid_screening(held) and held["id"] in ids
                and held.get("origin") == "decision"
                and _snapshot_key(held) in old_keys):
            decision.screenings[index] = {
                **held, **new_screener, "relabelled_at": at,
                "before_relabel": held.get("before_relabel") or {
                    name: held.get(name) for name in SNAPSHOT_FIELDS
                },
            }
            corrected += 1
    return corrected
@

A reset keeps the screenings, as history; [[counted_screenings]] sets
aside those recorded before it (above).  So that the history says so
too, the reset record notes how many screenings it set aside, under
[[screenings]], when there were any; a reset of a decision without
screenings writes the record it wrote before.  The count is of the
screenings the reset saw.  Those another process recorded at the same
time are marked by the merge (above), but the reset record is not
changed afterwards: it is part of the decision's state, and two
versions of one record would be united as two resets.

<<count the screenings a reset sets aside>>=
set_aside = len(counted_screenings(decision))
if set_aside:
    record["screenings"] = set_aside
@

\paragraph{What [[sessions show]] says.}
[[screenings_summary]] counts the screenings in one line, empty for a
session without any, so that the display of every other session stays
as it was: the counted screenings, on how many papers, how many of
them are snapshots of the decision, and, when there are any, how many
a reset set aside and how many entries are malformed.  Papers are
counted by id, since copies of one work under one id are one paper.

<<review data structures>>=
def screenings_summary(decisions) -> str:
    """One line counting the screenings of `decisions`; "" if none."""
    entries = [r for d in decisions for r in d.screenings]
    if not entries:
        return ""
    counted = [(d, r) for d in decisions for r in counted_screenings(d)]
    valid = sum(1 for r in entries if is_valid_screening(r))
    papers = len({get_paper_id(d.paper) for d, _ in counted})
    snapshots = sum(1 for _, r in counted if r.get("origin") == "decision")
    parts = [
        f"Screenings: {len(counted)} on {papers} paper(s), {snapshots} of "
        "them snapshots of the decision"
    ]
    if valid > len(counted):
        parts.append(f"{valid - len(counted)} set aside by a reset")
    if len(entries) > valid:
        parts.append(f"{len(entries) - valid} malformed, skipped")
    return "; ".join(parts) + "."
@

Let's check the record itself: a screening of a decided paper brings a
snapshot of the decision's verdict, once per verdict, and leaves the
decision as it was; a pending paper's has none; each source gives the
snapshot its screener, the human-confirmed classifier call as a human's
verdict with the model it confirmed; a bad value is refused.

