\paragraph{What a sample tells of its papers.}
\label{sec:sample-giveaway}
A review sample is screened again, by the TUI's blind mode
(\cref{sec:tui-blind-review} in the TUI module) or from the screen
sheet of [[sessions export]] (\cref{sec:screen-sheet} in the CLI
module), and both must say what the sample itself gives away, since
neither can hide it.  The two functions live here, with the samples,
so that the CLI need not import the TUI for them.

[[sample_holding]] names the sample that drew a paper.  A banded draw
is several samples, one per band (\cref{sec:llm-sample-bands} in the
LLM review module), and the paper's own band is named, by its own id.
The samples are searched in the order the sample filter lists them,
and a sample whose drawn decision has been reset since does not count,
as in [[sampled_for_review]].  The LLM review module's helpers are
imported where they are used, since that module imports this one.

<<review data structures>>=
def sample_holding(
    session: "ReviewSession", decision: ReviewDecision
) -> str | None:
    """The id of the first sample that drew `decision`, or None."""
    from scholar.llm_review import reset_since_draw

    paper_id = get_paper_id(decision.paper)
    for sample in session.llm_samples:
        if any(e.get("paper_id") == paper_id for e in sample.papers) \
                and not reset_since_draw(decision, sample.drawn_at):
            return sample.sample_id
    return None
@

[[sample_giveaway]] gives one line for each sample that tells
something of the given papers: one drawn from a single status tells
that status of every paper in it, and one drawn by confidence, in
bands or between bounds, tells each paper's range of confidence.  A
session may hold several samples, one of them mixed and another of
discards only, so each line names its sample, by the id that [[llm
sample --report]] and [[sessions screen --sample]] use, and says how
many of the listed papers it holds; neither the TUI's list nor the
sheet shows which sample drew a paper.  The words come from
[[describe_sample_criteria]], which the sample's report uses too,
given only the status and the population, so that one band's bounds
are not read as the whole sample's.

<<review data structures>>=
def sample_giveaway(session: "ReviewSession", decisions) -> str:
    """One line for each sample drawing `decisions` that tells something
    of them anyway (their status, their confidence), or ""."""
    from scholar.llm_review import describe_sample_criteria

    listed: dict[str, int] = {}
    for decision in decisions:
        sample_id = sample_holding(session, decision)
        if sample_id is not None:
            listed[sample_id] = listed.get(sample_id, 0) + 1
    lines = []
    for sample in session.llm_samples:
        count = listed.pop(sample.sample_id, 0)
        if not count:
            continue
        criteria = sample.criteria
        told = []
        if len(criteria.get("statuses") or ()) == 1:
            population = describe_sample_criteria({
                key: criteria[key]
                for key in ("statuses", "population_sources")
                if key in criteria
            })
            told.append(f"holds {population} only: their status is known.")
        if ("stratum" in criteria or criteria.get("no_confidence")
                or criteria.get("min_confidence") is not None
                or criteria.get("max_confidence") is not None):
            told.append("It was drawn by confidence: the range of "
                        "confidence of each of its papers is known."
                        if told else
                        "was drawn by confidence: the range of confidence "
                        "of each of its papers is known.")
        if told:
            papers = "paper" if count == 1 else "papers"
            lines.append(f"Sample {sample.sample_id} ({count} listed "
                         f"{papers}) " + " ".join(told))
    return "\n".join(lines)
@


\subsection{Samples of the rest of a screening tier}
\label{sec:tier-sample-record}

A review that screens a first tier (\cref{sec:screening-tier}) and
counts the rest can say nothing, from the tier alone, about what the
rest holds; the issue that asked for this
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/315}) reports a
literature look by the author that found a fixed place cut to be no
stopping rule.  What it can
state is an estimate from a random sample of the rest, screened like
the tier.  [[sessions export -f screen-sheet --pending --rest-sample N
--seed S]] (\cref{sec:rest-sample-sheet} in the CLI module) draws that
sample with the sampler of [[llm sample]] (\cref{sec:tier-sample} in
the LLM review module) and stores the draw here, so that the sample
can be reported once it is screened, and checked: from the seed, the
rule and the digest of the rest, anyone with the session can repeat the
draw.

The record is not an [[LLMSample]].  That one holds unreviewed machine
decisions with the status each had when drawn, and its report counts
how often a human changed them; the papers of this sample were pending
when drawn and have no status to change.  A [[TierSample]] keeps how
it was drawn ([[seed]], [[drawn_at]] in UTC, the requested [[size]]),
the tier rule exactly as [[BASE-tier.json]] writes it
([[TierRule.data]]), the number of pending papers the rule left out
([[rest]]) and a digest of their sorted ids ([[rest_digest]]), and the
drawn paper ids in the order drawn.  When the rest holds no more papers
than asked for, all of it is taken and the record says it is a
[[census]].  A later search adds papers to the session, and with them
to the rest; the stored ids stand, and the digest tells that the rest
has changed since.  Another draw is another record.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class TierSample:
    """A seeded random sample of the pending papers a tier rule left out.

    `rule` is the rule as `TierRule.data()` gives it; `rest` the number
    of pending papers outside the tier when drawn and `rest_digest` a
    digest of their sorted ids; `papers` the drawn paper ids in the
    order drawn. `census` tells that the whole rest was taken.
    """
    sample_id: str
    drawn_at: str
    seed: int
    size: int
    rule: dict
    rest: int
    rest_digest: str
    papers: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    census: bool = False

    def to_dict(self) -> dict:
        """Return the sample as JSON-ready data."""
        return {
            "sample_id": self.sample_id,
            "drawn_at": self.drawn_at,
            "seed": self.seed,
            "size": self.size,
            "rule": dict(self.rule),
            "rest": self.rest,
            "rest_digest": self.rest_digest,
            "papers": list(self.papers),
            "census": self.census,
        }

    @classmethod
    def from_dict(cls, data: dict) -> "TierSample":
        """Rebuild a sample saved by `to_dict`."""
        return cls(
            sample_id=data["sample_id"],
            drawn_at=data.get("drawn_at", ""),
            seed=data.get("seed"),
            size=data.get("size", 0),
            rule=dict(data.get("rule") or {}),
            rest=data.get("rest", 0),
            rest_digest=data.get("rest_digest", ""),
            papers=[str(p) for p in data.get("papers") or []],
            census=bool(data.get("census", False)),
        )
@

A session lists its draws in [[tier_samples]], oldest first.  The list
is saved only when it holds a draw, so a session without one is saved
as before, byte for byte; a file saved before the list existed loads
with none.  An older Scholar reads only the keys it knows and writes
only those (\cref{sec:session-persistence}), so it drops the list on
its next save, and with it the record of the draw, though not the
decisions made on the drawn papers.  Draws only accumulate, like the
samples of machine decisions, so a concurrent save adds the draws only
the file has, by [[sample_id]] (\cref{sec:session-integrity}); the id
ends in random digits for the reason given for [[LLMSample]] ids
(\cref{sec:tier-sample} in the LLM review module).  The paper ids are
repointed when copies are folded or a paper is renamed, as those of
the review samples are ([[repoint_paper_ids]],
\cref{sec:session-renames}).

