\paragraph{What a draw shows so far.}
A drawn paper is looked up by its id.  Its id may have moved since:
a fold or a rename (an adopted preprint, a DBLP homonym number or a
name written surname first dropped from a DOI-less id) repoints the
stored id, and [[drawn_decisions]] follows both maps itself as well
([[current_paper_id]], \cref{sec:session-renames}), for a session
whose ids were not repointed yet.  The rename record is the mechanism;
the version links ([[version_link_dois]]) remain a fallback for one
case it cannot know: a preprint adopted by a Scholar that kept no
rename record (between #318 and #319, or an older build saving the
file in between), whose adopted paper still names the DOI it was
drawn under.  The fallback is asked only when no decision holds the
id the maps give, and it is taken only when exactly one paper names
the link.  Any other paper renamed without a record keeps no trace of
its old id and is counted as not found, as is a paper no longer in the
session.  The report says
how many and counts them neither as screened nor as unscreened.

A drawn paper is \emph{screened} when a decision under its id is kept
or discarded, by anyone.  Copies of one paper under one id are one
drawn paper.  When its decided copies give different verdicts (below),
the record has none: which copy came first in the session would decide
the count, and nothing would say that they disagree.  It is counted
apart ([[copies_apart]], by id) and the rate is withheld, as scholar
lists copies decided apart elsewhere ([[same_id_pairs]],
\cref{sec:session-copies}) for someone to settle; the first decided
copy names the decider.

What the sample estimates is what screening the rest would find, so a
screened paper \emph{bears on the question} when its decision says so,
whoever made it ([[tier_sample_verdict]]).  In a session that uses
categories (a decision holds one of [[AUDIT_CATEGORIES]], as
[[sessions screen]] tells it), a keep bears when its category is of
bearing order ([[supports-claim]] or [[qualifies-claim]], up to
[[AUDIT_ORDER_BEARING]]) and does not when it is adjacent or off topic.
In a session without categories, a keep bears: keeping a record is
there the screener's whole judgement that it bears.  A discard does not
bear, whatever category it carries, since the status is the decision,
as in the audit table.

This is not the audit table's rule for a \emph{cited} row
([[check_bucket]], \cref{sec:semantic-check}), which counts only the
keeps a human made or confirmed: the table says what the text cites,
and an unreviewed keep is a candidate there.  A sample of the rest is
often screened by a model ([[llm classify --tier-sample]]), and under
the cited rule its keeps without a category counted as not bearing:
the review of this change measured a rate of 0 for a sample in which
an unreviewed classifier had kept five records of sixty, and 5 of 60
when a human had kept the same five.  So the rule here takes every
screener's word, and the report names who screened, by the labels the
rest of the reports use ([[decision_source_items]] over the screened
drawn papers), next to every count: an unreviewed verdict is stated as
such rather than withheld.

Two kinds of screened paper do not enter the rate.  A keep without a
category in a session that uses categories could bear or not: it is
counted as \emph{undetermined}, and the rate is withheld until it has a
category, since counting it either way would decide for the screener.
A discard made without a judgement of the work---under a criterion
about the record ([[AUDIT_RECORD_CRITERIA]]: no abstract, too little
information) or as another version of a cited source
([[AUDIT_VERSION_NOTES]])---says nothing about whether the work bears;
the audit table ranks such discards apart for the same reason
([[audit_copy_rank]]).  It is \emph{excluded}, counted by its reason,
and the rate is over the other screened papers, [[judged]].

A discard does not bear, but a discard that carries a bearing category
(a human's \enquote{supports, excluded}, which the audit table shows
as such) is a screener's word that the work bears on the question and
still does not belong, which a reader of the rate should know.  It
counts as not bearing, and the count of such discards is stated
([[discarded_bearing]], by [[audit_excluded]], the audit table's test).

<<review data structures>>=
TIER_BEARING = "bearing"
TIER_NOT_BEARING = "not bearing"
TIER_UNDETERMINED = "undetermined"
TIER_EXCLUDED = "excluded"


def uses_categories(decisions) -> bool:
    """Whether any of `decisions` holds one of `AUDIT_CATEGORIES`."""
    return any(t in AUDIT_CATEGORIES for d in decisions for t in d.tags)


def tier_sample_verdict(
    decision: ReviewDecision, categories: bool
) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """What a decided drawn paper says of the question, and why not.

    Returns `TIER_BEARING`, `TIER_NOT_BEARING`, `TIER_UNDETERMINED` (a
    keep without a category when the session uses `categories`) or
    `TIER_EXCLUDED` with the record criterion or version note.
    """
    if decision.status == DecisionStatus.DISCARDED:
        note = next(
            (t for t in decision.tags if t in AUDIT_VERSION_NOTES), None
        )
        criterion = audit_exclusion_criterion(
            decision, theme_names={}, notes=AUDIT_STRINGS["en"]["notes"]
        )
        if note is not None:
            return TIER_EXCLUDED, note
        if criterion in AUDIT_RECORD_CRITERIA:
            return TIER_EXCLUDED, criterion
        return TIER_NOT_BEARING, None
    if not categories:
        return TIER_BEARING, None
    category = audit_category(decision.tags)
    if category is None:
        return TIER_UNDETERMINED, None
    if AUDIT_CATEGORIES[category] <= AUDIT_ORDER_BEARING:
        return TIER_BEARING, None
    return TIER_NOT_BEARING, None
@

The counts keep each part apart: the screened papers, who screened
them, the bearing ones, the undetermined and the excluded ones (by
reason), and the papers not yet screened or not found.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class TierSampleCounts:
    """What a `TierSample` shows in a session so far, per drawn paper."""
    drawn: int
    screened: int = 0
    bearing: int = 0
    unscreened: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    not_found: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    undetermined: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    excluded: dict[str, int] = field(default_factory=dict)
    deciders: list[tuple[str, int]] = field(default_factory=list)
    copies_apart: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    discarded_bearing: int = 0

    @property
    def judged(self) -> int:
        """The screened papers the rate is over."""
        return (self.screened - sum(self.excluded.values())
                - len(self.undetermined) - len(self.copies_apart))


def drawn_decisions(
    session: "ReviewSession", paper_ids
) -> dict[str, list[ReviewDecision]]:
    """The decisions each drawn paper id names now ([] when none).

    Follows the fold tombstones and the renames (`current_paper_id`),
    then, for an id no decision holds, a version link that exactly one
    paper of the session holds.
    """
    by_id: dict[str, list[ReviewDecision]] = {}
    for decision in session.decisions:
        by_id.setdefault(get_paper_id(decision.paper), []).append(decision)
    held = set(by_id)
    linked: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    for paper_id, decisions in by_id.items():
        for link in version_link_dois(decisions[0].paper):
            linked.setdefault(link, set()).add(paper_id)
    found = {}
    for drawn in paper_ids:
        paper_id = current_paper_id(session, drawn, held)
        if paper_id not in held and len(linked.get(paper_id, ())) == 1:
            paper_id = next(iter(linked[paper_id]))
        found[drawn] = by_id.get(paper_id, [])
    return found


def tier_sample_counts(
    session: "ReviewSession", sample: TierSample
) -> TierSampleCounts:
    """Count what the drawn papers of `sample` show in `session`."""
    counts = TierSampleCounts(drawn=len(sample.papers))
    categories = uses_categories(session.decisions)
    screened = []
    for paper_id, held in drawn_decisions(session, sample.papers).items():
        decided = [d for d in held if d.is_decided]
        if not held:
            counts.not_found.append(paper_id)
        elif not decided:
            counts.unscreened.append(paper_id)
        else:
            screened.append(decided[0])
            verdicts = {
                tier_sample_verdict(d, categories)[0] for d in decided
            }
            verdict, reason = tier_sample_verdict(decided[0], categories)
            if len(verdicts) > 1:
                counts.copies_apart.append(paper_id)
            elif verdict == TIER_EXCLUDED:
                counts.excluded[reason] = counts.excluded.get(reason, 0) + 1
            elif verdict == TIER_UNDETERMINED:
                counts.undetermined.append(paper_id)
            elif verdict == TIER_BEARING:
                counts.bearing += 1
            elif audit_excluded(decided[0]):
                counts.discarded_bearing += 1
    counts.screened = len(screened)
    counts.deciders = [
        (label, count)
        for label, count in decision_source_items(screened) if count
    ]
    return counts
@

The deciders are those that screened a drawn record.
[[decision_source_items]] keeps the human row at zero, so that a
report on a whole session says aloud that no human decided; here a
\enquote{human: 0} beside a sample a model screened would read as a
count of the sample, so the zero rows are dropped where the counts are
taken, for every output, the JSON included.

The parts are worded once ([[tier_sample_deciders_text]],
[[tier_sample_counts_text]]) for the line of [[sessions show]], the
listing of [[sessions tier-sample]] and the report's sentence.  The
labels of the deciders hold commas (\enquote{LLM, unreviewed}), so the
parts are separated by semicolons, as in [[decision_sources_sentence]].

<<review data structures>>=
def tier_sample_deciders_text(counts: TierSampleCounts) -> str:
    """Who screened the drawn papers: `human: 5; LLM, unreviewed: 55`."""
    return "; ".join(f"{label}: {count}" for label, count in counts.deciders)


def tier_sample_excluded_text(counts: TierSampleCounts) -> str:
    """The reasons of the excluded papers: `no-abstract: 20`."""
    return "; ".join(
        f"{reason}: {count}"
        for reason, count in sorted(counts.excluded.items())
    )


def tier_sample_counts_text(counts: TierSampleCounts) -> str:
    """The counts of a draw in a few words, the screened ones first."""
    text = f"{counts.screened} screened"
    if counts.screened:
        text += f" (by {tier_sample_deciders_text(counts)})"
    text += f", {counts.bearing} bearing"
    if counts.discarded_bearing:
        text += (f", {counts.discarded_bearing} discarded although "
                 "categorised as bearing")
    if counts.excluded:
        text += (f", {sum(counts.excluded.values())} excluded without a "
                 "judgement of the work "
                 f"({tier_sample_excluded_text(counts)})")
    if counts.undetermined:
        text += (f", {len(counts.undetermined)} undetermined (kept "
                 "without a category)")
    if counts.copies_apart:
        text += (f", {len(counts.copies_apart)} with copies decided "
                 "apart")
    if counts.not_found:
        text += f", {len(counts.not_found)} not found"
    return text
@

[[sessions show]] states each draw in one line, and its JSON gives the
record with the counts.  The line names the draw by the id that [[llm
classify --tier-sample]] and [[sessions tier-sample]] take.  The rate
and its interval are left to [[sessions tier-sample --report]] and the
report: they need the interval of the LLM review module, which
[[sessions show]] does not import, since importing it loads the
synthesis caches.

<<review data structures>>=
def tier_sample_line(sample: TierSample, counts: TierSampleCounts) -> str:
    """One line stating a draw and what it shows so far."""
    drawn = (f"all {counts.drawn} (a census)" if sample.census
             else f"{counts.drawn}")
    return (
        f"Sample of the rest {sample.sample_id}: {drawn} drawn of "
        f"{sample.rest} pending papers outside the tier (seed "
        f"{sample.seed}); {tier_sample_counts_text(counts)}."
    )


def tier_sample_data(sample: TierSample, counts: TierSampleCounts) -> dict:
    """The draw as JSON data, with what it shows so far."""
    return {
        **sample.to_dict(),
        "drawn": counts.drawn,
        "screened": counts.screened,
        "deciders": dict(counts.deciders),
        "bearing": counts.bearing,
        "judged": counts.judged,
        "excluded": dict(sorted(counts.excluded.items())),
        "undetermined": list(counts.undetermined),
        "copies_apart": list(counts.copies_apart),
        "discarded_bearing": counts.discarded_bearing,
        "unscreened": list(counts.unscreened),
        "not_found": list(counts.not_found),
    }
@

The report's sentence names the tier's rule in words, so that a reader
of a method section knows which records the rest was.  The words are
the rule's own [[meaning]], stored with it ([[TierRule.data]]), with
its values put in for the names of its parameters: they cannot drift
from what selected the tier, and a rule stored before a change of
wording keeps its own words.  The conditions are joined by \enquote{or},
since any one of them selects; a rule of one provider names it, not
\enquote{one of} it.

The values are the user's text, put in as written: a function gives
each replacement ([[as_written]]), since [[re.sub]] reads a
replacement string's backslashes as escapes, and a [[--query-like]]
holding [[\d]] once stopped the report with a traceback.  Only white
space other than a plain space (a line break, a tab) becomes a space,
so that the sentence stays one line in every output.

<<review data structures>>=
def one_line(value) -> str:
    """`value` as text with every white space but a space made a space."""
    return re.sub(r"[^\S ]", " ", str(value))


def as_written(value):
    """A replacement for `re.sub` that puts `value` in as written."""
    text = one_line(value)
    return lambda match: text


def tier_rule_words(rule: dict) -> str:
    """The stored tier `rule` (`TierRule.data()`) in words."""
    meaning = {**TIER_CONDITION_MEANINGS, **(rule.get("meaning") or {})}
    parts = []
    if rule.get("top") is not None:
        parts.append(re.sub(r"\btop\b", as_written(rule["top"]),
                            meaning["top"], count=1))
    if rule.get("min_providers") is not None:
        parts.append(re.sub(r"\bmin_providers\b",
                            as_written(rule["min_providers"]),
                            meaning["min_providers"], count=1))
    providers = list(rule.get("providers") or [])
    if providers:
        text = meaning["provider"]
        if rule.get("query_like"):
            text = re.sub(r"\bquery_like\b",
                          as_written(f'"{rule["query_like"]}"'), text,
                          count=1)
        else:
            text = re.sub(r" whose .*\bquery_like\b.*$", "", text)
        pattern = (r"\bone of providers\b" if len(providers) == 1
                   else r"\bproviders\b")
        parts.append(re.sub(pattern, as_written(", ".join(providers)),
                            text, count=1))
    return ", or ".join(parts)
@

Let's verify the record: it round-trips, a session without a draw is
saved without the key, an old file loads with none, and a draw saved by
another process meanwhile is kept.  Then the counts: a drawn paper is
screened once decided, by a model too; it bears when a human keeps it
under a theme or a classifier keeps it as supporting the claim, not
when it is discarded with a bearing category or kept as adjacent; a
paper whose preprint was adopted is found through the rename record,
or without one through its version link, and a paper gone is not
found.

<<test functions>>=
class TestTierSampleRecord:
  """The draw of a tier's rest is stored with the session (#315)."""

  <<tier sample record test methods>>

<<tier sample record test methods>>=
def _sample(self, papers=("doi:10.5555/rest-1",), sample_id="d1"):
  return TierSample(
    sample_id=sample_id, drawn_at="2026-11-23T10:00:00+00:00", seed=7,
    size=2, rule={"top": 10, "min_providers": None, "providers": [],
                  "query_like": None},
    rest=40, rest_digest="0123abcd", papers=list(papers),
  )

def _save_empty(self, name):
  save_session(ReviewSession(
    query="q", providers=["openalex"], timestamp=datetime(2026, 11, 23),
    name=name,
  ))

def test_round_trip_and_old_files(self):
  import json
  from scholar.review import _get_sessions_dir

  self._save_empty("rest-record")
  path = _get_sessions_dir() / "rest-record.json"
  assert "tier_samples" not in json.loads(path.read_text())
  session = load_session("rest-record")
  assert session.tier_samples == []
  session.tier_samples.append(self._sample())
  save_session(session)
  assert load_session("rest-record").tier_samples == [self._sample()]
  assert TierSample.from_dict(self._sample().to_dict()) == self._sample()

def test_concurrent_save_keeps_a_draw(self):
  self._save_empty("rest-race")
  stale = load_session("rest-race")
  drawer = load_session("rest-race")
  drawer.tier_samples.append(self._sample())
  save_session(drawer)
  stale.tier_samples.append(self._sample(sample_id="d2"))
  save_session(stale)
  final = load_session("rest-race")
  assert [s.sample_id for s in final.tier_samples] == ["d2", "d1"]

def _decision(self, n, status=DecisionStatus.PENDING, tags=(),
              source=ReviewSource.HUMAN):
  return ReviewDecision(
    paper=Paper(title=f"Rest {n}", authors=["Ada Lovelace"],
                doi=f"10.5555/rest-{n}"),
    provider="openalex", status=status, tags=list(tags), source=source,
  )

def test_the_counts(self):
  kept, out = DecisionStatus.KEPT, DecisionStatus.DISCARDED
  llm = ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED
  decisions = [
    self._decision(1, kept, ["a theme"]),
    self._decision(2, kept, ["supports-claim"], llm),
    self._decision(3, out, ["supports-claim"]),
    self._decision(4, kept, ["adjacent-subtopic"]),
    self._decision(5),
    self._decision(8, out, ["no-abstract"], llm),
    self._decision(9, out, ["duplicate-record-of-cited-source"]),
  ]
  adopted = self._decision(6, kept, ["qualifies-claim"])
  adopted.paper = Paper(
    title="Rest 6", authors=["Ada Lovelace"], doi="10.48550/rest-6",
    published_versions=[{"doi": "10.5555/rest-6",
                         "relation": "preprint-of"}],
  )
  session = ReviewSession(query="q", providers=["openalex"],
                          timestamp=datetime(2026, 11, 23),
                          decisions=decisions + [adopted])
  sample = self._sample([f"doi:10.5555/rest-{n}" for n in range(1, 10)])
  counts = tier_sample_counts(session, sample)
  assert (counts.drawn, counts.screened, counts.bearing) == (9, 7, 2)
  assert counts.unscreened == ["doi:10.5555/rest-5"]
  assert counts.not_found == ["doi:10.5555/rest-7"]
  assert counts.undetermined == ["doi:10.5555/rest-1"]
  assert counts.excluded == {"no-abstract": 1,
                             "duplicate-record-of-cited-source": 1}
  assert counts.judged == 4 and counts.discarded_bearing == 1
  assert tier_sample_line(sample, counts) == (
    "Sample of the rest d1: 9 drawn of 40 pending papers outside the "
    "tier (seed 7); 7 screened (by human: 5; LLM, unreviewed: 2), 2 "
    "bearing, 1 discarded although categorised as bearing, 2 excluded "
    "without a judgement of the work "
    "(duplicate-record-of-cited-source: 1; no-abstract: 1), 1 "
    "undetermined (kept without a category), 1 not found."
  )
