\subsection{Snowball Round}

When performing manual snowballing (adding references or citing papers to the
session), we track each round for documentation in the final report. This
allows systematic literature reviews to document their snowballing methodology.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class SnowballRound:
    """
    Record of a snowballing round.

    Tracks when snowballing was performed, which papers were used as sources,
    the direction (references or citations), and how many new papers were added.
    """
    timestamp: datetime
    source_papers: list[str]  # Paper IDs that were snowballed from
    direction: str  # "references" or "citations"
    papers_added: int  # Count of new papers added (excluding duplicates)
@


\subsection{Search Record}

To reconstruct the exact [[scholar search]] that surfaced a paper, a session
must remember not just the query sent to each provider but the \emph{filters}
and \emph{limit} applied alongside it.  These belong to the individual search,
not the session as a whole: a session accumulates searches across [[rq]]
re-runs and snowball rounds, and those can carry different filters, so a single
session-level filter set would misattribute one search's constraints to
another.  We therefore record one [[SearchRecord]] per [[(query, provider)]]
search.

A record must also say what the search \emph{returned}, or a session
cannot tell a query that matched nothing from one the provider never
answered---the gap that let a blocked DBLP leave zero hits across four
review sessions.  So the record keeps the outcome of the search as well:
[[hits]] is the number of papers it produced, [[error]] the reason it
failed (\cref{sec:search-result-error}), and [[retries]] how often it was
repeated before the answer was accepted.  Records written before these
fields existed load with [[hits]] unknown ([[None]]), no error and no
retries: we cannot reconstruct what an old search returned, and claiming
\enquote{answered} for it is no worse than what such a record has always
claimed.  The record also keeps the [[total]] the service reported, and
whether it was an estimate, so that a count cut at the limit reads as
one (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/166}); older records
load with the total unknown.  And it keeps when the search ran and when
its answer was fetched (\cref{sec:search-dates} in the [[scholar]]
module), which differ for an answer served from the cache, so that a
report can state the date each source was searched
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}).  Older records
load with both dates unknown, and the session's own [[timestamp]],
reset at every append, is no stand-in for them.

\label{sec:search-record-key}
A search is identified by its query, its provider \emph{and} its
filters.  The same words sent to OpenAlex with [[--year 2010-2019]] and
with [[--year 2020-2024]] are two searches, with their own papers and
their own hit counts.  When the key was only [[(query, provider)]], the
second run replaced the first one's record, and the first run's filters
and hits were lost although its papers stayed in the session
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/140}).  The filters enter
the key through [[filters_key]] (\cref{sec:filters-key} in the
[[scholar]] module), the canonical string of the stored dictionary,
because two dictionaries can describe the same filters in different
orders.  The limit is deliberately not part of the key: it decides how
many of a search's papers we fetch, not which search it is, and a re-run
with a larger limit should replace the earlier record of that search,
not stand beside it.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class SearchRecord:
    """One provider search: its query, filters, limit, and outcome.

    ``filters`` is the dict form of the ``SearchFilters`` that were applied
    (``None`` when none were, or when the record predates filter
    persistence); ``limit`` is the per-provider result cap.  ``hits`` is
    the number of papers the search produced (``None`` for records that
    predate it), ``error`` why the provider failed (``None`` when it
    answered), and ``retries`` how often the search was repeated.
    ``total`` is the number of matching records the service reported
    (``None`` when unknown), ``total_estimated`` whether it called that
    an estimate. ``searched_at`` is when the search ran, ``fetched_at``
    when its answer was fetched from the service (UTC ISO times;
    ``None`` when unknown) and ``from_cache`` whether the answer came
    from the cache (``None`` when unknown).
    ``via`` names the endpoint that answered when the provider has
    several (``None``: the provider's default, or not recorded).
    ``order`` is how the answer was ordered (``relevance``, ``date``,
    ``citations`` or ``unknown``; ``None``: not recorded).
    ``retired`` is ``{"at", "by", "note"}`` when the search was retired
    from the review's searches (``None``: it is current).
    """
    query: str
    provider: str
    filters: dict | None = None
    limit: int | None = None
    hits: int | None = None
    error: str | None = None
    retries: int = 0
    total: int | None = None
    total_estimated: bool = False
    searched_at: str | None = None
    fetched_at: str | None = None
    from_cache: bool | None = None
    via: str | None = None
    retired: dict | None = None
    order: str | None = None

    @property
    def key(self) -> tuple[str, str, str]:
        """The `(query, provider, filters key)` identifying the search."""
        return (self.query, self.provider, filters_key(self.filters))

    @property
    def is_legacy(self) -> bool:
        """Whether the record predates stored filters, which are unknown.

        Such records have neither filters nor a limit; every search
        recorded since stores its limit.
        """
        return self.filters is None and self.limit is None

    <<search record properties>>
@

[[via]] names the endpoint that answered when a provider has more than
one (\cref{sec:search-result-via} in the scholar module): [[bulk]] for
a Semantic Scholar search that went to its bulk search (and
[[bulk, most cited first]] when it had a limit).
It is not part of the key: which endpoint a provider uses follows from
the query and the limit, so it does not tell two searches apart that
the key would merge.
Nor is it part of [[record_rank]], by which a session keeps one of two
records of a search (\cref{sec:wider-record}): a bulk run of a query
that the relevance search once answered is the same search, and the
newer or wider run replaces the older record like any other.
That is intended.  For a Boolean query the relevance answer was the
zero of \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/161}, and for a
plain-word query the bulk record is the wider one, [[--limit 0]]
against a limit.
It is stored with the record and read back with [[rec.get]], so a
session saved before it existed loads with [[None]], which reads as
\enquote{not recorded}, never as a claim about the endpoint.

[[order]] says how the provider ordered its answer
(\cref{sec:search-result-order} in the scholar module): [[relevance]],
[[date]], [[citations]] or [[unknown]], and [[None]] when not recorded.
It is a field of its own rather than a part of [[via]], because every
provider has an order while few have a second endpoint, and because
the place of a record in an answer
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/302}) is read against
it.  It follows the rules of [[via]]: not part of the key, since the
order a provider is asked for follows from the provider (and, for
Semantic Scholar, from query and limit, which the key and the limit
hold); nor part of [[record_rank]].  So the first run of a search
after Scopus and Web of Science began to be asked for relevance
replaces the record of an earlier, newest-first run of the same key as
any newer run does, by its date, and the limit, totals and dates rules
choose between records as before.  It is saved only when set, and read
back with [[rec.get]], so an older file loads with [[None]] and an
older Scholar ignores the key.

One kind of record cannot take part in that key.  Sessions written
before filters were stored (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/61},
June 2026) kept only the pairs of query and provider; loading them, or
records that later saves copied from them, gives records whose
[[filters]] is [[None]] because the filters are \emph{unknown}, not
because there were none.  Their key would claim an unfiltered search.
They can be told apart: the change that started storing filters
(commit [[1e02d34]], \#61) stored the limit with them, in the same new
[[SearchRecord]], and since \#55 (commit [[e478a05]], the same day)
[[Search.execute]] gives every result its limit, as [[rq]] does with
[[effective_limit]].  So a record from a search always has a limit,
and one with neither filters nor limit is a legacy record
([[is_legacy]]).  Code outside scholar that builds a [[SearchResult]]
without a limit, like the workaround in
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/135}, makes records that
look legacy too; they are then treated as the older searches are.

A legacy record is handled as before filters joined the key: a new run
of its query on its provider replaces it, whatever the new run's
filters (\cref{sec:legacy-records}).  Keeping it beside the new record
would make [[found-via]] print, for it, a command without filters that
may never have been run.

\label{sec:merged-records}
A second kind of old record cannot be split either.  Until
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/164} a multi-provider
[[search -n]] stored its merged result as one record: provider
[[openalex, dblp]], query [[q | q]], the deduplicated count of all
providers together and their errors joined, each prefixed with its
provider.  Searches now record one record per provider
(\cref{sec:review-found-by}), but sessions hold hundreds of the old
ones, and their papers link to them.  They load unchanged, since
rewriting them would invent per-provider counts that were never
stored.  What changes is how they are read: [[is_merged]] recognises
them by the comma that no provider name contains, and
[[search_outcome_label]] says what such a record is---\enquote{merged
record (providers openalex, dblp): outcome cannot be split}, with the
total and the provider-labelled errors after it---instead of a count
that a reader would take for each provider's.

<<search record properties>>=
@property
def merged_providers(self) -> list[str]:
    """The providers of a merged record, or `[provider]` for one search."""
    return [p.strip() for p in self.provider.split(",") if p.strip()]

@property
def is_merged(self) -> bool:
    """Whether the record is a merged one from before #164."""
    return len(self.merged_providers) > 1
@

\paragraph{Retired searches}
\label{sec:retired-searches}
A session keeps every search that was run with [[-n]]: the final
queries of a review, and also their first versions, malformed attempts
with zero hits and merged records from older versions.  The review's
method text describes only the final queries, while the audit caption
and the report counted every record (\enquote{DBLP failed for 21 of 22
queries}), so the two disagreed
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/275}).  Removing the
draft records would be wrong: the papers they returned stay in the
session and in the audit table, linked to them through [[found_by]]
(\cref{sec:review-found-by}), and a paper's provenance would then point
at a search the session no longer holds.  So a record can be
\emph{retired}: [[retired]] holds when ([[at]], a UTC ISO time), by
whom ([[by]], only as given) and why ([[note]]), and the record stays,
with its links.  The reports count the current records and state the
retired ones apart (\cref{sec:retired-in-reports}); nothing is hidden.

The mark is not part of the record's key: a retired search is still
that search, and its papers' links still name it.  Nor is it part of
[[record_rank]] (\cref{sec:wider-record}), which decides which run of a
search a session keeps; whether someone retired the search says
nothing about which run is the better answer.  A new run of the
search, with the same key, is a decision to run it again, so it makes
the search current: the new run's record carries no mark, and when the
rules keep it, it replaces the retired one
(\cref{sec:rerun-of-retired}).  When the rules keep the old record
instead (a failed re-run beside an answer, a narrower re-run beside a
wider one), the record of the search is still the retired one, and so
is the mark.

The field is saved only when set, so a session without retired
records is written as before, and a file from before the field loads
with [[None]].  A Scholar older than the field reads the file and
drops the mark at its next save, as it drops every key it does not
know; those searches then count as current again in every report, so
every installation must be updated before searches are retired.

<<search record properties>>=
@property
def is_retired(self) -> bool:
    """Whether the search was retired from the review's searches."""
    return self.retired is not None
@

To retire a record, a caller must be able to name it.  Its query alone
does not always do (one query with two filter sets is two records, and
a merged record's query is [[q | q]]), and its place in the session's
list does not stay put: [[create_review_session]] puts the records of
each new run first, and superseding a merged record removes one, so a
number that [[sessions show]] printed named another record after any
[[search -n]] in between.  The record's key does stay put, since it is
what makes the record that search (\cref{sec:search-record-key}), and
a re-run of the search keeps it.  So a record's id is the first
[[RECORD_ID_LENGTH]] hexadecimal digits of the SHA-256 digest of its
key, the query, the provider and the filters key joined by the unit
separator, which none of them contains.  Seven digits, as a short
commit hash has, keep the listing narrow; a caller may give any prefix
of at least [[RECORD_ID_MIN_PREFIX]] digits, and a prefix that two
records share is refused rather than resolved.

<<search record properties>>=
@property
def record_id(self) -> str:
    """A short id of the search: a digest of its key, stable across runs."""
    digest = hashlib.sha256("\x1f".join(self.key).encode("utf-8"))
    return digest.hexdigest()[:RECORD_ID_LENGTH]
<<review imports>>=
import hashlib
<<review constants>>=
RECORD_ID_LENGTH = 7
RECORD_ID_MIN_PREFIX = 4
<<review data structures>>=
def records_with_id_prefix(
    records: list[SearchRecord], prefix: str
) -> list[SearchRecord]:
    """The records whose `record_id` starts with `prefix` (any case)."""
    prefix = prefix.strip().lower()
    return [r for r in records if r.record_id.startswith(prefix)]
@

Three small functions serve every view: the records that count, the
ones retired, and the words that describe a retirement.  The label
names the date only, since the time of day adds nothing for a reader.
The note is stored on one line, its white space collapsed: it is shown
inside a line of [[sessions show]] and inside a cell of the report's
table, and a line break would break both.

<<review data structures>>=
def current_records(records: list[SearchRecord]) -> list[SearchRecord]:
    """The records of `records` that are not retired, in order."""
    return [r for r in records if r.retired is None]


def retired_records(records: list[SearchRecord]) -> list[SearchRecord]:
    """The retired records of `records`, in order."""
    return [r for r in records if r.retired is not None]


def retire_record(
    record: SearchRecord,
    at: str,
    note: str | None = None,
    by: str | None = None,
) -> None:
    """Mark `record` as retired at `at` (UTC ISO), with `note` and `by`.

    The note's white space is collapsed to single spaces; a note of
    white space only is stored as None.
    """
    note = " ".join((note or "").split()) or None
    record.retired = {"at": at, "by": by, "note": note}


def retired_label(record: SearchRecord) -> str:
    """Describe a record's retirement, e.g. "retired 2026-11-20: draft".

    "" for a current record.
    """
    return retirement_label(record.retired)


def retirement_label(mark: dict | None) -> str:
    """Describe a retirement mark as `retired_label` does; "" for None."""
    if mark is None:
        return ""
    label = ("retired " + str(mark.get("at") or "")[:10]).strip()
    if mark.get("by"):
        label += f" by {mark['by']}"
    if mark.get("note"):
        label += f": {mark['note']}"
    return label
@

<<test functions>>=
class TestRetiredSearchRecord:
  """A retired record keeps its key and is described in one place."""

  def test_mark_label_and_split(self):
    draft = SearchRecord("q", "dblp", None, 100, hits=3)
    final = SearchRecord("q", "openalex", None, 100, hits=5)
    key = draft.key
    retire_record(draft, "2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00", "first version",
                  "the author")
    assert draft.key == key and draft.is_retired
    assert draft.retired == {"at": "2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00",
                             "by": "the author", "note": "first version"}
    assert retired_label(draft) == (
      "retired 2026-11-20 by the author: first version"
    )
    assert retired_label(final) == ""
    assert current_records([draft, final]) == [final]
    assert retired_records([draft, final]) == [draft]

  def test_label_without_note_or_by(self):
    record = SearchRecord("q", "dblp")
    retire_record(record, "2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00")
    assert retired_label(record) == "retired 2026-11-20"

  def test_not_part_of_the_rank(self):
    record = SearchRecord("q", "dblp", None, 100, hits=3)
    rank = record_rank(record)
    retire_record(record, "2026-11-20T10:00:00+00:00")
    assert record_rank(record) == rank
