The append looks a returned paper's previous decision up by id, one
decision per id.  A session can hold two under one id, though: a work
decided apart as two copies, which a re-run of a session without a
session file leaves (\cref{sec:decision-store-fallback}) and the copy
lists show (\cref{sec:session-copies}).  The lookup kept the last of
them, the restore below added only what the lookup held, and so the
next append dropped the other copy, with only the save's warning that
the decided papers had become fewer
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/228}).  Now the
lookup holds the first decision of each id, and the other copies are
carried through as they are, after the conversion
([[<<carry the other copies of an id through>>]]).  The returned paper
attaches to the first copy only.  The first, because the order of a
session's decisions is kept through an append, and with it which copy
is which (the save matches copies by their order,
\cref{sec:session-integrity}); and in the pairs the re-run leaves, the
first copy is the one a search reached, the one beside it a record
restored after the conversion, so the returned paper goes to the copy
it went to before.  Nothing in the two decisions would name a better
choice: they differ, and which is right is the user's to say.

