\paragraph{A re-run of a retired search}
\label{sec:rerun-of-retired}
A retired search (\cref{sec:retired-searches}) that is run again with
[[-n]] was run again on purpose, so it is current again.  Nothing in
the merge above needs to change for that: the new run's record is a
new [[SearchRecord]] without a mark, and where the rules keep it, it
takes the retired record's place.  Where they keep the retired record
(the re-run failed outright, or asked for fewer results), the session's
record of the search is still the one that was retired, and the mark
stays with it.  Either outcome changes what the reports count, so the
run says so, one line per search on standard error.  The lines go
through the logger at warning level, the level the command line shows
by default, as the module's other notices about a session do; this
module never prints.

<<say what the run did to retired searches>>=
run_keys = {r.key for r in current_run}
kept_ids = {id(r) for r in session.search_records}
for record in retired_records(previous_session.search_records):
    if id(record) not in kept_ids:
        logger.warning(
            "search %r on %s was retired (%s); running it again makes "
            "it current", record.query, record.provider,
            retired_label(record),
        )
    elif record.key in run_keys:
        logger.warning(
            "search %r on %s was run again, but the session keeps its "
            "earlier record (the new run failed or asked for fewer "
            "results), which stays retired", record.query,
            record.provider,
        )
@

Let's verify the three runs: an answered re-run replaces the retired
record and says so; a failed re-run leaves the retired answer and its
mark, and says that too; and a run of another query leaves the mark
without a word.

