\paragraph{A namesake's record.}
The store is keyed by paper id, and a DOI-less paper's id is a hash of
its title and first author, so the record the lookup finds may be that
of another work with the same title and first author: an editorial
column, a talk given twice, a report and the article written years
later.  Until November 2026 such a namesake took over the record's
decision whole and appeared already screened, with the other work's
verdict and tags
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/203}).  A session
file's decisions already ask [[plausibly_same_work]] here
(\cref{sec:session-copies}), and the store follows the same rule, in
both of its forms, rather than a second one:
\begin{description}
\item[Under the legacy hash id] A paper with a DOI is looked up under
  its legacy hash id as well, for a paper that gained a DOI since it
  was decided.  A record found there whose work cannot be the returned
  one is dropped, as [[prev_dec]] is: the returned paper is new and
  gets a pending decision of its own, with the search's links.  The
  record is not lost.  Its id is not among the returned ones, so the
  restore above brings it back under its own id with its own decision,
  and without links, since no search of this run returned that work.
  The record is judged as the paper it was decided on, which had no
  DOI, or the store would have keyed it by the DOI.  The paper rebuilt
  from it can have one all the same, an arXiv DOI derived from the
  record's arXiv URL; held against the published version's DOI, that
  derived DOI would make the preprint's record another work, and the
  published version, which took the decision before
  (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/203}), would come out
  pending.  So the comparison leaves the rebuilt DOI out and asks only
  whether the years agree.
\item[Under the paper's own id] Two DOI-less works sharing the hash
  share the record's key.  The record keeps its decision and its paper,
  and the returned work is noted in the decision's [[same_id_copies]]
  with its year, venue and provider, as [[is_same_id_namesake]] does
  for a session file.  This branch must add the record's decision to
  the session itself: the restore skips records whose ids the search
  returned.  The search link is not recorded, because the search did
  not return the record's work.
\end{description}
A paper with a DOI is never a namesake of a record under that DOI: one
DOI is one work, and [[is_same_id_namesake]] asks for a DOI-less paper
too.  [[is_record_namesake]] does not compare ids, unlike
[[is_same_id_namesake]], because a record's paper need not have the id
it is stored under (below): a DOI-less paper is looked up under its own
id only, so the record it finds is under that id, also when the
rebuilt paper has a DOI.  The decision kept is the one the restore
would have added ([[restored_decision]]), since it stays the record's.

[[store_record_of]] is the lookup, both places of it: the record under
the paper's own id, else, for a paper with a DOI, the one under its
legacy hash id, if it may be the paper's work.

<<review session functions>>=
def store_record_of(paper: Paper, store: Any) -> tuple[str | None, Any]:
    """The key and record of `paper` in the old per-query decision store.

    `store` is a `notes.SearchDecisions` or `None`.  The record under
    the paper's own id, else, for a paper with a DOI, the one under its
    legacy hash id when it is plausibly the same work, judged without
    a DOI (a record under a hash id was decided without one).
    `(None, None)` when there is none.
    """
    if store is None:
        return None, None
    paper_id = get_paper_id(paper)
    record = store.decisions.get(paper_id)
    if record is not None:
        return paper_id, record
    if not paper.doi:
        return None, None
    legacy_id = legacy_hash_paper_id(paper.title, paper.authors)
    record = store.decisions.get(legacy_id)
    if record is None:
        return None, None
    decided = replace(paper_from_decision_record(record), doi=None)
    if not plausibly_same_work(paper, decided):
        return None, None
    return legacy_id, record
@

A record under a legacy hash id that a returned paper takes over must
not be restored as well.  Its key is not among the returned ids, so the
restore added it beside the DOI copy that took its decision, two
decided copies of one work
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/211}).  But whether a
returned paper takes the record is known only once it is converted:
the branches before the store lookup can catch it first, when another
decision of the session is the same work (a restored record whose
rebuilt paper has the returned id, a published version the returned
preprint names, an adopted preprint).  A first fix decided beforehand
that every record the lookup would find was taken, and a record whose
paper such a branch caught was then neither used nor restored: a
decided record vanished.  So the restore leaves these records out only
for now.  It asks the lookup which keys the returned papers seek
([[store_keys_sought]], each paper normalised as the conversion does
it, since the homonym number would change its legacy hash), the
conversion notes each key the store branch uses, and after the
conversion every sought key no paper used is restored as usual.

A record left over this way stands as its own copy beside the decision
that caught its paper.  Folding it into that decision would have to
choose between two decisions of one work, which may disagree, and that
choice is not the append's to make: as for every other pair of copies
an append leaves, the session's copy lists may show the pair and
[[sessions reconcile]] folds those it may (\cref{sec:session-copies}).
Not every such pair is listed yet: two DOI-less decisions under one id
are not, and a restored DBLP record keeps its homonym number, so its
work key differs from its copy's
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/215}).  A record under a
returned paper's own id, which the restore before the conversion skips
as returned, is sought as well: it too is restored after the loop when
an earlier branch caught its paper, rather than lost.

<<review session functions>>=
def store_keys_sought(results: list[Any], store: Any) -> set[str]:
    """The keys of the store records that papers in `results` look up.

    Each paper is normalised and looked up as `create_review_session`
    converts it (`store_record_of`); whether it takes the record is
    decided by the conversion.
    """
    sought = set()
    for result in results:
        for paper in result.papers:
            paper = without_homonym_numbers(
                _normalize_paper_identifiers(paper)
            )
            key, _ = store_record_of(paper, store)
            if key is not None:
                sought.add(key)
    return sought
