A record a returned paper would take over under its legacy hash id is
restored only after the conversion, and only if no paper took it.  The
tests hold the store record [[H]] of a work, discarded, beside a second
record of it that reaches the returned paper first, through one of the
branches that come before the store lookup.  In the first, a DBLP
record from before the homonym numbers were stripped
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/120}) is kept under the
hash of [[Ada Lovelace 0001]], and its arXiv URL rebuilds it with the
arXiv DOI the search returns; the returned paper merges into it
([[existing]]).  In the second, the published version's record is kept
under its DOI, and the search returns the preprint with the
published DOI among its versions, as an adopted preprint has it
([[replaced]]).  In both, [[H]] must stand beside, with its decision.
The third test is the lookup's own normalisation: a returned paper
whose author still carries the homonym number takes the record under
the plain hash, and is the only decision.

