\paragraph{Renames.}
\label{sec:session-renames}
A fold is not the only way a paper changes its id.  A paper is
\emph{renamed} when the session goes on holding it, with its decision,
under another id: at an append, when a DBLP homonym number or a name
written surname first is dropped from a DOI-less id
(\cref{sec:review-renamed-authors,sec:review-surname-first}) and when a
paper whose preprint was adopted elsewhere arrives and its published
entry takes the preprint's identity; and when [[scholar enrich]] or the
TUI adopts a preprint (\cref{preprint-module}).  A DOI-less decision
met by its DOI copy is no rename: the append keeps it and adds the DOI
copy beside it, and only [[sessions reconcile]] folds the two
(above), which repoints the stored ids as every fold does.  Paper
ids stored elsewhere in the session then named nothing: the papers of
an LLM review sample and of a sample of a tier's rest, drawn before the
rename, counted as \enquote{not found}
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/319}).  The tier
sample looked such a paper up through its version links, which an
adoption leaves and the other renames do not.

Every rename is therefore recorded in the session, in one place:
[[renamed]], a map from the old id to the new one, saved as
[[renamed]] when it holds any, united with the file's on every save
and carried over by an append, exactly as [[folded]] is.  Whoever reads
a stored paper id asks [[current_paper_id]], which follows both maps.
It is a map of its own, not more entries in [[folded]], because a
tombstone means more than \enquote{this id is now that one}, and the
extra meaning is wrong for a rename.  [[apply_tombstones]] would be
safe: a rename leaves no copy under the old id, so a copy found there
is one a stale process brought back.  But an append routes a returned
paper of a folded-away id to the folded-into decision only if
[[plausibly_same_work]] allows it, and notes it as a namesake
otherwise.  A published version returning after its preprint was
adopted can be years younger than the preprint, so as a tombstone the
adoption would turn the returning published version into a namesake,
where the append now folds it into the adopted entry by its version
link (above).  Folding is the business of [[sessions reconcile]]; a
rename only follows a paper that is already one decision.

The note and decision records follow a rename through
[[migrate_paper_id]] (\cref{sec:notes-migrate-paper-id} in the notes
chapter); an append's conversion is one [[moving_records]] batch, so
with the notes lock held by another process it waits once, not once
per rename, and one warning names the notes left for
[[sessions reconcile]], which finds them through this record.

[[record_rename]] records one rename and repoints the stored ids at
once; [[repoint_paper_ids]] does the repointing for both maps, for the
snowball rounds' source papers and both kinds of sample, and runs again
whenever the session is read from a file and after every merge with
the file on save.  It looks at the decisions only when something is
stored to repoint: an append builds its session without the samples,
which come in from the file at the save, so the renames of a large
append cost no pass over the decisions each.  So a sample drawn in another process, merged in at
a save, or stored in a file written before this record existed, is
repointed as soon as a rename record names its paper's old id.  A
rename made before this record existed left no trace (except the
version link of an adoption, which is no rename record); its drawn
paper stays \enquote{not found} in [[llm sample --report]] and in the
tier sample's counts, as before.

A stored id that some decision of the session still holds is not
repointed, though a map names it.  That decision is the one the sample
drew: a copy a stale process kept apart beside its fold
(\cref{sec:session-copies}), or a paper that a later search brought
back under the old id after the rename.  Repointing it would make the
sample report another decision than the one drawn.

A chain of renames and folds (a paper renamed, then folded; an id
folded into one renamed later) is followed to its end through both
maps, with the same guard against a cycle as [[resolve_folded_id]].
Where both maps name one id (possible only when a later search brought
a paper back under it), the fold is followed, as before renames were
recorded.

A paper can be renamed back: [[a]] to [[b]], then [[b]] to [[a]].
[[record_rename]] then drops the entry it undoes, so a session keeps
[[b]] to [[a]] only.  The merge with the file on save unites the maps,
though, and the file may still hold the older [[a]] to [[b]], which
would make a cycle of two entries; each process that saved one of them
had it right when it saved.  After the union, [[settle_rename_cycles]]
keeps of such a pair the entry that leads to the id a decision holds
and drops the other; if a decision holds both ids or neither, the pair
stays, and [[current_paper_id]]'s guard ends the walk.  Longer cycles
cannot arise from renames, which always lead to the id just given.

<<review session functions>>=
def current_paper_id(
    session: ReviewSession, paper_id: str, held: set[str] | None = None
) -> str:
    """The id under which `session` now holds the paper stored as `paper_id`.

    Follows the fold tombstones (`folded`) and the renames (`renamed`)
    to the end of the chain. An id in `held` (the ids of the session's
    decisions, computed when not given) is its own current id.
    """
    if held is None:
        held = {get_paper_id(d.paper) for d in session.decisions}
    seen = {paper_id}
    while paper_id not in held:
        following = session.folded.get(paper_id) or session.renamed.get(
            paper_id
        )
        if following is None or following in seen:
            break
        paper_id = following
        seen.add(paper_id)
    return paper_id


def settle_rename_cycles(session: ReviewSession) -> None:
    """Drop the entry of a rename pair `a -> b`, `b -> a` that leads away
    from the id a decision of `session` holds."""
    held = {get_paper_id(d.paper) for d in session.decisions}
    for old_id, new_id in list(session.renamed.items()):
        if session.renamed.get(new_id) != old_id:
            continue
        if old_id in held and new_id not in held:
            del session.renamed[old_id]


def record_rename(session: ReviewSession, old_id: str, new_id: str) -> None:
    """Record that `session` now holds the paper `old_id` as `new_id`.

    Repoints the paper ids stored in the session (`repoint_paper_ids`).
    """
    if old_id == new_id:
        return
    if session.renamed.get(new_id) == old_id:
        del session.renamed[new_id]
    session.renamed[old_id] = new_id
    repoint_paper_ids(session)


def repoint_paper_ids(session: ReviewSession) -> None:
    """Repoint stored paper ids of folded-away or renamed papers.

    The source papers of the snowball rounds and the drawn papers of
    both kinds of sample; an id a decision still holds stays.
    """
    if not session.folded and not session.renamed:
        return
    if not (session.snowball_rounds or session.llm_samples
            or session.tier_samples):
        return
    held = {get_paper_id(d.paper) for d in session.decisions}
    for snowball_round in session.snowball_rounds:
        snowball_round.source_papers = [
            current_paper_id(session, paper_id, held)
            for paper_id in snowball_round.source_papers
        ]
    for sample in session.llm_samples:
        for paper in sample.papers:
            if paper.get("paper_id"):
                paper["paper_id"] = current_paper_id(
                    session, paper["paper_id"], held
                )
    for draw in session.tier_samples:
        draw.papers = [
            current_paper_id(session, paper_id, held)
            for paper_id in draw.papers
        ]
