The record is united on every save, like the tombstones.  Two real
processes race: one appends a search that drops a homonym number, one
draws a sample of the tier's rest under the old id, and each saves
while the other has loaded the session before it.  In both orders the
file keeps the rename, and the sample finds the paper.  Where the
renaming process saves last, its merge brings the sample in and
repoints it.  Where the sampling process saves last, it still holds the
paper's decision under the old id, which a stale process writes back
(as a stale process writes back a copy folded away while it held it,
\cref{sec:session-copies}); the sample then names that decision, which
a decision holds, so it is not repointed and is found all the same.
The two decisions the file then holds for one paper are a known gap,
not repaired here
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/327}): renames have
no tombstone drop of the untouched stale copy.

