Two processes can also rename one paper two ways: one adopts a
preprint for it (as [[scholar enrich]] does, through
[[record_rename]]), while the other's append drops the homonym number
from its id.  Each loaded the session before the other saved.  The
saved file then holds the rename of the process that saved last (the
union lets our entry win, as for every map entry), and both decisions:
the renamed one of each process, since each process's merge pairs only
copies under one id and the two new ids differ.  So one paper is held
twice, under the adopted DOI and under the plain hash, which is the
gap of a stale process writing back a renamed paper
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/327}).  Neither
decision is lost, and the sample drawn before both names an id a
decision holds, in both orders the adopted one: the adopting process
repoints the sample it holds, and the appending process never holds
the sample (an append builds its session without samples, which come
in from the file at its save) and so takes the file's sample as it
is, whose id a decision holds and is therefore not repointed.  The
sample thus does not always follow the map's entry; it follows the
first process that repointed it and saved.  The map keeps one entry per old
id, so when the appending process saves last, the adoption's entry,
from the old id to the adopted DOI, is not in the file at all: the
adopted decision is then held under an id no rename leads to.  The
sample is unaffected (it names that id, which a decision holds), and
the lost entry is part of the same gap
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/327}).

