\paragraph{What is left for the user.}
What is not folded is listed, never silently kept.  Four kinds remain,
all found by reading the session only, so [[sessions show]] and the
report can list them for any session, old ones included, without
changing it:
\begin{description}
\item[Foldable copies] ([[foldable_copies]]): pairs that
  [[sessions reconcile]] would fold.  They are counted with a hint to
  run it.
\item[Conflicting copies] ([[conflicting_copies]]): a pair decided with
  different statuses, and a decision under whose DOI-less id a search
  also returned a work of a distant year.
\item[Copies decided alike, not merged] ([[alike_copies]]): a pair with
  the same status but different tags or sources.  Folding them would
  mean choosing tags or a source for the user.
\item[Possible copies, not reconciled] ([[possible_copies]]): DOI-less
  decisions that the audit table would merge with a DOI decision
  ([[audit_work_key]]) but that are in none of the lists above.  The
  pairing uses the exact title-and-surname hash, while the audit
  table's key ignores punctuation, case and year, so it joins more: on
  copies of the real sessions behind issue~\#170, the review of this
  change found 2\,995 DOI-less records that share a work key with a DOI
  record, of which only 403 were pairs.  The rest, and the pairs that
  cannot be folded (no year or author, a pending copy with tags or a
  machine's trace), are counted here, so that nothing goes
  unreconciled without being said.  Folding is not widened to that key:
  it ignores the year, and a fold is not taken back.
\end{description}
A pair here is first a DOI-less copy and its DOI copy, the pairs
[[sessions reconcile]] works on.  The other kind is two decisions under
one id.  A session should hold one decision per id, but a re-run of a
session without a session file can leave two: the store's record of an
id is restored after the conversion when no returned paper took it, and
a returned paper can have reached that id through another decision, a
Scopus record written surname first and renamed to it
(\cref{sec:decision-store-fallback},
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/215}).  Such a pair is
listed like the others when both are decided and their decisions
differ ([[same_id_pairs]]): as conflicting when the statuses differ,
as alike when only tags or sources do.  Two copies in one state are a
duplicate entry, not a decision to make, and a pending copy beside a
decided one is not a second verdict, so neither is listed.  The pairs
are taken for every id, a DOI included: one id is one paper of the
session, whatever made it.

[[sessions reconcile]] leaves these pairs alone.  A fold moves a
DOI-less id onto its DOI copy's id and leaves a tombstone from the one
to the other; two copies under one id have no other id to move to, and
since their decisions differ, keeping one means choosing between them,
which is the user's to do.  The pair lasts until then: an append
carries every copy of an id through (\cref{sec:review-create-strict}),
a save tells the copies apart by their order
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}), and [[sessions decide]] refuses to
decide copies decided apart unless told [[--all-copies]], which is how
the user settles them (the command chapter's [[sessions decide]]).

<<review session functions>>=
def same_id_pairs(
    session: ReviewSession,
) -> list[tuple[ReviewDecision, ReviewDecision]]:
    """Pairs of decided decisions under one paper id that differ.

    In session order; a third copy of an id pairs with each of the
    others.
    """
    by_id: dict[str, list[ReviewDecision]] = {}
    for decision in session.decisions:
        if decision.is_decided:
            by_id.setdefault(get_paper_id(decision.paper), []).append(decision)
    return [
        (first, second)
        for copies in by_id.values()
        for i, first in enumerate(copies)
        for second in copies[i + 1:]
        if decision_verdict(first) != decision_verdict(second)
    ]
