The copies a re-run of a session without a session file leaves beside
each other come from the probes of the review that raised
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/215}.  In the first, a
Scopus record written surname first is renamed to the returned paper's
id, and the store's own record of that id is restored after the loop
(\cref{sec:decision-store-fallback}): two decisions under one id, one
discarded, one kept.  In the second, a DBLP record from before the
homonym numbers were stripped rebuilds with the returned arXiv DOI and
takes the returned paper, while the store's record under the plain hash
stands beside it: a DOI-less copy and a DOI copy.  The DOI is a
preprint's, which [[doi_copy_of]] does not pair, so the DOI-less copy
is a possible copy, and that only once the DOI copy's first author has
lost the number.  Two cases
built directly pin the rest: copies under one id decided alike but
tagged apart are listed as alike, and neither of them again as a
possible copy of the preprint beside them; copies under one id in the
same state, or not both decided, are not listed.  Last, the number is
stripped only where it does not change the paper's id: a DOI-less
record keeps the id it is stored under, its note stays attached, and
the append's renaming still moves the note when the stripped copy comes
back.

