A link's place is saved as [[rank]] in the link's object, and only when
known, so a file without places reads as before and a link without one
is written as before.  A stored place that is not a whole number from
one is read as unknown rather than trusted ([[stored_rank]]): it is a
hand-editable file.  A Scholar from before places reads a link through
the two keys and the filters above and ignores any other key of the
object, so it loads such a file and drops the places on its next save,
as it does with the other fields it does not know.

Let's verify that each link gets the paper's place in its own answer
and keeps it through what happens to links: a merge of two providers,
a deduplicated answer, saving and loading, a second query, a re-run of
the same search (the smaller place stays), saves of two processes at
once in both orders, and a fold of two copies; and that a link from an
older file loads without a place.

