A session can hold two decisions under one id, copies of one work
decided apart (\cref{sec:session-copies}).  Keyed by paper id alone,
the baseline held one state for both, the file's copies one decision,
and the merge met each of our copies with the file's last one: a change
another process made to the first copy was lost, since the last copy
was unchanged and so taken for nothing new, and the last copy's links
passed to the first
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/228}).  So the
decisions are keyed by their occurrence when the baseline is taken:
the paper id for the first decision of an id, the id and [[#2]],
[[#3]], \dots\ for the copies after it, in session order
([[decision_keys]]).  With one decision per id, which is every session
but these, a key is the paper id, so the baseline and everything that
reads it by paper id are unchanged.  The baseline also maps each key to
its paper id ([[decision_ids]]), which the merge needs to know which of
the copies it loaded a session no longer holds under their id
(\cref{sec:copies-in-the-merge}).  The paper states stay keyed by paper
id: when a session is loaded, the paper registry hands every copy of
an id the same [[Paper]] object, so the copies have one paper state.

A key names a place in the session as it was when the baseline was
taken, and places move: this process renames a copy, and the next copy
of its id becomes the first; another process drops, swaps or inserts
copies.  So each decision also carries its key from then
([[ReviewDecision.baseline_key]]), set where a baseline is taken from
the session's own order ([[assign_baseline_keys]]): when the file is
loaded, when it has been saved, and when a session that loaded nothing
takes its built state.  [[dataclasses.replace]] carries the key over,
as the append does for a decision it continues.  The key stands for
the decision only while the decision is still under the paper id it had
then ([[base_key_of]]).  A decision made since has none, and one whose
id has changed (renamed, or the survivor of a fold, which takes the DOI
copy's id) answers for the id it now has; both are looked up by their
paper id, as every decision was before copies were told apart.  A fold's
survivor so meets the baseline of the DOI copy it replaced, as it
always did.

<<review data structures>>=
def decision_keys(decisions: list[ReviewDecision]) -> list[str]:
    """The baseline key of each decision, in order.

    The paper id for the first decision of an id; for later decisions
    under the same id, the id with `#2`, `#3`, ... appended.
    """
    seen: dict[str, int] = {}
    keys = []
    for decision in decisions:
        paper_id = get_paper_id(decision.paper)
        seen[paper_id] = seen.get(paper_id, 0) + 1
        n = seen[paper_id]
        keys.append(paper_id if n == 1 else f"{paper_id}#{n}")
    return keys


def assign_baseline_keys(session: "ReviewSession") -> None:
    """Give each decision of `session` its key by the session's order."""
    for key, decision in zip(decision_keys(session.decisions),
                             session.decisions):
        decision.baseline_key = key


def base_key_of(
    decision: ReviewDecision, base: "SessionBaseline | None"
) -> str:
    """The key of `decision` in `base`.

    Its own key while its paper id is still the one it had then, else
    its paper id.
    """
    key = decision.baseline_key
    paper_id = get_paper_id(decision.paper)
    if key is not None and base is not None \
            and base.decision_ids.get(key) == paper_id:
        return key
    return paper_id


@dataclass
class SessionBaseline:
    """A session's decisions, papers and research context as loaded or
    saved.

    `save_session` compares against it to tell the changes made in this
    process from the changes another process saved meanwhile.
    `decisions` is keyed by `decision_keys`, and `decision_ids` maps
    each of those keys to its paper id.  `retired` maps each search
    record's key to its retirement mark (a copy; None when current).
    """
    decisions: dict[str, tuple]
    research_context: str | None
    record_keys: frozenset = frozenset()
    papers: dict[str, tuple] = field(default_factory=dict)
    decision_ids: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)
    retired: dict[tuple, dict | None] = field(default_factory=dict)

    @classmethod
    def of(
        cls, session: "ReviewSession", *, from_file: bool = False,
        stored: dict[str, Paper] | None = None,
    ) -> "SessionBaseline":
        """Record the current state of `session`.

        Paper states are recorded only `from_file`: when the session's
        papers are as its file holds them, just loaded or just saved.
        `stored` maps a paper id to the paper as the file holds it, where
        that differs from the session's paper (see `read_session_file`).
        """
        stored = stored or {}
        decisions, papers, ids = {}, {}, {}
        keys = decision_keys(session.decisions)
        for key, decision in zip(keys, session.decisions):
            paper_id = get_paper_id(decision.paper)
            decisions[key] = decision_state(decision)
            ids[key] = paper_id
            if from_file:
                papers[paper_id] = paper_state(
                    stored.get(paper_id, decision.paper)
                )
        return cls(
            decisions=decisions,
            research_context=session.research_context,
            record_keys=frozenset(r.key for r in session.search_records),
            papers=papers,
            decision_ids=ids,
            retired={
                r.key: dict(r.retired) if r.retired is not None else None
                for r in session.search_records
            },
        )
