Each paper's fields are recorded too, so that a save can tell a field
another process changed from one this process changed
(\cref{sec:paper-three-way}).  The baseline must keep \emph{copies}
of the values.  Papers are shared through the registry, and some code
changes them in place ([[clean_paper_text]] rewrites the title, venue
and abstract on the object; any list field can be appended to), so a
baseline holding references would change along with the paper and
such a change would look like no change.  [[paper_state]] records the
fields a session file stores ([[Paper.to_dict]], so a field added to
[[Paper]] and its serialisation is covered without a change here) as a
tuple of immutable values: lists become tuples and dicts sorted tuples
of their items, recursively.  Strings are immutable already and are
shared, not copied, which keeps a baseline small.  [[None]] and an
empty list stay apart ([[None]] against [[()]]), since for the check
fields they mean \enquote{never checked} and \enquote{checked, nothing
found} (\cref{crossref-module,unpaywall-module}).

\label{sec:paper-baseline}
Paper states are recorded only from a file: when [[read_session_file]]
loads one and when [[save_session]] has written one ([[from_file]]),
since only then are the session's papers the file's.  A baseline that
[[create_review_session]] builds (\cref{sec:session-own-file}) holds
decision states only, and so do the papers that a same-file append
adds to the loaded baseline from the built state.  A built decision
state is a fair ancestor: the build makes a paper pending or restores
its stored decision, and nothing else.  A built paper is not.  Its
fields come from this run's search results and from whatever file was
loaded, while the file the session is saved to holds its own copy,
gathered by other runs (another provider, another limit, a cached
answer of another date).  Where the two differ, nothing says that the
file's value replaced ours rather than that the two were gathered
apart, so the merge must not take the file's value for a change.
Without a paper state the gap fill decides ([[paper_filled_from]]):
the file's copy fills what ours lacks, and where both hold a value,
ours is kept, as for a decision changed on both sides, the file's
value staying in the [[.bak]].  The Crossref notices are the
exception: both copies' notices are kept (\cref{sec:united-notices}),
since a retraction lost costs more than a withdrawn notice kept.  This
holds in the three cases a built baseline covers.
\begin{description}
\item[An append into another file] (\cref{sec:session-own-file}).
    The target's copy and ours were gathered by different runs, possibly
  for different questions, so neither is the newer.  A retraction
  the target holds survives our \enquote{checked, clean} from the
  loaded file only through the union of notices.
\item[A paper new in a same-file append.]  Another process that
  appended to the same file may have found the same paper first; its
  copy came from its own search, so it is a second gathering, not a
  change of ours.
\item[Two processes creating one new session.]  Both build from
  their own results, which need not be the same answer; the copy the
  first saved fills our gaps (its enrichment survives) but does not
  replace our values.
\end{description}
After the first save, the session's baseline is taken from the file it
wrote, so from then on its papers are judged three ways.

The cost was measured on a copy of the largest real session in
November 2026 (11\,162 papers, a 57\,MB file): the paper states held
about 5\,MB of the 86\,MB the loaded session took, and loading took
about a tenth longer (3.4 instead of 3.1\,s, the median of seven loads
each).  Reading the fields with one
[[attrgetter]] and freezing them with [[map]] saved about a sixth of
the states' time over a loop of [[getattr]] calls.  A digest per
paper instead of the values would save the memory but not the time,
since it is computed from the same values.  So we keep the values,
which also lets the merge compare one field at a time.

<<review data structures>>=
PAPER_STATE_FIELDS = tuple(Paper(title="", authors=[]).to_dict())
_paper_state_values = operator.attrgetter(*PAPER_STATE_FIELDS)


def frozen_value(value):
    """Return `value` as an immutable copy, comparable with `==`.

    Lists and tuples become tuples, dicts sorted tuples of their items;
    everything else (strings, numbers, None) is returned as it is.
    """
    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple)):
        return tuple(map(frozen_value, value))
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return tuple(sorted(zip(value, map(frozen_value, value.values()))))
    return value


def paper_state(paper: Paper) -> tuple:
    """Return the stored fields of `paper` (`PAPER_STATE_FIELDS`), frozen."""
    return tuple(map(frozen_value, _paper_state_values(paper)))
