\paragraph{The retirement mark of a search both hold.}
\label{sec:retired-in-merge}
A search record is chosen whole: of our record and the file's record of
one key, [[session_preferred_record]] keeps one, by the rank of the run
(\cref{sec:wider-record}).  The retirement mark
(\cref{sec:retired-searches}) is no part of that rank, and it is set by
a command that changes nothing else, so it would be lost whenever the
other side's record was kept, or whenever our record was kept and the
other process had retired it: a stale process saving a decision
would bring a retired search back into the counts, or drop a
retirement made after it loaded.

Where both records are of the same run, the mark is therefore merged
like a field of a decision, three ways against the baseline, which
holds each record's mark as loaded or saved ([[SessionBaseline.retired]]):
a side that left the mark as it was yields to a side that changed it,
and when both changed it, ours is kept, as for a decision changed on
both sides: two processes that retire one search with different notes
keep the note of the one that saves last, and the other note is lost
(it stays in the [[.bak]] of that save).  Without a baseline mark for the key (a search new on
both sides, or a session without a baseline) ours is kept as well.
A baseline that [[create_review_session]] builds for a session that
loaded nothing (\cref{sec:session-own-file}) holds no mark: such a
session's records are this run's, and a new run is never retired.  The same run is
the same time, limit and outcome ([[same_run]]): only those records
can stand for each other.  Where the runs differ, the kept record is
the newer or better answer, and a new run of a search makes it current
(\cref{sec:rerun-of-retired}), so its own mark stands: retiring the old
run in one process while another ran the search again leaves the
search current.

<<settle the retirement mark of a search both hold>>=
if same_run(ours, record):
    mark = merged_retirement(
        ours.retired, record.retired,
        base.retired if base else {}, record.key,
    )
    if kept.retired != mark:
        kept = replace(kept, retired=mark)
<<review session functions>>=
def same_run(a: SearchRecord, b: SearchRecord) -> bool:
    """Whether two records of one search record the same run."""
    return (
        (a.searched_at, a.fetched_at, a.limit, a.hits, a.error)
        == (b.searched_at, b.fetched_at, b.limit, b.hits, b.error)
    )


def merged_retirement(
    ours: dict | None, theirs: dict | None, base: dict, key: tuple
) -> dict | None:
    """The retirement mark of a run both sides hold, merged three ways.

    `base` maps record keys to the marks as loaded; without an entry
    for `key`, or when both sides changed the mark, `ours` is kept.
    """
    if key in base and ours == base[key] and theirs != base[key]:
        return theirs
    return ours
