Let's verify it with two real processes, in both orders.  In the first,
another process retires the search while this one holds the session,
and this process then saves a decision: the mark stays, and so does
the decision.  In the second, this process retires the search while
another holds the session, and the other saves a decision afterwards.
An undo made in another process is kept the same way.  Then, in one
process, since only records are involved and they do not pass through
the paper registry: both sides changed the mark, and ours is kept; and
a newer run saved by another process stays current although this
process retired the older one.

