\paragraph{Which copy in the file is ours.}
\label{sec:copies-in-the-merge}
The merge meets each of our decisions with the file's decision of the
same paper ([[pair_with_file]]).  With one decision per id there is
nothing to choose.  With copies of a work under one id
(\cref{sec:session-copies}) there is, and the places of the copies are
no safe guide, since they move between the load and the save: a first
version that paired the copies by place
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/pull/238}, review) paired a
copy with the wrong one whenever this process renamed a copy before it,
or another process dropped, swapped, inserted or folded one, and
statuses, tags and links passed between the copies.  So each id's
copies are paired in two passes.
\begin{description}
\item[By state] A copy the other process has not changed is in the file
  as we loaded it.  So our copy is paired with a file copy whose
  decision is the one our baseline holds for it ([[base_key_of]]).
\item[By place] What is left (a copy the other process changed, a
  decision made since the load) is paired in order with the file copies
  left, as one decision per id always was.
\end{description}
Links, same-id notes, paper fields and decision changes then pass only
between the copies so paired.

A file copy left unpaired is either one this session loaded and has
since renamed, folded or dropped, which must not come back, or one
another process added, which must be added.  The baseline tells them
apart: the copies of the id that it holds and that none of our copies
of the id carries as its key were loaded and are gone from here
([[decision_ids]]).  Each of them accounts for one unpaired file copy,
one with its loaded decision first, else any; the file copies left
over after that are new and are added.  With one decision per id this
is the rule the merge always had: a file decision whose id we loaded
and no longer hold is not brought back, any other is added.

