The first pass looks for the decision as loaded among the file copies
still free.  Copies in one state are interchangeable for the decision
but not for the rest: two copies kept alike can have been found by
different searches, and pairing them crosswise after another process
swapped them gave each the other's links.  So among the copies in the
state looked for, the one with our links and our provider is taken,
then one with either, then the first.  The place a copy had when loaded
is no guide here, since another process may have put an equal copy
before it.  Only a decision that answers for its own key
takes part:
one made since, or one whose id has changed, has no loaded state of its
own to look for, and the state of the copy whose id it borrows would
pair it with that copy.
The state looked for is the whole [[decision_state]], the human's
review record included, not the [[decision_verdict]] that copies are
compared by: here one decision is looked for in another version of the
file, and two copies decided alike at two moments differ only in their
records, which is exactly what tells them apart.  A copy whose record
another process has since replaced is no longer in its loaded state and
is paired by place, like any other changed copy.

