\paragraph{Merging a paper both processes hold.}
A paper's fields are facts gathered by searches and enrichment, and
scholar hardly ever clears one once it is known (in November 2026 the
sources assigned [[None]] to a paper field directly only in tests; the
one clearing we know of is the Unpaywall lookup's, below), so a field
that one copy has and the other lacks was gathered by one process and
not yet by the other, not removed on purpose.  The first rule is
therefore to fill gaps, the rule a re-run already applies to a stored
paper ([[stored_paper_with]], \cref{sec:review-create-strict}):
[[Paper.merge_with]] with this process's copy first.  Our value wins
where both have one; the file's fills the rest; sources and keywords
are united and the higher citation count kept.  The ids are equal by
construction (the file's decisions are looked up by our paper's id),
which is all [[merge_with]] asks.  Gaps are the common case of
enrichment (abstracts, keywords, PDF links).

Filling alone is not enough.  Where both copies hold a value, ours
would win even when the other process \emph{replaced} it after our
load: a newer Crossref result replacing an older one ([[updated_by]],
[[newer_versions]]; an empty list there is a result, \enquote{checked,
no notices}, not a gap) or a newer Unpaywall result ([[oa_status]],
[[oa_url]]) would be undone by a save from a process that loaded the
paper with the older one
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/206}).  A retraction
that [[scholar verify]] found could vanish that way.  So before
filling, a paper with a baseline takes the file's value of every field
this process still has as loaded ([[paper_merged_from]],
\cref{sec:paper-three-way}); [[paper_filled_from]], here, is the rule
without a baseline and the last step of the rule with one.

An equal id is not always one work: two papers without a DOI share the
hash id of their title and first author (\cref{sec:session-copies}).
The file's copy may then be a namesake that another process put under
our id, and filling our paper from it would give our work its
abstract and venue.  So nothing is filled unless the copies may be one
work ([[plausibly_same_work]], as everywhere a title match decides).

[[merge_with]] builds a new [[Paper]] and changes neither copy, so the
object the paper registry holds for this id, which other parts of this
process may share, is not changed behind their back.  The decision
gets the new object only when the file's copy added something: a save
that has nothing to fill leaves the decision holding the very object it
held, as before.  A paper new on disk is the reverse case: the file's
copy is all we have of the decision, but this process may already hold
the paper (the registry has it), and then that copy is ours and the
file's fills its gaps.  Registering it also keeps the rule that a
loaded paper is the one this process shares.  If the copy we hold is
another work, the file's decision keeps the file's paper: the decision
was taken on that work, and our namesake must not stand in for it.

