The window that remains, between reading the file and replacing it, is
milliseconds instead of hours, and [[save_session]] closes it by holding
[[file_lock]] across both.

\paragraph{The cost of a save.}
\label{sec:save-cost}
The lock is held for the whole merge and write, so the time a save
takes is time every other save of the session waits.  On an invented
session of five search links per decision, saved once after a load as
a command does, the save took the times of \cref{tab:save-cost} (on the
developer's machine, under the load of other jobs, so the figures are
rough: one run before, the median of three after), against the
94\,s at 30\,000 decisions that a reviewer measured before.  Two things
made the difference.
\begin{description}
\item[Pairing in one pass] The pairing read the whole baseline for
  every paper id (\cref{sec:pairing-cost}); with cProfile on a save of
  10\,000 decisions it took 24.9 of the save's 37.7\,s.  Profiled at
  4\,000 and 16\,000 decisions with the garbage collector off, no step
  of the save took more than 4.8 times as long at four times the
  size, so no step but that one grows faster than the session.
\item[No garbage collection during the save] A save reads a second
  copy of the session from the file, millions of objects, and every
  full collection meanwhile walks both copies.  So we hold the
  collector off for the save and turn it back on after, as it was (a
  process that had turned it off keeps it off); whatever cycles a save
  leaves wait for the next collection after it.  What it gains was
  measured four times, each on a machine busy with other jobs.  With
  the pairing in one pass, a save at 30\,000 decisions took 12.3\,s
  with the collector on throughout and 6.0\,s with it off throughout
  (one run each, load average about 5); with the collector held off
  for the save only, 7.7 to 8.4\,s in three runs.  The reviewer of the
  change measured 8.5 and 12.6\,s with the pause against 13.3 and
  18.8\,s without it at 30\,000 decisions, about a third less, and
  1.7 to 2.1\,s against 2.6\,s at 5\,000.  So the gain is real and
  grows with the session, but it is no fixed fraction.
\end{description}

