\paragraph{When the lock cannot be taken.}
\label{sec:session-lock}
A save that waited [[LOCK_TIMEOUT]] (ten seconds) for the lock used to
go ahead without it, which is what every save did before the lock
existed (\cref{sec:file-lock}).  For a session that is the lost update
the lock is there to prevent, and it happened: two processes each
saving a session of over 30\,000 decisions, when a save took a minute
and a half, waited past the ten seconds, and the one that then wrote
without the lock replaced the search records the other had saved
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/316}).
A save of a session therefore never writes the file without its lock
([[session_lock]]).
\begin{description}
\item[It waits longer] up to [[SESSION_LOCK_TIMEOUT]], five minutes.
  A holder that is working is done long before that (a save of
  30\,000 decisions takes seconds, \cref{sec:save-cost}, and a few may
  be queued), and a holder that is stuck (a process suspended with
  Ctrl-Z in mid-save) is reported within minutes instead of hanging
  every other command for good.  After the first [[LOCK_TIMEOUT]] it
  says which file it is waiting for, and then again about once a
  minute ([[SESSION_LOCK_REPEAT]]), since a silent wait of minutes
  looks like a hang; each line says that Ctrl-C stops the wait.
\item[It refuses rather than writes] If the lock still cannot be taken,
  nothing is written to the session file.  This process's session is
  kept beside it, in a file of its own, and [[SessionNotSaved]] names
  both files and how to merge the kept one in later.  The command
  fails: the CLI prints the error as one line and exits with status~1
  (\cref{sec:cli-main}).
\item[An interrupt keeps the session too] Ctrl-C during the wait is
  the user's way to stop waiting, and the wait is now minutes long and
  announced, so it must not cost the session: the lock is taken apart
  from the merge, and a [[KeyboardInterrupt]] during the wait keeps the
  session as a refusal does; the CLI then exits with 130, the status
  of an interrupted command, after the one line and without a
  traceback.  An interrupt after the lock is taken, in the merge or the
  write, is as it always was: the file is replaced atomically, so it is
  the old one or the new one.  A second Ctrl-C while the session is
  being kept is held back until the kept file is written (below).
  Only Ctrl-C (SIGINT) is an interrupt here.  Python does not handle
  SIGTERM, so a SIGTERM ends [[sessions decide]], the TUI and the other
  commands on the spot, without a kept file and with exit status 143,
  as before this change; [[scholar enrich]] alone turns SIGTERM into an
  interrupt while it runs (\cref{sec:enrich-chunks}), and so keeps its
  session.
\item[A lock file that cannot be opened is said so] A lock that cannot
  be opened or created (another user's lock file in a shared
  directory, a read-only directory) is no other process's doing, and
  no wait helps, so the error names the lock file and says to check
  its permissions.  The save still refuses, since writing without the
  lock is what this section removes, and keeps the session beside the
  file.  Where the directory itself is not writable, no file can be
  kept there either, so the error says that in the same line and no
  kept file is tried.
\end{description}
[[reconcile_session]], which loads, folds and saves under the lock, and
the copy before a run ([[keep_copy_before_run]]) take the lock the same
way, before they have changed anything: refused, they keep nothing,
and the error says to run the command again.  Their notices say that
nothing has been changed yet.

