Keeping the session takes seconds for a large one (the whole session
is serialised), and a user who pressed Ctrl-C once and sees nothing
happen presses it again.  That second interrupt used to stop the keep
half-way and leave an empty kept file, which [[sessions list]] then
advertised and [[sessions recover]] refused.  Two things prevent it.
The kept file is created only once its text is ready
([[keep_unsaved_session]], below), and it is removed if anything stops
the write, so an interrupted keep leaves no file.  And while the
session is kept, Ctrl-C is held back ([[InterruptHeldBack]]): the
handler of SIGINT only notes that it came, and the command ends as an
interrupted one (exit status 130) once the file is written.  We chose holding back over
giving up because the keep is the last chance for this process's
changes, and it ends in seconds; giving up would lose a review for the
sake of a few seconds.  The holding back works only in the main
thread, the only one Python delivers signals to; elsewhere, or if the
interrupt comes in the moment before it is installed, the keep stops
and the error says in its one line that the session could not be kept.

The class lives in the utilities (\cref{sec:interrupt-held-back}),
since an export's file moves hold signals back too; the keep holds only
SIGINT, its default.

