\paragraph{The baseline as JSON.}
The baseline is compared with [[==]]: tuples of decision fields,
frozen paper fields, the record keys as a set, the retirement marks by
record key.  JSON has no tuples, sets or enumerations, and a tuple read
back as a list would make every decision look changed, so the kept
file writes the [[SessionBaseline]] fields under their names through a
small codec ([[baseline_data]], [[baseline_from_data]]).  Each value
JSON lacks is written as an object with one key that names its type
(tuple, list, dict as a list of pairs, frozenset, or enum with the
enumeration's name and the value); the enumerations are the two a
decision's state holds, [[DecisionStatus]] and [[ReviewSource]], from a
fixed list; everything else must be a string, a number, a boolean or
null.  Reading checks every tag, the field names and the fields'
types, and anything else is an error ([[SessionFileError]]).
A first version pickled the baseline.  The review of this change
showed what that costs: a kept file whose baseline is a pickle runs the
pickle's code when it is recovered, before anything can be checked,
and session files are shared and committed, so a kept file may come
from someone else.  A pickle from another Scholar version also read
back without an error while holding that version's fields.  So nothing
on this path unpickles, and a kept file of the pickled shape is refused
as unreadable.

<<review session functions>>=
BASELINE_ENUMS = {"DecisionStatus": DecisionStatus,
                  "ReviewSource": ReviewSource}


def baseline_value_data(value):
    """`value` (a part of a `SessionBaseline`) as plain JSON data."""
    if isinstance(value, Enum):
        name = type(value).__name__
        if BASELINE_ENUMS.get(name) is not type(value):
            raise TypeError(f"no enumeration of a baseline: {name}")
        return {"enum": [name, value.value]}
    if value is None or isinstance(value, (bool, int, float, str)):
        return value
    if isinstance(value, tuple):
        return {"tuple": [baseline_value_data(v) for v in value]}
    if isinstance(value, list):
        return {"list": [baseline_value_data(v) for v in value]}
    if isinstance(value, frozenset):
        return {"frozenset": [baseline_value_data(v) for v in value]}
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return {"dict": [[baseline_value_data(k), baseline_value_data(v)]
                         for k, v in value.items()]}
    raise TypeError(f"no value of a baseline: {type(value).__name__}")


def baseline_value(data):
    """The value `baseline_value_data` wrote as `data`.

    Raises `ValueError` for anything it does not write.
    """
    if data is None or isinstance(data, (bool, int, float, str)):
        return data
    if not (isinstance(data, dict) and len(data) == 1):
        raise ValueError(f"not a baseline value: {data!r:.60}")
    [(tag, body)] = data.items()
    if not isinstance(body, list):
        raise ValueError(f"not a baseline value: {data!r:.60}")
    try:
        if tag in ("tuple", "list", "frozenset"):
            items = [baseline_value(v) for v in body]
            return {"tuple": tuple, "list": list,
                    "frozenset": frozenset}[tag](items)
        if tag == "dict" and all(
            isinstance(p, list) and len(p) == 2 for p in body
        ):
            return {baseline_value(k): baseline_value(v) for k, v in body}
        if (tag == "enum" and len(body) == 2 and body[0] in BASELINE_ENUMS
                and isinstance(body[1], str)):
            return BASELINE_ENUMS[body[0]](body[1])
    except TypeError as error:
        raise ValueError(f"not a baseline value: {error}") from error
    raise ValueError(f"not a baseline value: {data!r:.60}")
@

The fields are written under their names and read back only if the
names are exactly [[SessionBaseline]]'s and each field has the type the
merge reads it as; a baseline of [[None]] (a session constructed in
code) is written as null.

<<review session functions>>=
BASELINE_FIELD_TYPES = {
    "decisions": dict, "research_context": (str, type(None)),
    "record_keys": frozenset, "papers": dict, "decision_ids": dict,
    "retired": dict,
}


def baseline_data(base: "SessionBaseline | None") -> dict | None:
    """`base` as plain JSON data, for a kept file."""
    if base is None:
        return None
    return {name: baseline_value_data(getattr(base, name))
            for name in BASELINE_FIELD_TYPES}


def baseline_from_data(data) -> "SessionBaseline | None":
    """The baseline `baseline_data` wrote; `ValueError` if it is not one."""
    if data is None:
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, dict) or set(data) != set(BASELINE_FIELD_TYPES):
        raise ValueError("not a baseline: its fields differ")
    values = {name: baseline_value(data[name]) for name in data}
    for name, kind in BASELINE_FIELD_TYPES.items():
        if not isinstance(values[name], kind):
            raise ValueError(f"not a baseline: {name} has the wrong type")
    if not all(isinstance(k, str) and isinstance(v, tuple)
               for k, v in [*values["decisions"].items(),
                            *values["papers"].items()]):
        raise ValueError("not a baseline: a state is not a tuple")
    if not all(isinstance(k, str) and isinstance(v, str)
               for k, v in values["decision_ids"].items()):
        raise ValueError("not a baseline: a paper id is not a string")
    if not all(isinstance(k, tuple) for k in
               [*values["record_keys"], *values["retired"]]):
        raise ValueError("not a baseline: a record key is not a tuple")
    return SessionBaseline(**values)
