\paragraph{Taking what the other process changed.}
\label{sec:paper-three-way}
A paper that this process loaded from the file or saved to it has a
baseline: its fields as the file stored them then
([[SessionBaseline.papers]], above; a paper of a built baseline has
none).
With it, each field of a paper both hold is judged like a decision.
A field whose value here still equals the baseline was not touched by
this process; if the file's value differs, another process changed it
since, and the file's value is taken, whatever it is (a retraction
notice that replaced \enquote{checked, no notices}, an open-access
status that replaced \enquote{closed}).  A field whose value here
differs from the baseline was changed by this process (its own
Crossref or Unpaywall result, say) and keeps our value, also when the
other process changed it too: as with decisions, the process saving
now has the later word, and the other value is in the [[.bak]].
Gaps are then filled as before ([[paper_filled_from]]), so a field we
changed to nothing still takes the file's value, as without a
baseline.  The reverse is not a change: a field the file holds as
[[None]] is a gap there and is never taken.  Such a [[None]] comes
from a Scholar that predates the field or from a hand edit, which must
not turn our \enquote{checked, clean} into \enquote{never checked}.
The one exception we know of is a deliberate clearing: an Unpaywall
lookup that finds a once open paper closed now writes [[oa_url]] as
[[None]] ([[_normalize_oa]], \cref{unpaywall-module}).  We then take
the file's [[oa_status]] (\enquote{closed}) but keep our older
[[oa_url]], a stale link, which we accept rather than lose check
results to every older writer.

Six fields are not judged this way but accumulate.  [[sources]],
[[keywords]] and [[tags]] are united and the higher [[citation_count]]
kept, as [[merge_with]] always merges them: each provider that returns
or enriches a paper adds its name, its terms and its count, and every
other merge of two copies (deduplication, a re-run, the fill above)
unites them.  Judging them three ways would let the process that saved
last drop what the other had added, and a lower count from another
provider is no newer than a higher one.  The version links
([[preprint_versions]], [[published_versions]]: a published paper's
known preprints, and the published version an adopted preprint
stands for) accumulate the same way, but
[[merge_with]] keeps them whole from one side, so they are united
here ([[united_version_links]]): deduplication appends a link per
preprint it folds into a published entry, and adoption appends the
published DOI it replaced, so two processes can each add a link, and
neither link may be lost.

Taking the file's value of a field cannot change the paper's id.  Our
copy and the file's have the same id, so they have the same normalised
DOI, or neither has a DOI and they have the same normalised title and
first author's surname (\cref{sec:paper-registry}); any mix of the two
copies' [[doi]], [[title]] and [[authors]] keeps that id.  The
namesake guard comes first, as in [[paper_filled_from]]: the file's
copy of another work lends no field, changed or not.

