\paragraph{One answer, two fields.}
[[oa_status]] and [[oa_url]] are one Unpaywall answer:
[[annotate_paper]] writes both together, and for a paper that is
closed the link is [[None]] ([[_normalize_oa]],
\cref{unpaywall-module}).  Judged one field at a time, with
[[None]] read as a gap, an answer of \enquote{closed, no link} that
replaced \enquote{green, a repository link} came out as
\enquote{closed} beside the old link: the status was taken, the link
filled back in, by the other process's own save (it changed both, then
the gap fill refilled the link it had cleared from the file) and by
ours.  So the pair is decided as a whole, by its status, before the
single fields.  When our pair is as loaded and the file's differs with
a status, the file's pair is taken, its [[None]] link included.
Otherwise, when our pair holds a status or differs from the baseline,
it stands whole: it is restored after the gap fill, so the file's link
is never put beside our status.  A pair without a status on either
side is left to the gap fill, as before.  Without a baseline only that
last rule applies, so the pair stays whole there too.

Only this pair is a group.  [[pdf_url]] is not part of the answer:
providers and preprint adoption set it, and [[annotate_paper]] only
fills it when it is empty, so judging it alone is right.  The
Crossref pair [[updated_by]] and [[newer_versions]] is written
together too ([[annotate_papers]]), but a check never writes [[None]]
to either (\enquote{checked, nothing found} is an empty list,
\cref{crossref-module}), so the [[None]] rule never splits the pair.
It can be split only when each process changed one list, and then
each list is still what a Crossref check reported.

