Two links are one when they name the same DOI, compared as
[[Paper.id]] compares DOIs ([[Paper._normalize_doi]]): the writers
compare spellings as stored, and a link spelt differently by two
processes is still one version.  Ours comes first and each link stays
whole, so when both name a DOI with different PDF links ours is kept.
A link without a DOI (a preprint deduplication found only as a PDF) is
one with another only when they are equal.

<<review session functions>>=
def united_entries(
    ours: list[dict] | None, theirs: list[dict] | None, key
) -> list[dict] | None:
    """Return the entries of `ours`, then those of `theirs` it lacks.

    Two entries are one when `key` maps them to the same value. Returns
    `ours` itself when `theirs` adds nothing; two None stay None, and
    an empty list with None stays an empty list.
    """
    if not theirs:
        return ours if ours is not None else theirs
    entries = list(ours or [])
    seen = {key(entry) for entry in entries}
    for entry in theirs:
        if key(entry) not in seen:
            entries.append(entry)
            seen.add(key(entry))
    return entries if ours is None or len(entries) > len(ours) else ours


def united_version_links(
    ours: list[dict] | None, theirs: list[dict] | None
) -> list[dict] | None:
    """Return the links of `ours`, then those of `theirs` it lacks.

    Links are matched by normalised DOI, links without a DOI by
    equality. Returns `ours` itself when `theirs` adds nothing.
    """
    return united_entries(ours, theirs, version_link_key)


def version_link_key(link: dict):
    """Return what identifies a version link: its DOI, else the link."""
    doi = link.get("doi")
    if doi:
        return ("doi", Paper._normalize_doi(doi))
    return ("link", frozen_value(link))
@

\paragraph{Crossref notices both copies found.}
\label{sec:united-notices}
The gap fill keeps our value wherever both copies hold one, which
for the Crossref notices ([[updated_by]], [[newer_versions]]) can
lose a retraction.  An append into another file loads a session in
which the paper was checked clean (an empty [[updated_by]]), saves
into a target whose copy holds a retraction, and the gap fill kept our empty
list (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/219}); a paper
new in a same-file append and the new-session race
(\cref{sec:paper-baseline}) are in the same position.  Without a
paper state nothing tells which list is the newer, but the two errors
are not alike: keeping a notice that Crossref has since withdrawn
misleads until the next [[scholar verify]], which replaces the whole
list, while dropping a retraction lets the paper be cited as sound.
So without a paper state the two lists are united
([[united_crossref_notices]]).

With a paper state the three-way rule mostly decides.  A list only
the file changed is taken (a list we left as loaded), and a list only
we changed is ours: our own check replaced the loaded list, and the
file still holding that loaded list is no evidence for its notices,
which our check no longer saw.  When both changed, both processes
checked Crossref after the load, and neither knows which check came
later; the same asymmetry applies, so their lists are united too,
where other fields keep ours.

<<unite the Crossref notices both copies found>>=
for name in CROSSREF_NOTICE_FIELDS:
    mine, their = getattr(ours, name), getattr(theirs, name)
    if base and base[name] in (frozen_value(mine), frozen_value(their)):
        continue
    notices = united_crossref_notices(mine, their)
    if frozen_value(notices) != frozen_value(mine):
        changed_there[name] = notices
@

A notice is identified by its DOI, normalised as for version links,
and its kind: the [[type]] of an [[updated_by]] notice (a correction
and a retraction may name one notice DOI, \enquote{retraction} and
\enquote{correction} being different findings) and the [[relation]]
of a [[newer_versions]] entry, the only fields besides the DOI that
[[_normalize_updated_by]] and [[_normalize_newer_versions]] write as
identity (\cref{crossref-module}; the others are a label, a date and a
source).  [[_normalize_updated_by]] keeps a notice whose DOI Crossref
left out, as [[None]]; such a notice is one with another only when
they are equal.  Each notice stays whole, ours first, as the version
links do.

<<review session functions>>=
CROSSREF_NOTICE_FIELDS = ("updated_by", "newer_versions")


def united_crossref_notices(
    ours: list[dict] | None, theirs: list[dict] | None
) -> list[dict] | None:
    """Return the Crossref notices of `ours`, then those of `theirs` it
    lacks, matched by normalised DOI and kind (`crossref_notice_key`)."""
    return united_entries(ours, theirs, crossref_notice_key)


def crossref_notice_key(notice: dict):
    """Return what identifies a notice: its DOI and kind, else itself."""
    doi = notice.get("doi")
    if doi:
        kind = notice.get("type", notice.get("relation"))
        return ("doi", Paper._normalize_doi(doi), kind)
    return ("notice", frozen_value(notice))
@

Let's verify the race of
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/206} in real
processes: A loads a session whose paper was checked clean
(an empty [[updated_by]]), B finds a retraction and saves, A decides the
paper and saves.  The file keeps A's decision and B's retraction.  The
other cases run in one process, with B's save in its own registry as
above.  When both change the notices, the two lists are united
(\cref{sec:united-notices}; our change is an [[append]] to the loaded
list, which a baseline holding references would follow and miss, and
then take B's list alone), and a list only we changed is ours.  Our
own change of Unpaywall's [[oa_status]] wins over B's, and B's alone is
taken.

