\paragraph{An append loads only its own file.}
\label{sec:append-own-file}
Not carrying the path kept the other session's \emph{file} intact, but
not its decisions.  The append was still built from the session it had
loaded, and saved that session's decisions into its own file, which
then listed papers its searches never returned, kept or discarded by
another review, and nothing said so
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/213}).  Called without a
name for query [[foo]], [[create_review_session]] took over the named
session [[foo]], whatever its query.  The command line never makes that
call: [[search --review]] names the session after its query when [[-n]]
is not given, and [[search -n]] and [[rq]] always pass a name.  Its
form there is the other one: [[search bar -n foo]] without a
[[foo.json]] took over the unnamed session of the query [[foo]].

The cause is that [[load_session]] answers another question than the
append asks.  It finds the session a \emph{user} means by a name or a
query, as [[sessions show X]] does, so it tries [[X]] both ways.  An
append needs the session it continues, which is the one it will be saved
into: the file [[own_session_file_name]] gives for the new session's
name, or for its query when it has no name.  [[load_own_session]] loads
that file.  It finds every session Scholar wrote under that identity,
since Scholar has named the files this way since session files exist:
[[save_session]] wrote [[<name>.json]] or the query's hash from the
first version of this module on.  A file that only a hand rename made,
such as an unnamed session of query [[q]] moved to [[q.json]], is the
session named [[q]] to the append: [[-n q]] continues it.

One file of the other kind is still the append's.  Since
[[search foo --review]] names its session [[foo]], an unnamed session
of the query [[foo]] (saved by code that passed no name, or by hand)
has always been continued by that very command: [[foo.json]] did not
exist, so [[load_session("foo")]] fell back to the query's hash.  The
search and the session are the same, only the name is a default.  So
when a named append has no file of its own, its name is its query, and
the hash file of that query holds an unnamed session of the same query,
the append continues it.  Each condition keeps out a session that only
shares the file name: a [[foo.json]] that exists is the session
[[foo]]; a search [[bar -n foo]] is not a run of the query [[foo]]; a
hash file holding a named session, or another query, is a copy.

The other candidate is not ignored in silence.  A user whose append
until now took over the other file would otherwise see an empty review
and believe the decisions lost, so when the file the old lookup would
have tried exists and is not continued, one line names it, says what
it holds (the stored name, or the query of an unnamed session; we read
the file's two fields, since its file name alone does not say), and
how to continue it: [[-n]] with the file's stem, which loads that file
as its own, whatever it holds.  The level depends on what the append
does.  When its own file does not exist, the append starts a new
session where the old lookup would have continued the other one, so the
user most likely meant that one: a warning, which the command prints.
When its own file exists, the append continues its own session, as the
user asked, and the other file is only a pointer, logged at INFO
([[--verbose]] shows it), so that a session's every append does not
repeat it.

The hint looks for [[<query>.json]], and a query is not always a valid
file name: one longer than the file system allows makes [[exists]] raise
[[OSError]] (\enquote{File name too long}), which made [[load_session]]
fail the whole unnamed search.  Such a file cannot exist, so the error
reads as absent.

