\paragraph{Session arguments typed by the user.}
\label{sec:session-arguments}
Every command that works on a saved session ([[sessions show]],
[[decide]], [[export]], [[relabel]], [[llm classify]], [[enrich]], and
the others) takes the session as an argument the user typed, and that
argument used to go straight to [[load_session]], which tries
[[<arg>.json]] and then the file of the query's hash.  An argument can
fit two files: the named session [[foo]] lives in [[foo.json]], the
unnamed session of the query [[foo]] in the hash file, and
[[load_session("foo")]] opens [[foo.json]] whenever it exists.  The
unnamed session could not be addressed at all, and a list that named
it by its label (\enquote{foo}) sent the user to the other file
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/237}).  The appends,
listings and pooled samples were freed of the lookup earlier (issues
\#213, \#221 and \#231); the commands' arguments are what remains.

[[resolve_session_argument]] is the one rule for those arguments.
\begin{description}
\item[A file name.]  An argument ending in [[.json]] names a file in the
  sessions directory, so [[foo.json]] and [[2c26….json]] each pick
  their file.  A path is accepted only into the sessions directory: an
  argument must not reach a file outside it, nor be taken for a file
  inside it that it does not name (see below).
\item[A label.]  Otherwise the argument is a session's name or query,
  tried as before: [[<arg>.json]], then the query's hash file.
\item[Never a silent choice.]  Every file the argument can mean is
  looked at, and when more than one exists the argument is refused
  with [[AmbiguousSessionError]], whose message names each file, says
  what it holds (the stored name, or the query of an unnamed session)
  and how to pick it.  The commands turn it into exit status~2, the
  status of a usage error: the command was not wrong about the
  session, only unclear.
\end{description}
An argument that fits one file opens the same file as before, so every
session that has no namesake (on the user's machine, all of them in
November 2026) works exactly as it did.  A query that is too long to be
a file name makes [[exists]] raise [[OSError]]; such a file cannot
exist, so the error reads as absent, as in [[load_own_session]], and
the session as not found.

<<review session functions>>=
class SessionArgumentError(ValueError):
    """A session argument that names no session file unambiguously."""


class AmbiguousSessionError(SessionArgumentError):
    """A session argument fits more than one session file."""

    def __init__(self, argument: str, paths: list[Path]):
        self.argument = argument
        self.paths = paths
        described = " and ".join(
            f"{path.name} ({describe_session_file(path)})" for path in paths
        )
        choices = " or ".join(path.name for path in paths)
        super().__init__(
            f"Session {argument!r} is ambiguous: it fits {described}. "
            f"Give the file name to pick one: {choices}."
        )


def describe_session_file(path: Path) -> str:
    """Say what a session file holds: its stored name or unnamed query."""
    stored = _stored_identity(path)
    if stored is None:
        return "unreadable"
    name, query = stored
    if name:
        return f"session {name!r}"
    return f"unnamed session of query {query!r}"


def _session_file_exists(path: Path) -> bool:
    try:
        return path.exists()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False


def resolve_session_argument(argument: str) -> Path | None:
    """Return the session file a user-typed argument names, or None.

    `foo.json` names that file in the sessions directory; a path to a
    file there works too. Any argument is also tried as a session name
    (`<arg>.json`) and as a query (its hash file).

    Raises:
        SessionArgumentError: A `.json` path outside the sessions
            directory.
        AmbiguousSessionError: More than one of the files exists.
    """
    sessions_dir = _get_sessions_dir()
    candidates = []
    if argument.endswith(".json"):
        <<refuse a file outside the sessions directory>>
        candidates.append(sessions_dir / Path(argument).name)
    candidates.append(sessions_dir / f"{argument}.json")
    candidates.append(sessions_dir / f"{get_query_hash(argument)}.json")
    found = list(dict.fromkeys(
        path for path in candidates if _session_file_exists(path)
    ))
    if len(found) > 1:
        raise AmbiguousSessionError(argument, found)
    return found[0] if found else None
@

A file argument with a directory part names a file somewhere, and only
a file in the sessions directory is a session Scholar saves to.  Taking
the base name alone, as an earlier version did, opened the session of
that name in the sessions directory instead: [[sessions decide
/elsewhere/foo.json]] changed [[review_sessions/foo.json]] and left the
file the user named untouched, where [[load_session]] had said
\enquote{not found}.  So a path whose directory is not the sessions
directory is refused, with a message that says where the files are; a
path into the sessions directory (as [[sessions list -f json]] prints
them) is that file.  A label with a slash, such as the query
\enquote{TCP/IP}, does not end in [[.json]] and is looked up as before.

