\section{Selecting a screening tier}
\label{sec:screening-tier}

A large review cannot screen every record with the same care.  The rule
this section serves
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/302}) screens a first
tier and counts the rest: the first [[K]] records of each answer that a
source ranked by relevance, plus the records that several databases
returned.  The issue reports the measurement behind it, on the author's
own sessions: in relevance-ordered answers the records bearing on a
claim sat near the top, and a work that four providers returned bore on
it more often than one that a single provider returned.  Scholar does
not decide whether the rule is right for a review; it makes the tier
selectable and says exactly what was left out, so that a method can
state \enquote{screened [[N]] of [[M]] returned, by this rule}.

The tier is read from the links (\cref{sec:review-found-by}).  Each
link names a search record and, for links made since places were kept,
the paper's place in that record's answer ([[link_rank]]).  Three
conditions select a paper; a rule gives any of them, and a paper is in
the tier when it meets at least one:
\begin{description}
\item[A place] ([[top]]) The paper stood at place [[top]] or better in
  the answer of a record whose [[order]] is [[relevance]]
  (\cref{sec:search-result-order} in the [[scholar]] module).  A place
  in a newest-first or unordered answer says nothing about relevance,
  so it never selects: the first record of a date-ordered answer is
  merely the newest.  OpenAlex's search by meaning
  ([[openalex-semantic]]) answers with at most 50 works, ranked, so a
  place cut of 50 or more takes all of such an answer.  That is meant:
  the author's rule screens what a search by meaning returns, as a check
  on the keyword searches.
\item[Several databases] ([[min_providers]]) Records of at least that
  many different databases returned the paper.
\item[A provider] ([[providers]], optionally narrowed by
  [[query_like]]) A record of one of these providers returned the
  paper, of a query holding the text (any case).  The same rule screens
  everything that Scopus and Web of Science returned for a
  counter-query, and Scholar cannot know which query is one; this
  condition lets the screener say so.
\end{description}

The second condition counts databases, not provider names.  Several
providers search one database: OpenAlex itself, the preprint servers
pinned to their OpenAlex source and OpenAlex's search by meaning all
return OpenAlex's records, and a paper that the three returned is one
database's answer three times, not the agreement of three.  Scholar
already says which providers are one service where it counts requests:
they share a quota group ([[quota_group]], \cref{ch:ratelimit}), read
as the rate limiter reads it.  So a provider's database is its quota
group.  The session stores provider names, not objects, and the
commands that read a session ([[sessions show]], the CSV) must not
import the providers module: importing it registers every provider,
and each loads its search cache, which made [[sessions show]] slower
by a fifth of a second on a small session in the review of this
change, and more with larger caches.  So the names whose database is
not their own are listed here, in [[PROVIDER_DATABASES]], as
[[RANKED_PROVIDERS]] lists the ranked ones in the [[scholar]] module,
and a test keeps the list equal to the quota groups of the registered
providers.  A name not in the list (most providers, and an
unregistered or renamed one) is its own database, the only reading
that claims nothing.

Only current records count (\cref{sec:retired-searches}): a retired
search is one the review no longer stands by, so it should neither put
a paper in the tier nor be named in the counts.  A merged record from
before searches were recorded per provider
(\cref{sec:search-result-parts} in the [[scholar]] module) does not say
which of its providers returned a paper, so it counts for no database,
and its links carry no place.  An old link that names no filters counts
for its record when the session has exactly one record of its query and
provider, as [[search_record]] resolves it.

A paper may lack what a condition reads, and the counts say so, apart
for each cause because each calls for another remedy.  \emph{Place
unknown}: no link holds a place, as for every link made before places
were kept; running the search again records them.  \emph{Placed only
outside a relevance order}: its places are in answers ordered by date,
by citations or not at all.  Neither is selected by [[top]].
\emph{Databases unknown}: its links name only merged records or old
links that match several records, so no database can be counted for it;
such a paper is never selected by [[min_providers]], and without this
count an old session would select nothing by it silently.  Running its
searches again per provider makes the databases known.

<<review filtering functions>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class TierRule:
    """What puts a paper in the screening tier; any condition suffices.

    `top`: a place at most this in a relevance-ordered answer;
    `min_providers`: returned by records of at least this many
    databases (providers sharing a quota group are one); `providers`
    (with `query_like`, a part of the query, any case): returned by a
    record of one of these providers.
    """

    top: int | None = None
    min_providers: int | None = None
    providers: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    query_like: str | None = None

    def conditions(self) -> list[str]:
        """The names of the conditions this rule gives, in a fixed order."""
        given = {
            "top": self.top is not None,
            "min_providers": self.min_providers is not None,
            "provider": bool(self.providers),
        }
        return [name for name in TIER_CONDITIONS if given[name]]

    def data(self) -> dict:
        """The rule as JSON data, with what each condition means."""
        return {
            "top": self.top,
            "min_providers": self.min_providers,
            "providers": list(self.providers),
            "query_like": self.query_like,
            "meaning": dict(TIER_CONDITION_MEANINGS),
        }
