\section{Checking the quotes in machine reasons}
\label{sec:reason-quotes}

A machine decision can carry a reason: the classifier's [[llm_reason]]
(\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}) and an agent's [[agent_reason]]
(\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}).  Screeners are asked to quote the
record's own words in it, and a reviewer reads such a quote as evidence:
the abstract says this, so the verdict follows.  Scholar used to store a
reason as given, and nothing compared it with the record it was attached
to (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/256}).  A check of
an agent-screened session found a reason that quoted another record (the
rows of the screener's output and of its reasons file had slipped
against each other), quoted strings that were the screener's paraphrase
inside quote marks, and reasons that were placeholders or a shifted
column.  A quote that is not in the record is either a mix-up, and then
the verdict may belong to another paper too, or the screener's wording
passed off as the source's.  Either way the reader should be told.

This section holds the check itself, as pure functions of a reason and a
paper; \cref{sec:sessions-decide} in the CLI module runs it on a reasons
file before saving, [[llm classify]] at the end of its run, and
[[sessions show]] and the report (\cref{sec:report-provenance}) state
its counts.  Nothing is stored: the reason, the title and the abstract
are all in the session, so the check is computed whenever it is shown,
and a stored verdict could only go stale.

The check has limits, which every place that shows it inherits:
\begin{itemize}
\item It compares with the title and abstract the session holds
  \emph{now}.  An abstract filled in or replaced after the decision
  ([[scholar enrich]]) may no longer contain what the screener read.
\item The classifier saw only the first [[MAX_ABSTRACT_CHARS]] of an
  abstract (\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}); a quote from the rest is
  found here although the model cannot have read it.
\item The passages [[llm classify --full-text]] shows the model are not
  stored, so only that run can check a quote against them; afterwards
  such a quote is reported as not found.
\item A quote that is found shows that the words are the record's, not
  that the decision is right.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What counts as a quote}

A quote is the text between two \emph{double} quote marks: straight
ones ([["..."]]) and these typographic pairs, guillemets either way
round:
\enquote{\textquotedblleft\dots\textquotedblright},
\enquote{\textquotedblright\dots\textquotedblright},
\enquote{\quotedblbase\dots\textquotedblleft} (and
\enquote{\quotedblbase\dots\textquotedblright}),
\enquote{\guillemotleft\dots\guillemotright},
\enquote{\guillemotright\dots\guillemotleft}.  Single quote marks are
not quotes here: the apostrophe of \enquote{students' answers} is the
same character as a closing single quote, typographic or straight, so
pairing single marks would cut passages out of possessives and
contractions and report them as unmatched.  The pattern lists each
pair's closing mark explicitly and lets a quote span lines; it is
written with [[\u]] escapes because this file is woven into a
pdf\LaTeX{} document.  A quote mark without its partner opens nothing,
and neither does a straight mark right after a letter or digit: that is
an inch or seconds mark, as in [[5" disks]], and would otherwise pair
with the next real mark and take the text between for a quote.
Exactly one group of the pattern takes part in a match, so the last
group that matched ([[lastindex]]) is the quote.

<<review quote check>>=
QUOTE_PATTERN = re.compile(
    r'(?<![^\W_])"([^"]*)"'
    r"|\u201c([^\u201c\u201d]*)\u201d"
    r"|\u201d([^\u201d]*)\u201d"
    r"|\u201e([^\u201c\u201d\u201e]*)[\u201c\u201d]"
    r"|\u00ab([^\u00ab\u00bb]*)\u00bb"
    r"|\u00bb([^\u00ab\u00bb]*)\u00ab"
)


def quotes_in_reason(reason: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """Return the passages `reason` puts between double quote marks.

    Straight and typographic double marks count (curly pairs, low-high
    pairs, guillemets either way round); single marks do not, since an
    apostrophe is the same character.
    """
    if not reason:
        return []
    return [
        match.group(match.lastindex)
        for match in QUOTE_PATTERN.finditer(reason)
    ]
