Only a quote of at least [[MIN_QUOTE_WORDS]] words is checked.  A
screener who quotes one or two words may be marking a term---the
name of a method, a construct, a criterion of the review---rather than
lifting a passage, and a term need not be in the abstract for the
reason to be sound: \enquote{not about \enquote{pair programming}} is a
fair reason to discard a paper whose abstract never says it.  Such a
quote is counted as \emph{not checked}, never as matched, so a reason
that holds only short quotes adds to no count of verified quotes.  A
word is a run of letters or digits, joined by inner hyphens and
apostrophes, so \enquote{e-learning} is one word and an ellipsis none.

<<review quote check>>=
MIN_QUOTE_WORDS = 3
QUOTE_WORD = re.compile(r"[^\W_]+(?:['-][^\W_]+)*")


def is_checked_quote(quote: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `quote` is long enough to be checked (`MIN_QUOTE_WORDS`)."""
    return _is_long_enough(normalized_for_quotes(quote))


def _is_long_enough(folded: str) -> bool:
    return len(QUOTE_WORD.findall(folded)) >= MIN_QUOTE_WORDS
@

\subsection{Comparing a quote with the record}

A quote is compared with the record's title and abstract after both
sides are folded the same way, so that differences a reader would not
call a misquotation do not count:
\begin{description}
\item[Markup] [[clean_metadata_text]] (\cref{sec:clean-provider-text}
  in the utilities) decodes entities and drops inline tags, which a
  session recorded before ingest cleaning may still hold; the screener
  read the rendered text.  It names the tags it drops, so tag-like
  markup of other systems stays, such as the [[<jats:italic>]] and
  [[</jats:p>]] of JATS abstracts: the check drops any [[<]] that is
  followed by a name of letters, digits, [[:]] and [[-]] (starting with
  a letter), optional [[name="value"]] attributes and [[>]]
  ([[QUOTE_TAG]]).  A comparison in running text, [[x < y]] or
  [[a <c and d> e]], has no such shape and stays.
\item[Compatibility forms] NFKC folds ligatures (\enquote{fi} as one
  character), full-width letters and the ellipsis character, which
  becomes three full stops.
\item[Case and white space] are folded and collapsed, since a copied
  abstract brings its line breaks and a screener may change the case of
  a quote's first word.
\item[Soft hyphens] are dropped: they are invisible in the text the
  screener copied.
\item[Hyphens and dashes] are unified, so a hyphen typed for the
  abstract's en dash is the same character on both sides.
\item[Quote marks] of every kind, single ones and apostrophes included,
  are removed from both sides: a reason that writes the abstract's inner
  double quotes as single ones, inside its own double quotes, or a
  straight apostrophe for a curly one, still matches.  Removing a mark
  on both sides never makes words with different letters equal.
\end{description}
Accents are not folded, so a quote that writes \enquote{naive} for the
abstract's \enquote{na\"{\i}ve} is reported, and neither is \LaTeX{}
mathematics, which an abstract may hold as source and a screener may
have read rendered.  Both are false alarms a reader can dismiss at a
glance; folding them would make different words equal.
A word broken at a line end, \enquote{exam- ple}, is not joined again:
the same pattern is a real compound in \enquote{pre- and post-test},
which joining would corrupt, so such a quote is reported.

Folding is most of the cost of the check, and it is paid for every
abstract of a session each time the session is shown: 30\,000 reasons
in a screened session.  Four shortcuts keep it cheap without changing
the result.  The tag pattern runs only on a text that holds a [[<]].
NFKC and the unified characters touch only characters outside ASCII,
so an ASCII text skips both; its own quote marks are removed apart.  The characters to unify
are looked up one by one with [[in]] and replaced only when present,
since an abstract holds few of them: [[str.translate]] with a mapping
was about fifty times slower on a 1500-character abstract that held a
dash and a pair of quote marks, and a regular expression about four
times.  And splitting and joining at white space gives the stripped text back
unchanged when the text holds no white space but single spaces, which
a few [[in]] tests can tell; [[QUOTE_OTHER_SPACES]] lists the white
space characters that NFKC leaves as they are.  On a short text, a
quote or a title, these tests are not worth it (profiling showed them
as the largest single cost while quotes took them too), so a text
shorter than [[QUOTE_SHORT_TEXT]] characters is split and joined
directly.

<<review quote check>>=
QUOTE_TEXT_FOLDS = {
    "\u00ad": "",
    **dict.fromkeys("\u2010\u2011\u2012\u2013\u2014\u2015\u2212", "-"),
    **dict.fromkeys(
        "\u201c\u201d\u201e\u201f\u00ab\u00bb\u2018\u2019\u201a\u201b"
        "\u2039\u203a", ""
    ),
}
QUOTE_ASCII_MARKS = "\"'`"
QUOTE_TAG = re.compile(
    r'</?[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9:-]*(?:\s+[A-Za-z0-9:-]+="[^"<>]*")*\s*/?>'
)
QUOTE_OTHER_SPACES = tuple(
    character for character in map(chr, range(0x3001))
    if character.isspace() and character != " "
    and unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", character) != " "
)
QUOTE_SHORT_TEXT = 300


def normalized_for_quotes(text: str | None) -> str:
    """Fold `text` for the quote check.

    Markup cleaned (tag-like markup too), NFKC, soft hyphens and quote
    marks dropped, dashes unified, case-folded and white space
    collapsed. Accents are kept.
    """
    text = clean_metadata_text(text or "")
    if "<" in text:
        text = QUOTE_TAG.sub("", text)
    if not text.isascii():
        text = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)
        for character, replacement in QUOTE_TEXT_FOLDS.items():
            if character in text:
                text = text.replace(character, replacement)
    for mark in QUOTE_ASCII_MARKS:
        if mark in text:
            text = text.replace(mark, "")
    text = text.casefold()
    if (len(text) < QUOTE_SHORT_TEXT or "  " in text
            or any(space in text for space in QUOTE_OTHER_SPACES)):
        return " ".join(text.split())
    return text.strip()
@

The shortcuts must give what the plain definition gives: tags
dropped, NFKC, every unified character replaced and every quote mark
removed, case-folded, split and joined.  Let's
compare the two on texts that take each path, short and long, and
check that [[QUOTE_OTHER_SPACES]] holds every white space character NFKC keeps,
also beyond the range it is built from.

<<reason quote test methods>>=
def test_the_folding_shortcuts_change_nothing(self):
    import sys
    import unicodedata
    from scholar.utils import clean_metadata_text

    def plain(text):
        text = QUOTE_TAG.sub("", clean_metadata_text(text))
        text = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)
        for character, replacement in QUOTE_TEXT_FOLDS.items():
            text = text.replace(character, replacement)
        for mark in QUOTE_ASCII_MARKS:
            text = text.replace(mark, "")
        return " ".join(text.casefold().split())

    texts = [
        "Plain ASCII text.", " Leading and trailing ", "two  spaces",
        "a\ttab", "a\nline", "no\u00a0break", "line\u2028separator",
        "ogham\u1680space", "Stra\u00dfe \u201cquoted\u201d \u2013 dash",
        "soft\u00adhyphen", "\ufb01ne \uff26ull-width", "&amp; <i>x</i>",
        "<jats:p>it's \"x\"</jats:p>", "a <c and d> e",
    ]
    long_tail = " filler" * QUOTE_SHORT_TEXT
    for text in texts + [text + long_tail for text in texts]:
        assert normalized_for_quotes(text) == plain(text), text[:40]
    others = {
        chr(i) for i in range(sys.maxunicode + 1)
        if chr(i).isspace() and unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", chr(i)) != " "
    } - {" "}
    assert set(QUOTE_OTHER_SPACES) == others
@

A screener shortens a long passage with an ellipsis: \enquote{\dots},
three full stops (spaced or not), or either in brackets or
parentheses.  The quote is
split there into parts that must each occur, in that order, in the same
text (title or abstract; a quote does not run from one into the other).
Punctuation at the edges of a part is ignored, chiefly the full stop or
comma a screener leaves inside the closing mark.  Only punctuation and
brackets are dropped, never a letter or digit, so every word of the
quote is still checked.

A part must begin and end at a word boundary of the text, so
\enquote{students learn} is not found in \enquote{students learned}.
Nothing else is lenient: there is no similarity threshold, because a
threshold would accept exactly the paraphrases the check is there to
find.  A quote is matched or it is reported.

<<review quote check>>=
QUOTE_ELLIPSIS = re.compile(
    r"[\[(]\s*\.(?:\s*\.){2,}\s*[\])]|\.(?:\s*\.){2,}"
)
QUOTE_EDGE_CHARACTERS = " .,;:()[]"


def quote_parts(quote: str) -> list[str]:
    """The folded parts of `quote` that must occur in order."""
    return _parts_of(normalized_for_quotes(quote))


def _parts_of(folded: str) -> list[str]:
    stripped = (
        part.strip(QUOTE_EDGE_CHARACTERS)
        for part in QUOTE_ELLIPSIS.split(folded)
    )
    return [part for part in stripped if part]


def _find_at_word_bounds(text: str, part: str, start: int) -> int:
    """Index of `part` in `text` from `start`, at word bounds, or -1."""
    while (found := text.find(part, start)) >= 0:
        end = found + len(part)
        cut_before = (found > 0 and text[found - 1].isalnum()
                      and part[0].isalnum())
        cut_after = (end < len(text) and text[end].isalnum()
                     and part[-1].isalnum())
        if not cut_before and not cut_after:
            return found
        start = found + 1
    return -1


def quote_occurs(parts: list[str], texts: Sequence[str]) -> bool:
    """Whether some text in `texts` holds `parts`, in order."""
    for text in texts:
        position = 0
        for part in parts:
            found = _find_at_word_bounds(text, part, position)
            if found < 0:
                break
            position = found + len(part)
        else:
            return True
    return False


def unmatched_quotes(reason: str | None, paper: Paper,
                     extra_texts: Sequence[str] = ()) -> list[str]:
    """The checked quotes of `reason` not found in `paper`'s record.

    Compared with the title, the abstract and `extra_texts` (such as
    the full-text passages a classifier was shown). Quotes shorter than
    `MIN_QUOTE_WORDS` words are not checked and never returned.
    """
    return _unmatched_among(checked_quotes(reason), paper, extra_texts)
@

Each quote is folded once, and the record's texts only when the reason
holds a quote to check, since folding is the cost of the check and most
of it is the abstract.  [[checked_quotes]] pairs each checked quote, as
written, with its folded parts; the quote as written is what a reader
is shown.

<<review quote check>>=
def checked_quotes(reason: str | None) -> list[tuple[str, list[str]]]:
    """The quotes of `reason` long enough to check, with folded parts."""
    checked = []
    for quote in quotes_in_reason(reason):
        folded = normalized_for_quotes(quote)
        if _is_long_enough(folded):
            checked.append((quote, _parts_of(folded)))
    return checked


def _unmatched_among(checked, paper, extra_texts=()) -> list[str]:
    if not checked:
        return []
    texts = [
        normalized_for_quotes(text)
        for text in (paper.title, paper.abstract, *extra_texts) if text
    ]
    return [quote for quote, parts in checked
            if not quote_occurs(parts, texts)]
@

