\section{Checking a batch of reasons as a whole}
\label{sec:batch-check}

The checks of \cref{sec:reason-quotes} and the line checks of a reasons
file (\cref{sec:decide-quote-check} in the CLI module) look at one row
at a time, and a row can pass them all and still not be a screener's
verdict.  Agent screening through [[sessions decide --reasons-file]]
and [[sessions screen --verdicts-file]] showed how
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/291}): screeners given
several hundred records each read some of them and filled the rest
with one verdict, one shared reason and one confidence.  Every such
line is well formed: an id that exists, a reason with letters, a
confidence in range, no placeholder, often no quote to check.  What
gives the rows away is what they share: the same reason on many
records with different titles, and the same confidence on every row of
a batch.  Scholar used to store every row as a reasoned verdict, and
nothing in the session tells such a row apart afterwards.

This section holds the check as one pure function over a set of rows:
a reasons file, a verdicts file, one [[--reason]] given to many
records, or the reasons a session already stores.  It returns
findings, each with its kind, its count and the records concerned;
printing them, and refusing a batch under [[--strict]], is the
commands' business (\cref{sec:decide-batch-check} in the CLI module),
and so is the read-only report over a stored session
([[sessions check-batches]], \cref{sec:stored-batches} below).

The check is a heuristic about what defaulted rows look like, and it
can err both ways.  A check that warns on honest batches will be
switched off, and one that only fires on a batch that is all one row
is useless.  Its thresholds are therefore scholar's own choice, each
stated with what it rests on below: the case reported in the issue (in
one pass, three of four batches of about 365 rows had been filled so,
and batches of 100 had not), what an honest batch looks like, and one
measurement of stored sessions (\cref{sec:batch-calibration}).  No
figure is known about how often screeners default rows, and none is
assumed.  A finding is a pattern for a reader to judge, not a verdict
on the screener: seven identical correction notices on seven records
of one kind are honest, the same \enquote{no abstract; the title shows
an unrelated subject} on nineteen different records is not, and only a
reader can tell the two apart.  So the output says what is shared, by
how many records, and which, and leaves the judgement to the reader.

\subsection{Rows and findings}

A row is what the check needs of one line of a batch.  It names its
record as the batch named it (a line's id, or the paper id), carries
the record's title, the reason and, from a verdicts file, the verdict
and its tags.  The confidence is the row's \emph{own}: the third column
of a reasons file, the fifth of a verdicts file.  A [[-c]] given for
the whole batch applies to every row by construction, like one
[[--reason]], so it says nothing about how the rows were written and
is not put into the rows.  A row also carries the batch id it came
with, when the file or the command named one
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/292}), so that the
rows can be grouped into the screener's batches; the check itself does
not read it.

<<review batch check>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class BatchRow:
    """One row of a screening batch, as `batch_findings` reads it.

    `paper` names the record as the batch named it; `title` is the
    record's title (copies of one work share it); `confidence` is the
    row's own, not one given for the whole batch; `status` and `tags`
    are a verdicts file's verdict (None and empty for a reasons file);
    `batch` is the batch id the row came with, or None.
    """

    paper: str
    title: str | None
    reason: str | None
    confidence: float | None = None
    status: str | None = None
    tags: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    batch: str | None = None


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class BatchFinding:
    """What the batch check found: kind, rows concerned, their records.

    `count` is the number of records concerned (for a shared reason,
    the records with different titles), `rows` the number of rows
    checked, `papers` the records concerned in batch order (each once),
    `detail` one sentence for a reader. An `information` finding is
    reported but never refuses a batch. `reason` is the folded reason
    of a `shared-reason` finding (`batch_reason_key`), else None.
    """

    kind: str
    count: int
    rows: int
    papers: tuple[str, ...]
    detail: str
    information: bool = False
    reason: str | None = None
@

Two rows share a reason when their texts are equal once white space is
collapsed and case is folded: a screener that pastes one reason does
not vary its case, but a line break or a doubled space from a script's
template should not hide the copy.  Punctuation is kept, since
\enquote{Off topic} and \enquote{Off topic.} are already a near-miss a
reader would not call one reason, and a looser comparison would start
to call short honest reasons copies of each other.  A title is reduced
to its case-folded letters and digits, as the audit table reduces it
([[audit_work_key]]), so the copies of one work that two providers
spelt with other punctuation still count as one; a row without a title
counts as a work of its own.

The two foldings differ on purpose, and they set a known limit.  A
title is folded for case, punctuation and a trailing full stop, but not
further: two editions of a book, or a paper and its erratum
(\enquote{Correction to: \dots}), have different titles and count as
different works, which they are, though a screener may honestly give
them one reason.  A reason is folded for white space and case only, so
\enquote{Off topic} and \enquote{Off topic.} are two reasons, and so
are reasons into which a screener's template wrote the record's own id
or title.  Such templated reasons defeat the check: every row's reason
is then its own.  The check does not fold further, by stripping
punctuation, ids or numbers, because honest reasons then start to
collide: \enquote{Duplicate of doi:10.1/a} and \enquote{Duplicate of
doi:10.1/b}, each naming its own original, would become one reason once
ids are stripped, and a check that counts different reasons as one
cries wolf; a defaulted batch that
varies its reason this way is left to the quote check and to a reader.

<<review batch check>>=
def batch_reason_key(reason: str | None) -> str | None:
    """A reason with white space collapsed and case folded; None if empty."""
    if not reason or not reason.strip():
        return None
    return " ".join(reason.split()).casefold()


def batch_title_key(row: BatchRow) -> str:
    """The work a row is about: its folded title, else its own id."""
    folded = unicodedata.normalize(
        "NFKD", clean_metadata_text(row.title or "")
    ).casefold()
    letters = "".join(c for c in folded if c.isalnum())
    return f"title:{letters}" if letters else f"id:{row.paper}"
@

\subsection{The findings}

\paragraph{A reason shared by records with different titles.}
A reason is reported when it is the reason of at least
[[SHARED_REASON_MIN_RECORDS]] (ten) records with different titles,
however large the set of rows; each such reason is a finding of its
own, with the number of records, the reason cut to a line and the
first records.  Copies of one work, which a session can hold under two
ids (\cref{sec:session-copies}), may honestly share a reason, and two
rows naming one record do so by definition, so the records are counted
by title.  The count is per reason, not a share of the batch: a stored
session holds many screeners' batches merged into a few runs
(\cref{sec:stored-batches}), and there 275 rows on one reason were 9\,\%
of a run of 3\,007, while in a single screener's file of 365 rows the
same pattern is most of it.  A share would see the one and miss the
other; a count sees both.  The number ten rests on two things.  It
keeps a small batch out, as the issue asks, and leaves room for two or
three records excluded for one stated reason (\enquote{No empirical
evaluation.}).  And it lies between what honest and defaulted sessions
showed in the measurement of \cref{sec:batch-calibration}: there honest
reasons were shared by at most seven records, and those seven were a
class of near-identical records (correction notices of one kind),
while the sessions with defaulted rows had reasons shared by thirteen
records and more.

\paragraph{One confidence on every row.}
The finding is made when every row with its own confidence has the
same one, and there are at least [[FLAT_CONFIDENCE_MIN_ROWS]] (twenty)
of them.  A screener that gives round confidences, 0.8 and 0.9, uses
more than one value over a batch unless every record looks equally
clear to it, and below twenty rows a batch of equally clear records
(ten obvious off-topic hits, each given 0.9) is too likely to be
honest to warn about.  The reported batches were about 365 rows.  The
check asks for \emph{every} row, not most of them: a screener that
read forty records with confidences of their own and gave the other
three hundred one value does not make this finding, though its shared
reason does.

A validator outside Scholar, which checks one screener's batch of about
a hundred rows before it reaches Scholar, ignores flat confidences of
0.9 and above, on the ground that a batch of confidently off-topic
records sits at 0.93 to 0.95.  Scholar does not make that exception.
That ground describes confidences \emph{in a range}, and a range is
not flat: this rule asks for one value on every row, which a batch
spread over 0.93 to 0.95 already escapes.  What the exception would
let through is a batch with one high value on every row of twenty or
more, which is what a default looks like when the default is
\enquote{off topic}; the finding is a warning a reader can dismiss,
and refuses only under [[--strict]].

The confidence is judged only where the rows are one screener's batch:
a file given to [[sessions decide]] or [[sessions screen]].  A stored
run is not one (\cref{sec:stored-batches}), so the stored report does
not judge it.

\paragraph{No run check.}
An earlier version also looked for runs of ten or more consecutive
identical rows, to catch a defaulted block too small a share of a long
batch for the shared-reason rule.  With the count per reason that
cannot happen: ten consecutive rows about different works with one
reason are a reason shared by ten records, so the run was always
reported twice.  It is gone.

<<review batch check>>=
SHARED_REASON_MIN_RECORDS = 10
FLAT_CONFIDENCE_MIN_ROWS = 20
@

\paragraph{One reason for many records.}
A single [[--reason]] given to many records shares its reason by
construction: it is how a rule is applied on purpose (\enquote{No
abstract; excluded under criterion 3}).  So such a batch gets one
finding of its own, [[one-reason]], as information, which never
refuses a batch, and neither of the two above.

<<review batch check>>=
def batch_findings(
    rows: Sequence[BatchRow], *, one_reason: bool = False,
    confidence: bool = True,
) -> list[BatchFinding]:
    """Check a set of screening rows as a whole.

    Returns a `shared-reason` finding per reason given to at least
    `SHARED_REASON_MIN_RECORDS` records with different titles, most
    records first, then a `flat-confidence` finding (see
    `FLAT_CONFIDENCE_MIN_ROWS`) unless `confidence` is false; or, when
    `one_reason` says that one reason was given to every row on
    purpose, one `one-reason` finding as information.
    """
    if one_reason:
        return _one_reason_findings(rows)
    findings = shared_reason_findings(rows)
    if confidence:
        flat = _flat_confidence_finding(rows)
        if flat is not None:
            findings.append(flat)
    return findings


def _papers_of(rows) -> tuple[str, ...]:
    return tuple(dict.fromkeys(row.paper for row in rows))
@

The rows are grouped by reason, and each group about enough different
titles is a finding; the largest come first, since they are what a
reader wants to see first, and groups of equal size keep the order of
their first row.

<<review batch check>>=
BATCH_REASON_SHOWN_CHARS = 60


def _shown_reason(reason: str) -> str:
    reason = " ".join(reason.split())
    if len(reason) > BATCH_REASON_SHOWN_CHARS:
        reason = reason[:BATCH_REASON_SHOWN_CHARS - 1].rstrip() + "…"
    return f'"{reason}"'


def shared_reason_findings(rows: Sequence[BatchRow]) -> list[BatchFinding]:
    """One finding per reason shared by enough records with other titles."""
    groups: dict[str, list[BatchRow]] = {}
    for row in rows:
        key = batch_reason_key(row.reason)
        if key is not None:
            groups.setdefault(key, []).append(row)
    with_reason = sum(len(group) for group in groups.values())
    findings = []
    for key, group in groups.items():
        records = len({batch_title_key(row) for row in group})
        if records < SHARED_REASON_MIN_RECORDS:
            continue
        findings.append(BatchFinding(
            "shared-reason", records, with_reason, _papers_of(group),
            f"shared by {records} records with different titles "
            f"({len(group)} rows): {_shown_reason(group[0].reason)}",
            reason=key,
        ))
    return sorted(findings, key=lambda finding: -finding.count)


def _flat_confidence_finding(rows) -> BatchFinding | None:
    rated = [row for row in rows if row.confidence is not None]
    if (len(rated) < FLAT_CONFIDENCE_MIN_ROWS
            or len({row.confidence for row in rated}) != 1):
        return None
    return BatchFinding(
        "flat-confidence", len(rated), len(rows), _papers_of(rated),
        f"all {len(rated)} rows with a confidence have the same one, "
        f"{rated[0].confidence:g}",
    )


def _one_reason_findings(rows) -> list[BatchFinding]:
    papers = _papers_of(row for row in rows if row.reason)
    if len(papers) < 2:
        return []
    reason = next(row.reason for row in rows if row.reason)
    return [BatchFinding(
        "one-reason", len(papers), len(rows), papers,
        f"one reason for {len(papers)} records, a rule applied on "
        f"purpose: {_shown_reason(reason)}",
        information=True,
    )]
@

