\subsection{Testing the batch check}

The tests feed the function invented batches.  The reported shape
comes first: 365 rows, the first forty read, each with a reason and a
confidence of its own, and the other 325 one verdict, one reason and
one confidence.  It makes the shared-reason finding, and not the flat
one, since forty rows have confidences of their own.  A batch whose
reasons differ but whose every row says 0.9 makes the flat finding
alone.  Then the two shapes of the measurement: a run of 3\,000 rows in
which 275 share one reason, scattered among the others, as in session
D, is reported, and one in which seven share it, as in G, is not.

The honest batches are written as carefully, since a check that cries
wolf on them would be switched off: a handful of rows that agree in
everything; a large batch whose screener gives round confidences;
copies of one work sharing a reason, next to each other; three records
excluded for one stated reason in a batch of forty; a large batch of
discards, grouped by verdict as the help tells screeners to group them,
each with its own reason; a rule applied with one [[--reason]]; and the
thresholds themselves, one below and at each.

