\subsection{Reasons already stored}
\label{sec:stored-batches}

Reasons written before the check existed are in the sessions already,
and the check is how they can be examined.  A screener's batch is not
stored as such, and neither is anything that stands for it.  What is
stored is the \emph{run}: the decisions of one [[sessions decide]] run
share their agent's name and time ([[agent_by]], [[agent_decided_at]],
\cref{sec:agent-audit-fields}), and the screenings of one [[sessions
screen]] or [[llm classify --second-screen]] run share their
screener's kind, name and [[recorded_at]] (\cref{sec:screenings}).
But a run is neither a screener's batch nor a superset of one: a
screener's batch is split over several runs, one per verdict and tag
set, since status and tags apply to a whole [[decide]] run, and a
writer that loads several screeners' batches merges them into one run
(\cref{sec:batch-calibration}).

So [[stored_batch_check]] looks for shared reasons across the whole
session first, once over the agent reasons and once over the
screenings' reasons (a screener's reason and an agent's are different
screeners' words).  Per run it reports only what a run can show: that
every row of it carries one reason, which is what one [[--reason]]
leaves (or a file with one reason on every line, which a run cannot
tell apart), as information.  Such a run counts as \emph{one}
record in the session-wide count, under a key of its own, so that a
rule applied with [[--reason]] to fifty records is not reported as a
reason shared by fifty, while the same reason given again in other
runs still adds up: twelve such runs are twelve records, and a run of
300 plus nine rows elsewhere are ten.  Only a run of two or more
different works counts so; a run of one row is an ordinary row, since
an agent that records one paper per call ([[decide --paper-id X
--reason R]] for each record) leaves one run per record, and leaving
those out would hide exactly the loop the incoming check, which sees
one row per call, cannot catch.  The worst case stays a note: a
screener's file with one reason on every line, loaded as one run,
reads as a rule applied once.  When that file is given to [[sessions
decide]] or [[sessions screen]] it is checked as a file, and there it
is a reason shared by all its records, which [[--strict]] refuses;
the stored report says so beside its note.
One confidence on every row is not judged from runs: a run's rows may
be several screeners' or part of one's.

\paragraph{Stored batches.}
Where the rows carry a batch id ([[agent_batch]], a screening's
[[batch]]; \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/292}), the
batch is stored after all, and [[stored_batches]] checks each one as
the incoming check checks a file: shared reasons and one confidence on
every row, with the thresholds of \cref{sec:batch-check}.  This is the
one place where a stored confidence can be judged.  A batch is one
screener's piece of work, so its rows are grouped by the screener as
well as by the id: the agent's name for decisions, kind and name for
screenings, agent reasons and screenings apart as above.  The same
sheet screened by two screeners is two batches, and two screeners'
verdicts on one record are no shared reason.  A batch may span runs
(split by verdict) and a run may hold several batches (one file of
concatenated batches), which is why the id, not the run, groups them.

Two things differ from the run notes above.  A run whose rows all carry
one reason is not pooled into one record inside a batch: a screener's
file with one reason on every line is exactly what the batch check is
for, and in a batch it cannot be told from a rule applied with
[[--reason --batch]] either, so it is reported (a batch that is all
one reason as such, below), and the run note says which runs were one
reason, for a reader to judge.  And a confidence
given with [[-c]] to a whole [[decide]] run is stored on every row
just as a confidence written on every line is, so a batch recorded in
one run with [[-c]] and no confidences of its own shows one confidence
on every row; the report says so beside its findings.  Rows without a
batch id are checked only as before.

Only decisions that still carry an agent reason count: a later machine
decision clears it, and a reset keeps it in its history, so the
remaining rows are the ones whose reasons stand.  The snapshots of a
decision's verdict ([[origin]] [[decision]]) are not a screener's and
are left out.

