A batch whose records all carry one reason is what a rule given with
[[--reason --batch]] leaves, and also what a screener's file with the
same reason on every line leaves; storage cannot tell them apart.  It
is still reported, but worded as what it is, as the session-wide
check words a run of one reason: one [[one-reason]] note naming the
batch, from as many records with different titles as an ordinary
shared reason needs ([[SHARED_REASON_MIN_RECORDS]]), and no
flat-confidence finding, since a [[-c]] given to the rule puts one
confidence on every record by construction.  A smaller batch of one
reason gets nothing, as it would get no shared-reason finding either.

<<review batch check>>=
def stored_batch_findings(batch: str, rows) -> list[BatchFinding]:
    """The findings of one stored batch: one reason for all, or as a file."""
    reasons = {batch_reason_key(row.reason) for row in rows}
    works = {batch_title_key(row) for row in rows}
    if len(reasons) == 1 and None not in reasons:
        if len(works) < SHARED_REASON_MIN_RECORDS:
            return []
        return [
            replace(finding, detail=(
                f"one reason for all {finding.count} records of batch "
                f"{batch}: a rule given with --reason, or the same reason "
                f"on every line: {_shown_reason(rows[0].reason)}"
            ))
            for finding in batch_findings(rows, one_reason=True)
        ]
    return batch_findings(rows)
