\paragraph{Titles written in \LaTeX}
\label{sec:latex-titles}

Some providers hand over a title, an author or a venue written in
\LaTeX: [[Caf\'e]], [[Erd\H{o}s]], [[The \textit{wolf}]],
[[$\alpha$-stable]].
Until \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/304} the [[.bib]]
let such markup through, which printed the first three right and
stopped on the fourth (its [[$]] was escaped, so [[\alpha]] stood
outside math), and let every other backslash through too, so a title
such as [[x\y]] stopped pdf\LaTeX.
Escaping every backslash would print \enquote{Caf\textbackslash'e} and
\enquote{The \textbackslash textitwolf} (compiled, \TeX{} Live 2025),
so output that was right would become wrong.
Instead a value written in the \LaTeX{} we understand is turned into
the Unicode text it stands for, and that text is escaped like any
other; any other value is escaped literally.

The reading is [[pylatexenc]]'s, the library [[bibtexparser]] 2 uses
for its own \LaTeX{} decoding (its [[LatexDecodingMiddleware]], which
the [[--cited-from]] reader uses); [[bibtexparser]] 2.1.0 declares it
as its one dependency, and since we now import it ourselves it is
declared in our [[pyproject.toml]] as well.
Its documentation (version 2.10,
\url{https://pylatexenc.readthedocs.io/en/v2.10/latex2text/}, read
2026-11-23; 2.11 is installed) says that math is \enquote{incorporated
as normal text} by default, that comments are discarded unless
[[keep_comments]] is set, that \enquote{The usual LaTeX quote symbols}
become Unicode quotes; it says nothing about unknown macros or about
[[~]] and [[--]], so those were tried: an unknown macro is no macro its
context database holds ([[get_macro_spec]] returns [[None]]), [[~]]
becomes a no-break space and [[--]] an en dash.
Run over a whole title that was never \LaTeX, that is three traps: a
bare [[%]] would drop the rest of the title as a comment, a [[~]]
would become a space, and [[--]] and quote marks would come out other
than the escaper writes them.
So the decoder is not run over the whole value.
[[pylatexenc]]'s parser ([[LatexWalker]]) splits it into nodes, and
only the nodes that are allowed commands are decoded: a macro with its
arguments, a math group that holds an allowed command; a braced group
is kept with its braces and read inside; plain characters, [[~]],
[[--]] and quotes outside a command, and a math group without a
command ([[$x^2$]]), are kept exactly as written, so the escaper sees
them as it always did.
A bare [[%]] is written [[\%]] before the parse, since in a provider's
title it is a percent sign, not a comment: \enquote{50\% of
\textbackslash emph\{users\}} keeps its \enquote{50\%}.
Inside a command's arguments the decoder's conversion applies ([[~]] a
no-break space, which the escaper writes as a space; [[--]] an en dash,
which it writes [[--]] again).

\paragraph{What is decoded.}
The decoder knows some 940 macros, and run on text from the network it
is someone else's code on input nobody chose: a review found that a
title holding [[\href{...}{...}]] made it raise [[IndexError]], that a
thousand levels of nesting made it raise [[RecursionError]], and that
it turns [[\cite{x}]] into \enquote{<cit.>}, [[\url{u}]] into
\enquote{<u>} and [[\footnote{n}]] into \enquote{[n]}, text the title
never held.
So a value is decoded only when every command in it is on an
allow-list, by kind:
\begin{description}
\item[Accents] [[\'  \`  \^  \"  \~  \=  \.  \u  \v  \H  \c  \r  \k]]:
  each puts an accent on a letter, and for the letters titles use the
  decoder gives the precomposed character.
  [[\d]], [[\b]] and [[\t]] are left out: what they give
  (\enquote{a} with a dot or a bar below, two letters under a tie) has
  no character in the Latin ranges pdf\LaTeX{} sets, so the [[.bib]]
  would print a marker where \LaTeX{} printed the accent.
\item[Letters and ligatures] [[\ss \o \O \aa \AA \ae \AE \oe \OE \l \L
  \i \j]]: each stands for one letter.
\item[Font commands and switches] [[\textit \emph \textbf \textsc
  \texttt \textrm \textsf \textsl \textup]] and [[\em \it \bf \sc \tt
  \rm \sf \sl]]: they change how the text looks, and the [[.bib]] keeps
  the text; the switches the decoder lacks are added through its
  documented extension ([[add_context_category]] with
  [[MacroTextSpec]]) as printing nothing, the commands it lacks as
  printing their argument.
\item[Escaped specials] [[\& \% \_ \$ \# \{ \}]]: the character itself.
\item[Dashes and spaces] [[\textendash]], [[\textemdash]] and the
  control space [[\ ]]: the decoder gives the dash or the space, which
  the escaper writes back as [[--]], [[---]] or a space, the text
  \LaTeX{} printed before.
  The thin space [[\,]] is left out: it would come back as an ordinary
  space.
\item[Math symbols, inside math only] every macro of the decoder's own
  table whose replacement is one character and that takes no argument
  ([[\alpha]], [[\times]], [[\leq]], \dots), and the math alphabets
  [[\mathcal \mathbb \mathbf \mathit \mathrm \mathsf \mathtt \mathscr
  \mathfrak]]; the escaper sets the Greek letters and the common
  symbols in math mode again.
  A letter from a math alphabet ([[\mathcal{O}]] gives U+1D4AA) is
  folded to its plain letter with NFKC: pdf\LaTeX{} has no glyph for
  it, and the escaper would print the marker for text in other
  scripts.
\end{description}
Anything else, [[\cite]], [[\url]], [[\href]], [[\footnote]], [[\ref]],
[[\input]], [[\def]], an environment, any macro not listed, means the
value is not \LaTeX{} we understand, and it is escaped literally.

The value is decoded only when also:
\begin{itemize}
\item every backslash begins a command the parser found, so no
  backslash is left out of the check;
\item it parses without error (an unclosed [[$]] or brace does not);
\item it is at most [[LATEX_DECODE_MAX_CHARS]] (4000) characters long
  and nested at most [[LATEX_DECODE_MAX_DEPTH]] (8) levels deep: the
  longest title among the 6,594 papers of two copied real sessions has
  2042 characters, the longest author list 798, the longest venue 283,
  and a decoder run on a 100\,KB title took seconds;
\item every character a command decodes to is one the escaper can set
  (a letter of the Latin ranges pdf\LaTeX{} sets in T1, or a character
  it maps, such as a Greek letter), else the marker would replace what
  \LaTeX{} printed;
\item the decoded text is not empty;
\item and the decoder raises nothing: any exception from parsing or
  converting means the value is escaped literally, so no input can
  stop the writer.
\end{itemize}
An escaped brace, [[\{]] or [[\}]], decodes to a brace that means the
character, so it is handed back apart ([[LatexBrace]]) and written
[[\textbraceleft{}]] or [[\textbraceright{}]]; a brace in plain text
stays the case protection it was.
Values without a backslash never reach the parser, so their output is
byte for byte what it was.

