\paragraph{A cell that starts a table row}
The escaped text is a cell, and the cell that opens a row follows the
previous row's [[\\]]. That command looks ahead for a star and for
a length in brackets, so a title that begins
\enquote{[conference paper] \ldots} is read as a length (\enquote{Missing
number}, which stopped a real audit table), and one that begins with a
star loses it to [[\\*]]. [[latex_row_cell]] puts an empty group in
front of such a cell: [[{}]] typesets nothing and ends the look-ahead.
Every other cell is returned unchanged. The two tables whose first
cell is escaped text, the audit table and the provider-specific
queries, use it; the others start with a [[\citetitle]] or a number.

<<review report generation>>=
LATEX_ROW_LOOKAHEAD = ("[", "*")


def latex_row_cell(cell: str) -> str:
    """Protect `cell`, the first of a table row, from the `\\` before it."""
    if cell.lstrip().startswith(LATEX_ROW_LOOKAHEAD):
        return "{}" + cell
    return cell
@

