\subsubsection{Text in other scripts}
\label{sec:non-latin-text}

The escapers used to drop every letter beyond the Latin ranges. A title
wholly in Cyrillic---issue~\#220 met one, a 2018 paper on federated
authentication in supercomputer centres---then printed as an empty cell
of the audit table, a mixed title lost words, and nothing warned about
it. Dropping was the price of one hard constraint: the decks and books
these exports go into are built with pdf\LaTeX{} and T1 fonts, where a
raw Cyrillic or CJK character in the body is the error \enquote{Unicode
character \ldots{} not set up for use with LaTeX}. Xe\LaTeX{} and
Lua\LaTeX{} documents, on the other hand, set the same characters as
soon as their font has the script. (This section names such characters
by their code points for the same reason: it is woven into a
pdf\LaTeX{} document.)

The escaper cannot know which engine will read its output, so it leaves
the choice to the document: every run of characters pdf\LaTeX{} cannot
set is wrapped in one macro, [[\scholarnonlatin{...}]], whatever the
script. The document defines the macro (\cref{sec:non-latin-definition})
to print its argument under Xe\LaTeX{} and Lua\LaTeX{} and a short
visible marker under pdf\LaTeX{}, and an including document can define
it first to do better. Discarding the argument is safe under
pdf\LaTeX{}: [[inputenc]] makes each byte of a multi-byte UTF-8
character an active character, and the error is raised only when such
a character is executed, which never happens to the argument of a macro
that does not use it. That is checked by compiling, not assumed
(\cref{sec:non-latin-compile}).

A run spans a phrase, not a character. It continues over spaces,
digits and punctuation between two such characters, so the issue's
title becomes one macro and prints one marker instead of eight; the
specials inside are escaped as usual, since Xe\LaTeX{} typesets them.
It stops at a Latin letter, so a mixed title keeps its Latin words
readable under pdf\LaTeX{} too. A run without any letter or digit
(\enquote{$\star$}, an emoji) is decoration and is dropped as before: a
marker saying that text was lost would be false.

The Latin ranges themselves are not all settable. Below the cutoff,
pdf\LaTeX{} with T1 and [[inputenc]] sets exactly the characters that
the encoding files declare with [[\DeclareUnicodeCharacter]]:
[[t1enc.dfu]], [[ts1enc.dfu]], [[omsenc.dfu]] and [[utf8enc.dfu]] in
[[tex/latex/base]] of TeX Live 2025. We compiled every code point from
U+0080 to U+024F under T1, one per line, and the 213 that raised
\enquote{not set up for use with LaTeX} are exactly the ones none of
those files declares: the C1 controls, and letters such as
\enquote{kra} (U+0138, which stopped a real 10\,000-row audit table),
\enquote{h with stroke}, long s, and most of Latin Extended-B (the
Vietnamese horn letters, African and phonetic letters). They are
listed as [[T1_UNSET_RANGES]] and flagged like the characters beyond
the cutoff. Everything else in the range keeps passing through as it
always did, so a title in French, Polish or Swedish is unchanged. The
same experiment under OT1 fails on twenty more (the guillemets, eth,
thorn, ogonek letters, eng), which is one reason the standalone
document loads T1 too (\cref{sec:standalone-t1}).

None of these letters has a \TeX{} command that would set it in T1
without further packages, so they are wrapped rather than mapped. One
such letter inside a Latin word would otherwise cut the word in three,
\enquote{Ng}, a marker, \enquote{\ldots}, which helps nobody: a word
holding a flagged \emph{Latin} letter (its Unicode name begins with
LATIN, which also covers Latin Extended Additional, the Vietnamese tone
letters) is flagged whole. A word next to a flagged letter of another
script is left alone, so \enquote{Python} keeps printing between two
Chinese words without spaces.

Greek needs a rule of its own, because single Greek letters already
have a good rendering: math mode, for titles like
\enquote{$\pi$-ARL}. A Greek \emph{word} set that way becomes a string
of italic variables, and its accented letters, which the math tables
lack, used to be dropped. So a maximal sequence of Greek letters counts
as text when it has at least [[GREEK_WORD_MIN]] letters or a letter the
tables cannot set; shorter sequences such as \enquote{$\alpha\beta$}
stay in math mode, exactly as before.

Some characters beyond the cutoff are not text in another script at
all, and wrapping them hid readable words in real sessions (the
hpc-cli and cli-efficiency audit tables): the Latin ligatures
\enquote{fi}, \enquote{fl} and \enquote{ff} as single characters
(U+FB00 to U+FB06) in \enquote{Workflow}, sub- and superscript
numerals as in \enquote{CO$_2$}, the modifier letters of
transliterations as in \enquote{Hawai`i}, and the script letter ell.
They get a rendering of their own before or instead of the split:
\begin{description}
\item[Ligatures] are folded to their letters by compatibility
  normalisation (NFKC). We apply it to these seven characters only: on
  everything else it would also turn a superscript two into a plain 2
  and one half into \enquote{1/2} with a fraction slash.
\item[Sub- and superscripts] (U+2070 to U+209C: numerals, signs,
  parentheses and letters) become [[\textsubscript]] or
  [[\textsuperscript]] of the character they decompose to, both
  defined by the \LaTeX{} kernel, which the compile tests check
  (\cref{sec:non-latin-compile}). The table [[LATEX_SCRIPT_CHARS]] is
  built from the Unicode names and decompositions, not typed; a script
  minus decomposes to the minus sign, which becomes a hyphen, as in
  the table of mathematical symbols.
\item[The script ell] is set as math [[\ell]], like the other
  mathematical symbols.
\item[Modifier letters] used as apostrophes become the nearest ASCII
  marks: the turned comma (the Hawaiian okina) and the reversed comma
  a left quote, the modifier prime an apostrophe. The other spacing
  modifier letters (U+02B0 to U+02FF) are letters to Unicode but not
  text on their own; [[_run_kind]] does not count them, so alone they
  are dropped as before, and inside a run they stay part of it.
\end{description}
Control characters (category Cc) are dropped also inside a run. They
print nothing, and a host that prints the run under pdf\LaTeX{} with
T2A, as the fragment header suggests, stops at them (we tried a C1
control after a Cyrillic word).

The split is computed once per text, as a flag per character, and
[[split_nonlatin]] cuts the text into plain pieces and runs. The
escapers walk the pieces, and the audit table asks the same split
whether a title has any letter left outside the runs
(\cref{sec:audit-nonlatin-fallback}).

