The parameters above the table used to be the session's [[query]] and
[[providers]], which each append overwrites: a session of six searches
on six providers reported \enquote{The search covered scopus}, the
provider of the last append
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/163}).  They are now
derived from all search records: every provider any record names (the
providers of a merged record each count, \cref{sec:merged-records}),
the one query if all records share it and otherwise their number, and
the period in which the databases answered ([[search_source_date]]),
with the number of searches whose date is unknown.  A provider is
listed among those searched only if it answered at least one search,
as in the caption of the audit table: one that was skipped or failed
outright every time was not searched, and the report says so, with the
reason, under \enquote{Not searched}, so that a reader sees the gap
instead of a database the review never reached.  A provider that an
old merged record with papers names as failed may have answered part
of that search or nothing of it, which the record cannot tell; it is
listed among the providers searched, with the note the caption gives
it, \enquote{failed or incomplete in a merged record}
([[searched_providers_text]]), so the report and the caption say the
same.  The table itself
appears whenever a record knows its outcome or its dates, so that each
search is described with them, in a Date column when any date is
known; only a session whose records know neither, from before outcomes
were kept, still gets the table under the old conditions alone, and
its report looks as it did.  Sessions without any records fall back to
the session's own fields.

\paragraph{Searches without a recorded outcome}
\label{sec:unrecorded-outcome}

A record written before outcomes were kept has neither hits nor an
error ([[outcome_unknown]]).  Every search since records its number of
hits, also zero, and a failure records its reason, so the pair
[[hits is None]] and no error marks exactly those old records.  Such a
record says that the provider was queried, not that it answered:
DBLP served only its anti-bot page in the autumn of 2026, and a session
searched then holds DBLP records that look exactly like those of a
provider that answered
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/188}).  Counting
them as answered, as the report and the audit caption did, named DBLP
among the databases searched.  Counting them as failed would be as
wrong the other way.

So a provider counts as searched when one of its records answered, or
when a paper of the session names it ([[decision_provider_names]]: the
decision's [[provider]], split and compared as the audit rows split it,
[[PROVIDER_SEPARATORS]], ignoring case).  A paper the provider returned
shows that one of its searches answered.  The name is not always that:
before one record was kept per provider search
(\cref{sec:merged-records}), every paper of a merged search was given
every merged provider's name, whichever returned it, so such a session
could name a provider that only served an error page.  The names are
therefore read through the evidence the decision holds
([[decision_provider_evidence]], \cref{sec:provider-evidence}), as the
audit rows read them.  A provider known only from
records without an outcome is listed apart, under \enquote{Outcome not
recorded} ([[unrecorded_providers]]), and not under \enquote{Not
searched}, which would claim it was never reached.  This also holds
when another of its searches failed: the old one may have answered.
The audit caption applies the same rule (\cref{sec:audit-databases}).

The rule describes what the report claims, not which record a session
keeps.  [[record_rank]] still ranks such a record as answered, so a
failed re-run does not replace it while a paper links to it or names
its provider: the old run may have answered with them, and replacing
it would turn that into a failure.  Otherwise the failed re-run
replaces it (\cref{sec:unknown-outcome-replaced}).  A failed record
never counts as searched, whatever the papers say, so the report then
lists the provider under \enquote{Not searched} with the failure as
the reason, unless another of its records answered, or has no outcome
either and is listed under \enquote{Outcome not recorded}.

<<review report generation>>=
def outcome_unknown(record: SearchRecord) -> bool:
    """Whether the record cannot tell if its search answered.

    True for records written before outcomes were kept: no hit count
    and no error.
    """
    return record.hits is None and not record.error


def decision_provider_names(session: ReviewSession) -> set[str]:
    """The providers, lower-cased, that some decision of the session names.

    Each decision's names are read through `decision_provider_evidence`.
    """
    return {
        name.lower()
        for decision in session.decisions
        for name in decision_provider_evidence(decision)
    }


def unrecorded_providers(session: ReviewSession) -> list[str]:
    """The providers known only from searches without an outcome."""
    answered = set(searched_providers(session))
    return list(dict.fromkeys(
        name
        for record in current_records(session.search_records)
        if outcome_unknown(record)
        for name in record.merged_providers
        if name not in answered
    ))


def unrecorded_text(session: ReviewSession) -> str:
    """`unrecorded_providers` as a comma-separated list for the report."""
    return ", ".join(unrecorded_providers(session))
@

\paragraph{What a paper shows of its providers}
\label{sec:provider-evidence}

A decision's [[provider]] says which providers returned the paper,
except when an old merged search wrote it.  Until
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/164} a merged search gave
every paper the merged string, \enquote{openalex, dblp}, whichever
provider returned it (\cref{sec:merged-records}).  Sessions searched
that way while DBLP served only its anti-bot page name DBLP on papers it
never returned: in the audit rows, through them in the caption's
\enquote{Databases:} sentence, and through [[decision_provider_names]]
in the report's searched providers
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/191}).  Nor can a
string with several names be taken as a merged search's and discarded:
since \#164 a paper returned by two per-provider searches names both,
and the repair of the sessions damaged by \#192 united the stored strings
with the providers of each decision's links.

So a name counts when the decision holds evidence for it.  Two things
are evidence.  One is a link to a single-provider search
([[found_by]], \cref{sec:review-found-by}): such a record was written
only for a provider whose search returned the paper.  A link to a merged
record proves nothing, for the same reason as its string.  The other is
the paper's [[sources]], which each provider sets to its own name on the
papers it converts and which the search merge unions over the copies
it deduplicates; a paper that only OpenAlex returned has the one source
[[openalex]].  Names are compared ignoring case and split
on [[PROVIDER_SEPARATORS]], as the rows split them: the string, each
source and each link's provider alike.  A source can itself be a joined
string, since the notes-module fallback of [[create_review_session]]
([[paper_from_decision_record]]) stores the old record's provider as
the paper's only source, and a link counts
as single-provider only when its provider splits into one name,
whatever the separator.

Four cases keep the string as it is.  A paper without sources, as in
sessions from before sources were stored, has nothing that can rule a
name out, and its names all count, as they did.  A single-provider link
confirms a name and adds one, but it does not rule any out: links are
younger than the merged strings (they are kept since
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/128}), and a paper that
an old run returned and a later run returned again carries only the
later run's link, so its links do not show that the old run did not
return it (\cref{sec:unknown-outcome-replaced} makes the same point for
records).  Sources do not have that gap, as the next paragraph but one
explains.  Evidence that names none of the string's providers, such
as an enrichment source alone ([[s2]]) or the fallback's placeholder
[[previous]], contradicts the string rather than choosing among its
names, and is no better than none: dropping every name would leave the
row without a database at all.  A string with one name was never
written by a merged search, which always wrote two or more, so it
stands even when the sources disagree; the previous case already keeps
it, since evidence against its only name is evidence against every
name.  And a name that is not one of
the search providers in [[PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES]], such as [[snowball]],
which [[united_providers]] can join to a search's name, is not a
database a merged search could have named.

The two kinds of evidence are not equally clean, and they are not used
alike.  Sources also grow without a search: enrichment merges each
looked-up record into the paper ([[Paper.merge_with]] in
[[enrich_paper]]), which unions the enriching provider's name---S2,
arXiv, OpenAlex, IEEE, WoS or Scopus---into the sources, and preprint
adoption unions in its candidate's sources (\cref{preprint-module}),
which may name the provider that returned the preprint rather than the
published version.  So a source only confirms a name the string already
has; it never adds one, else every enriched paper would gain a database
that never searched it.  The error this leaves is in the old direction:
a provider that enriched a paper but did not return it is still named
if the merged string names it.  DBLP, the case that prompted this,
enriches nothing and is not a preprint candidate source (the title
search asks OpenAlex and arXiv, the other tiers copy the paper's own
sources), so a [[dblp]] in the sources means DBLP returned the paper.
Its absence tells as much: before \#164 a re-run gave the decision both
the paper as returned and the merged string of that run, so the sources
and the string come from the same search.  A single-provider link does
add its provider when the string lacks it, since the link itself is
proof; the order of the string is kept, and such names follow it.  On
the real sessions no decision with several names has a single-provider
link but no sources, so the links' part changes no row there.

One more limit comes from the paper registry: [[Paper.from_dict]]
returns the registered object for an id it already holds, so two
decisions of a session that share a paper id share one [[Paper]], and
the second is judged by the sources of the copy loaded first.

The rule was checked against copies of the real sessions before it was
adopted (70 sessions, November 2026).  A provider it drops from every
paper of a session is DBLP in sessions whose merged searches recorded
its failure or no outcome, OpenAlex where its merged search failed and
in two sessions (7\,892 papers) in which no paper has [[openalex]] in
its sources, and IEEE and S2 in three sessions where no paper has them
in its sources.  The absence is evidence because each provider names
itself on every paper it converts and the merge unions the sources of
every copy it combines, so a provider that returned any paper of the
session would appear in that paper's sources.  As a cross-check, none
of the 119\,726 names the rule drops there is the provider whose own
page the paper's [[url]] is.

<<review report generation>>=
def decision_provider_evidence(decision: ReviewDecision) -> list[str]:
    """The provider names of `decision` that its evidence supports.

    When the paper has `sources`, a name of the `provider` string
    counts only if a source or a single-provider search link
    (`found_by`) names it. Without sources, when the evidence names
    none of the string's providers (so also for a string of one name),
    and for names not in `PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES`, the string stands.
    Single-provider links add their providers at the end.
    """
    def split(text: str | None) -> list[str]:
        return [
            part.strip() for part in PROVIDER_SEPARATORS.split(text or "")
            if part.strip()
        ]

    names: list[str] = []
    for part in split(decision.provider):
        if part.lower() not in {n.lower() for n in names}:
            names.append(part)
    linked = [
        parts[0] for _, provider, _ in decision.found_by
        if len(parts := split(provider)) == 1
    ]
    sources = [part for source in decision.paper.sources or []
               for part in split(source)]
    if sources:
        attested = {n.lower() for n in linked + sources}
        kept = [
            name for name in names
            if name.lower() in attested
            or name.lower() not in PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES
        ]
        if any(name.lower() in PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES for name in kept):
            names = kept
    for provider in linked:
        if provider.lower() not in {n.lower() for n in names}:
            names.append(provider)
    return names
@

