\paragraph{Retired searches in the report}
\label{sec:retired-in-reports}
A retired search (\cref{sec:retired-searches}) is not one of the
review's searches any more, but it was run, and its papers are in the
report.  So the report describes the current searches as it described
all of them before: the query or the number of queries, the providers
searched, not searched or without a recorded outcome, and the period
all come from the current records only ([[current_records]]), and so
does the abstract's \enquote{The search covered \dots}.  Both then add
how many searches were retired: the parameters a \enquote{Retired}
item, the abstract the sentence of the audit caption
(\cref{sec:audit-retired}).  The table of searches keeps every record,
the retired ones marked after their query with what [[retired_label]]
says (\enquote{q (retired 2026-11-20: first version)}), so that a
reader sees what each was and why it no longer counts; the table
appears whenever any record is retired.  The mark sits in the query
cell rather than the hits column, which a session from before outcomes
were kept does not have.  A session without retired records gets the
report it got before.

Let's verify it on the session above with a draft query retired: the
draft's provider (HAL, known from the draft's merged record only, while
DBLP stays through a current record) and its period drop out of the
parameters and the abstract, which count it apart, and the table marks
it.

