A session whose searches are all retired has no current search to
describe, and the general wording said \enquote{Queries: 0},
\enquote{Providers: none} and that the search covered no database,
which reads as if nothing had been searched.  Its papers were found,
by searches that no longer count.  So such a session is worded on its
own: the parameters say that all its searches have been retired and
point to the table, which lists them, and the abstract says that the
papers were found through searches that have since been retired,
naming their databases by full name, as the caption's databases
sentence does (a semantic check is no database and is not named).  A
session without any records is not such a session; it keeps the
fallback to its own fields.

<<review report generation>>=
def all_searches_retired(session: ReviewSession) -> bool:
    """Whether the session has search records and all are retired."""
    return bool(session.search_records) and not current_records(
        session.search_records
    )


def retired_databases_text(session: ReviewSession) -> str:
    """", in A, B" naming the databases of the retired searches; "" if none.
    """
    shorts = list(dict.fromkeys(
        short for record in retired_records(session.search_records)
        for short in provider_short_names(record.provider)
        if short not in RANKED_SHORT_NAMES
    ))
    names = audit_database_names(
        shorts, AUDIT_STRINGS["en"], lambda short: [], codes=False
    )
    return f", in {', '.join(names)}" if names else ""
