\paragraph{Defining the macro for text in other scripts}
\label{sec:non-latin-definition}

Text the escapers wrap in [[\scholarnonlatin]] (\cref{sec:non-latin-text})
needs a definition wherever it is typeset, and the definition must
choose by engine: the argument under Xe\LaTeX{} and Lua\LaTeX{}, a
marker under pdf\LaTeX{}. The test is whether the engine's own
primitives exist, [[\XeTeXversion]] and [[\directlua]], which needs no
package. Each candidate is a [[\providecommand]], so the first
definition wins: a host document that defined the macro before
including our output keeps its own, and of our three lines only the
first that applies takes effect. The choice is made once, at
definition time, so the macro itself is a plain one-argument macro that
behaves the same in a table cell, a heading or a [[\write]].

The marker is localised by the caller (the audit table has Swedish);
the definition is written only when the output uses the macro, so a
document without such text is byte for byte what it was before.

The separator [[\scholarnonlatinsep]] (\cref{sec:non-latin-text}) is
defined the same way, before [[\scholarnonlatin]] and only when the
output uses it, so output in which no run touches a letter keeps its
three lines.  It is nothing under Xe\LaTeX{} and Lua\LaTeX{}, where
the run is printed and the source had no space; otherwise it is a
control space, [[\ ]], an interword space that a following letter
cannot swallow.

It does not ask whether [[\scholarnonlatin]] is defined already.  A
first version took a defined macro for a host's own, one that prints
the text, and made the separator empty; but in a book the macro is
usually defined by scholar itself, by an earlier fragment or by a
cited scholar [[.bib]] whose [[@preamble]] biblatex reads at the start
of the document, and the marker ran into the Latin words again
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/262}).  A definition
cannot tell who wrote it, so the host says it: a host that defines
[[\scholarnonlatin]] to print the text, as the fragment header
suggests for Cyrillic under pdf\LaTeX{}, defines
[[\scholarnonlatinsep]] as empty beside it, and the header gives that
line too.  A host that prints the text but does not define the
separator gets a space where the scripts touch, which is a visible
but harmless difference from the source.

<<review report generation>>=
NONLATIN_MARKER = "[non-Latin text]"
NONLATIN_SEP_DEFINITION = [
    r"\ifdefined\XeTeXversion\providecommand{\scholarnonlatinsep}{}\fi",
    r"\ifdefined\directlua\providecommand{\scholarnonlatinsep}{}\fi",
    r"\providecommand{\scholarnonlatinsep}{\ }",
]


def nonlatin_definition(
    marker: str = NONLATIN_MARKER, *, separator: bool = False
) -> list[str]:
    """Return the lines that define `\\scholarnonlatin` unless defined.

    With `separator`, the lines that define `\\scholarnonlatinsep` come
    first.
    """
    return (NONLATIN_SEP_DEFINITION if separator else []) + [
        r"\ifdefined\XeTeXversion\providecommand{\scholarnonlatin}[1]{#1}\fi",
        r"\ifdefined\directlua\providecommand{\scholarnonlatin}[1]{#1}\fi",
        f"\\providecommand{{\\scholarnonlatin}}[1]{{{marker}}}",
    ]


def uses_nonlatin(texts: Sequence[str | None]) -> bool:
    """Whether any of `texts` calls `\\scholarnonlatin` or its separator."""
    return any(
        text and (NONLATIN_MACRO + "{" in text or NONLATIN_SEP_MACRO in text)
        for text in texts
    )


def uses_nonlatin_sep(texts: Sequence[str | None]) -> bool:
    """Whether any of `texts` calls `\\scholarnonlatinsep`."""
    return any(text and NONLATIN_SEP_MACRO in text for text in texts)
@

A [[.bib]] carries the definition itself, because it is also cited from
documents we did not write, which do not define the macro: a user's
paper citing the report's [[.bib]], or the output of
[[search -f bibtex]] (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/243}).
BibTeX's place for such a definition is an [[@preamble]], which biber
copies into the [[.bbl]] that biblatex reads.  It is the same three
lines, joined on one line so that the line scanner of the provenance
commands, which looks for entries, never mistakes it for one, and it is
written only when an entry uses the macro, so that a [[.bib]] without
such text is byte for byte what it was.  In the report's own document
the macro is then defined twice; being [[\providecommand]]s, the first
definition read stays, and both give the same text.

<<review report generation>>=
def nonlatin_bib_preamble(entries: Sequence[str]) -> str | None:
    """Return a one-line `@preamble` defining `\\scholarnonlatin`.

    None when no entry uses the macro.
    """
    if not uses_nonlatin(entries):
        return None
    definition = nonlatin_definition(separator=uses_nonlatin_sep(entries))
    return '@preamble{ "' + " ".join(definition) + '" }'


def bibtex_file_text(entries: Sequence[str]) -> str:
    """Join BibTeX entries into a file, after the `@preamble` they need."""
    preamble = nonlatin_bib_preamble(entries)
    return "\n\n".join([preamble, *entries] if preamble else entries)
@

A standalone document wraps a body in the skeleton a reader expects from a
report: title page, abstract, table of contents, the body, an optional
bibliography command and an optional appendix. The title is escaped here
because session names are free text (an ampersand in a session name used
to be a hard \LaTeX{} error). The bibliography command is passed as a
literal string rather than a flag so a caller can choose its options; the
review report passes none, since it cites everything with [[\fullcite]]
and has no reference list of its own. The definition of
[[\scholarnonlatin]] goes into the preamble, after the packages, when
the title, abstract, body, appendix or the cited [[.bib]] ([[bib_text]],
whose fields biblatex typesets through [[\fullcite]]) uses it.

\label{sec:standalone-t1}
The standalone document loads the T1 font encoding before the other
packages. Without it pdf\LaTeX{} sets the report in OT1, which has no
[[\textquotedbl]]: every session with a quoted query, the normal case
for a phrase search, gave a report that stopped with
\enquote{Command \textbackslash textquotedbl unavailable in encoding
OT1}. OT1 also lacks the guillemets, eth, thorn and the ogonek
letters, and T1 is what the decks and books use, so a title that
compiles in one compiles in the other. This one line is the only
change to standalone output for Latin text; fragments are unchanged,
since the font encoding is the including document's choice, like
[[inputenc]].

<<review report generation>>=
STANDALONE_FONTENC = r"\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}"


def latex_document(
    body: list[str],
    *,
    title: str,
    date: str,
    bib_resource: str,
    abstract: str | None = None,
    bibliography: str | None = None,
    appendix: list[str] | None = None,
    bib_text: str = "",
) -> list[str]:
    """Wrap `body` in a compilable LaTeX document.

    `bib_text` is the content of the `.bib` the document cites, read
    only to decide whether `\\scholarnonlatin` needs a definition.
    """
    title = escape_latex(title)
    lines: list[str] = [r"\documentclass{article}", STANDALONE_FONTENC]
    lines.extend(LATEX_PACKAGES)
    texts = [title, abstract, *body, *(appendix or []), bib_text]
    if uses_nonlatin(texts):
        lines.extend(nonlatin_definition(separator=uses_nonlatin_sep(texts)))
    lines.extend(
        [
            f"\\addbibresource{{{bib_resource}}}",
            "",
            f"\\title{{{title}}}",
            f"\\date{{{date}}}",
            "",
            r"\begin{document}",
            r"\maketitle",
            "",
        ]
    )
    if abstract is not None:
        lines.extend([r"\begin{abstract}", abstract, r"\end{abstract}"])
    lines.extend(
        [r"\clearpage", r"\clearpage", r"\tableofcontents", r"\clearpage", ""]
    )
    lines.extend(body)
    if bibliography:
        lines.extend([bibliography, ""])
    if appendix:
        lines.append(r"\appendix")
        lines.extend(appendix)
    lines.append(r"\end{document}")
    return lines
@

A fragment is the same body without the skeleton, meant to be read by
[[\input]] from another document's body. It cannot set anything up for
itself: biblatex refuses [[\addbibresource]] outside the preamble, and
package loading is the including document's business. So instead of
declaring, the fragment \emph{instructs}: a comment header lists the
packages the including preamble must load (minus [[inputenc]], which the
includer has already decided on and which is hostile to Xe\LaTeX{}), the
[[\addbibresource]] line, and the need for biber. An abstract cannot be
emitted either, because an [[abstract]] environment belongs to the
including document; it is preserved as comment lines so that text written
by an LLM is not silently lost. Callers may add further comment lines
for facts that must reach the author but never the compiled page, such
as the session a table was made from.

The one thing a fragment does define is [[\scholarnonlatin]], when its
body or its [[.bib]] uses it: in the body, right after the header and
so before the first use, since a fragment has no preamble. Being a
[[\providecommand]], it yields to a definition the host made before the
[[\input]], and the header says so and how, because the marker is a
poor rendering of a title the host could print: under pdf\LaTeX{}
Cyrillic needs only the T2A encoding, which we checked by compiling
the suggested definition with the issue's title. The abstract does not
count, since in a fragment it is only a comment.

<<review report generation>>=
FRAGMENT_COMMENT_WIDTH = 76


def nonlatin_fragment_header(
    marker: str, separator: bool = False
) -> list[str]:
    """Return the header comment that explains `\\scholarnonlatin`.

    With `separator` it explains `\\scholarnonlatinsep` too.
    """
    lines = [
        "% Text that pdfLaTeX cannot set in T1 (Cyrillic, CJK, ...) is",
        r"% wrapped in \scholarnonlatin{...}. The \providecommand lines",
        "% below print the text under XeLaTeX and LuaLaTeX (load a font",
        f"% that has the script) and print {marker} under pdfLaTeX.",
        "% To print it under pdfLaTeX too, define the command before the",
        r"% \input, e.g. for Cyrillic with \usepackage[T2A,T1]{fontenc}:",
        r"%   \newcommand{\scholarnonlatin}[1]{{\fontencoding{T2A}"
        r"\selectfont #1}}",
    ]
    if separator:
        lines += [
            r"% Where such text touches a letter, \scholarnonlatinsep{}",
            "% stands between them: a space under pdfLaTeX, nothing under",
            r"% XeLaTeX and LuaLaTeX. If your \scholarnonlatin prints the",
            "% text, define the separator as empty beside it:",
            r"%   \newcommand{\scholarnonlatinsep}{}",
            "% otherwise you get a space where the scripts touch.",
        ]
    return lines


def latex_fragment(
    body: list[str],
    *,
    packages: list[str],
    bib_resource: str | None = None,
    abstract: str | None = None,
    generator: str = "",
    comments: list[str] | None = None,
    bib_text: str = "",
    nonlatin_marker: str = NONLATIN_MARKER,
) -> list[str]:
    """Wrap `body` in an input-able fragment with an instructional header.

    `bib_text` is the content of the `.bib` the fragment cites, read
    only to decide whether `\\scholarnonlatin` needs a definition;
    `nonlatin_marker` is what that definition prints under pdfLaTeX.
    """
    nonlatin = uses_nonlatin([*body, bib_text])
    separator = uses_nonlatin_sep([*body, bib_text])
    lines: list[str] = [r"% --- scholar LaTeX fragment " + "-" * 44]
    if generator:
        lines.append(f"% Generated by: {generator}")
    for comment in comments or []:
        lines.append(f"% {comment}")
    lines.append(r"% \input this file from the body of a document whose")
    lines.append("% preamble loads:")
    for package in packages:
        if "inputenc" in package:
            continue
        lines.append(f"%   {package}")
    if bib_resource:
        lines.append(f"%   \\addbibresource{{{bib_resource}}}")
        lines.append("% and which is compiled with biber.")
    if r"\begin{refsection}" in body:
        lines.extend(
            [
                "% Citations in this fragment sit in their own refsection,",
                r"% so the including document's \printbibliography is not",
                "% affected. Refsections cannot nest: \\input this file",
                "% outside any refsection of your own.",
            ]
        )
    if abstract:
        lines.append("% Abstract (not emitted; abstracts belong to the")
        lines.append("% including document):")
        for wrapped in textwrap.wrap(abstract, FRAGMENT_COMMENT_WIDTH):
            lines.append(f"%   {wrapped}")
    if nonlatin:
        lines.extend(nonlatin_fragment_header(nonlatin_marker, separator))
    lines.append("% " + "-" * 70)
    lines.append("")
    if nonlatin:
        lines.extend(
            nonlatin_definition(nonlatin_marker, separator=separator)
        )
    lines.extend(body)
    return lines
@

The decision record cites every paper the review touched with
[[\fullcite]], including the discarded ones. In a document that prints a
bibliography, those citations would drag every discarded paper into the
reference list, and a reader could no longer tell which papers the text
actually draws on. biblatex's reference sections solve this cleanly: a
[[refsection]] environment gives its citations their own numbering, and
[[\printbibliography]] defaults to the section it is called in. Wrapping
the decision record therefore keeps the bibliography equal to what the
prose cites, and [[\fullcite]] needs no bibliography to resolve. The same
wrapping protects an including document from a fragment.

<<review report generation>>=
def refsection(lines: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Wrap `lines` in a biblatex `refsection` environment."""
    return [r"\begin{refsection}", *lines, r"\end{refsection}"]
@

