\subsubsection{Field values that read back}
\label{sec:bibtex-field-values}

Every [[.bib]] Scholar writes was assembled with f-strings, the value
between a pair of braces as the provider sent it, or as
[[escape_bibtex]] left it
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/304}).
A BibTeX reader ends a braced value at the brace that balances the one
that opened it, so the braces inside a value decide where it ends.
Written that way, these inputs broke an entry (checked 2026-11-23 by
parsing each entry with [[bibtexparser]] 2.1.0, the reader of
[[--cited-from]], and running biber 2.21 of \TeX{} Live 2025 on it in
its [[--tool]] mode):
\begin{description}
\item[An opening brace without its pair] in the title, the authors, the
  venue, the DOI or the URL: the reader runs to the end of the file
  looking for the end of the entry, and the entry is lost
  (\enquote{Unexpectedly reached end of file}).
  [[escape_bibtex]] passes braces through on purpose
  (\cref{sec:latex-escaping}), so a title with a stray brace from a
  provider's markup is written so too.
\item[A closing brace without its pair] ends the field early: a DOI
  [[10.5555/a}b]] was read as another value, and in a title the
  fields after it were lost.
\item[A line break] in a DOI or URL parses, but biber turns it into a
  space, so the link points elsewhere.
\end{description}
Neither reader knows an escape for a brace: biber counts [[\{]] and
[[\}]] as braces like any other (an entry with [[doi = {10.5555/a\}b}]]
is a syntax error), and enclosing the value in double quotes instead
does not help either (\enquote{does not parse correctly}).
[[%]], [[#]] and [["]] inside the braces of a DOI were read back as
written by both, and the free-text fields have them escaped already
([[\%]], [[\#]], [[\textquotedbl{}]]).
A backslash is harmless for parsing in the middle of a value, but not
at its end or before a brace (below), and in a title it was not
harmless for compiling: [[escape_bibtex]] passed it through, so the
title [[x\y]] reached pdf\LaTeX{} as the command [[\y]] and the report
stopped (\enquote{Undefined control sequence}, compiled with the code
before this change).
The key needs nothing: [[cite_key_base]] keeps letters and digits of
the surname and the year only, and the suffixes are letters
(\cref{sec:cite-keys}), so no key holds any of these characters; it is
left untouched, so keys already cited do not change.

\paragraph{Why not [[bibtexparser]]'s writer.}
[[bibtexparser]] 2 can write a library as well as read it, and it is a
dependency already, so it was tried first, configured for our layout
(two-space indent, trailing comma, values in braces through its
[[AddEnclosingMiddleware]]).
Two plain entries came out as ours do but for a final line break; the
[[@preamble]] came out as [[@preamble{"..."}]], where ours is
[[@preamble{ "..." }]], so a [[.bib]] with text in other scripts would
change.
What decides it is the values: the writer puts braces around a value
without looking inside ([[f"{{{value}}}"]] in
[[bibtexparser/middlewares/enclosing.py]]), so the DOI
[[10.5555/a}b]] came out as [[doi = {10.5555/a}b}]], the very entry
that breaks, and writing the same library a second time doubled the
braces of every value, since the middleware changes the entries it
writes.
None of its middlewares balances braces.
The library would therefore need the same guard as our own lines, so
we keep the writer and give it the guard: one function that every field
goes through.

\paragraph{The guard.}
[[bibtex_field]] writes one field line, and what it does to the value
depends on how biblatex reads the field.
biblatex's data model declares [[doi]] a verbatim field and [[url]] a
URI ([[blx-dm.def]]): their characters are printed and linked as they
are, so we must not escape them, and a brace there is one character of
an identifier.

The two readers disagree on one more character, the backslash.
biber counts every brace, a backslash before it or not, while
[[bibtexparser]] takes a backslash before a brace as escaping it and
does not count that brace, also after a second backslash: a title
written [[{a\}]] is a whole field to biber, and it, [[{a\\}]] and
[[{a\}b{}]] each lose [[bibtexparser]] the entry (checked as above).
So no value may hold a backslash right before a brace or at its end.

In a DOI or URL, what cannot be written is percent-encoded, the URL's
own escape, which a browser and the [[doi.org]] resolver decode
(\cref{sec:identifier-in-path} in the network module):
a brace without a pair ([[%7B]], [[%7D]]), a backslash before a brace
or at the end ([[%5C]]), and every C0 control character
([[%00]] to [[%1F]]; biber turns a line break into a space, so the
link would point elsewhere).
Everything else stays as given, balanced braces too: the readers keep
them.
The readers of our [[.bib]] files undo exactly this encoding
([[read_bibtex_verbatim]]), so a DOI written as [[10.5555/a%7Db]] is
[[10.5555/a}b]] again when [[--cited-from]] matches it or
[[prov import]] keys its note.

For that to be exact, a value that holds one of the writer's own codes
as text must not read back as the character the code stands for: the
DOIs [[10.5555/a%7Db]] and [[10.5555/a}b]] are two papers, and with
both in a session [[--cited-from]] matched both entries to the second
and [[prov import]] filed the first paper's note under the second's id.
Escaping every [[%]] would fix it, but would change every URL that
holds a [[%20]] and the link it makes.
So only a [[%]] that the reader would take for the start of a code is
escaped: one followed by zero or more [[25]] and then one of the
writer's codes ([[7B]], [[7D]], [[5C]], [[00]] to [[1F]]) is written
[[%25]].
The reader decodes a code, and a [[%25]] that is followed by zero or
more [[25]] and then a code; any other [[%]] it leaves alone.
\Cref{tab:bibtex-verbatim-escapes} shows the cases.
The rule is exact because the writer's choice for each [[%]] depends
only on the text after it, and the reader sees the same text after it:
an escaped [[%]] becomes [[%25]] followed by the same text, which the
reader turns back into [[%]] by the same test, and an unescaped one is
followed by text that is no run of [[25]] and a code, so the reader
passes it.
A character the writer encodes becomes one of the codes, and no code
is [[25]], so the two kinds of escape never meet.
The rule is for the DOI (and the [[% ID:]] line of the provenance
blocks), which Scholar reads back.
A URL it never reads back, and there the [[%25]] would change the link:
[[q=%7Bfoo%7D]] written [[q=%257Bfoo%257D]] points to another address,
while a brace the writer encodes as [[%7B]] and a [[%7B]] the URL held
name the same resource.
So the [[url]] field takes the encoding without the [[%25]] rule
([[exact=False]]).

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Value & Written & Why \\
\midrule
[[a}b]] & [[a%7Db]] & a brace without a pair \\
[[a%7Db]] & [[a%257Db]] & a [[%]] before a code \\
[[a%257Db]] & [[a%25257Db]] & a [[%]] before [[25]] and a code \\
[[a%25b]] & [[a%25b]] & no code follows \\
[[a%20b]] & [[a%20b]] & [[20]] is no code \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The verbatim encoding of a DOI or URL: the value, what the
  writer writes, and why. Reading a written value gives the value.}
\label{tab:bibtex-verbatim-escapes}
\end{table}

In the free-text fields (title, authors, venue, and the year) a value
that is \LaTeX{} the decoder knows is decoded and escaped
(\cref{sec:latex-titles}); otherwise the escaper turns every backslash
of the text into [[\textbackslash{}]] (\cref{sec:latex-escaping}).
Either way the only backslashes left are those of the commands the
escaper writes, none of which ends in a backslash or stands before a
brace.
In a value that is not decoded, a brace escaped in the text, [[\{]] or
[[\}]] after an even run of backslashes, still means the character
itself, so it becomes [[\textbraceleft{}]] or [[\textbraceright{}]]
before anything else, as a decoded one does; the escaper alone would
make it a printed backslash before a brace that counts.
Then a brace without a pair becomes the same command.
Braces that pair stay: they are the case protection the escaper keeps
on purpose.
A C0 control character has no place in a title and biber 2.21 dies on
one (a title holding a NUL ended in a core dump), so a tab or a line
break becomes a space and the others are dropped.
For any other value nothing changes, so the [[.bib]] files of ordinary
papers are byte for byte what they were.

<<review report generation>>=
BIBTEX_VERBATIM_FIELDS = frozenset({"doi", "url"})
BIBTEX_TEXT_BRACES = {"{": r"\textbraceleft{}", "}": r"\textbraceright{}"}
BIBTEX_ESCAPED_BRACE = re.compile(r"(?<!\\)((?:\\\\)*)\\([{}])")
BIBTEX_TEXT_CONTROLS = {
    code: (" " if chr(code) in "\t\n\v\f\r" else None) for code in range(32)
}
BIBTEX_VERBATIM_CODES = r"(?:7B|7D|5C|[01][0-9A-F])"
BIBTEX_BEFORE_CODE = re.compile(rf"(?:25)*{BIBTEX_VERBATIM_CODES}")
BIBTEX_VERBATIM_ESCAPE = re.compile(
    rf"%(?:25(?=(?:25)*{BIBTEX_VERBATIM_CODES})|{BIBTEX_VERBATIM_CODES})"
)


def unpaired_braces(text: str) -> set[int]:
    """Return the positions of the braces in `text` that pair with none.

    Every brace counts, also one after a backslash, as biber counts.
    """
    opened: list[int] = []
    unpaired: set[int] = set()
    for position, char in enumerate(text):
        if char == "{":
            opened.append(position)
        elif char == "}":
            if opened:
                opened.pop()
            else:
                unpaired.add(position)
    return unpaired | set(opened)


def bibtex_text_value(text: str) -> str:
    """Return `text` escaped for a BibTeX text field, braces balanced.

    Escaped braces and unpaired braces become `\\textbraceleft{}` or
    `\\textbraceright{}`; tabs and line breaks become spaces, other C0
    control characters are dropped.
    """
    text = text.translate(BIBTEX_TEXT_CONTROLS)
    if latex_text_pieces(text) is not None:
        escaped = escape_bibtex(text)
    else:
        pieces, start = [], 0
        for match in BIBTEX_ESCAPED_BRACE.finditer(text):
            pieces.append(_escape_chars(
                text[start:match.start()] + match[1], BIBTEX_SPECIALS
            ))
            pieces.append(BIBTEX_TEXT_BRACES[match[2]])
            start = match.end()
        pieces.append(_escape_chars(text[start:], BIBTEX_SPECIALS))
        escaped = "".join(pieces)
    unpaired = unpaired_braces(escaped)
    return "".join(
        BIBTEX_TEXT_BRACES[char] if position in unpaired else char
        for position, char in enumerate(escaped)
    )


def _verbatim_code(char: str) -> str:
    return f"%{ord(char):02X}"


def bibtex_verbatim_value(text: str, exact: bool = True) -> str:
    """Return `text` for a verbatim BibTeX field (DOI, URL).

    Percent-encodes unpaired braces, a backslash before a brace or at
    the end and C0 control characters; with `exact` (a DOI, an id) also
    a `%` that would read as the start of a code, so that
    `read_bibtex_verbatim` undoes exactly what was written.
    """
    unpaired = unpaired_braces(text)
    out = []
    for position, char in enumerate(text):
        after = text[position + 1:position + 2]
        if (position in unpaired or ord(char) < 32
                or (char == "\\" and after in ("", "{", "}"))):
            out.append(_verbatim_code(char))
        elif exact and char == "%" \
                and BIBTEX_BEFORE_CODE.match(text, position + 1):
            out.append("%25")
        else:
            out.append(char)
    return "".join(out)


def read_bibtex_verbatim(value: str) -> str:
    """Undo `bibtex_verbatim_value` on a DOI or URL read from a .bib."""
    return BIBTEX_VERBATIM_ESCAPE.sub(
        lambda match: chr(int(match[0][1:3], 16)), value
    )


def bibtex_field(name: str, value: object) -> str:
    """Return the line `  name = {value},` with a value that reads back.

    DOI and URL are written verbatim, other fields escaped.
    """
    text = str(value)
    if name in BIBTEX_VERBATIM_FIELDS:
        body = bibtex_verbatim_value(text, exact=name == "doi")
    else:
        body = bibtex_text_value(text)
    return f"  {name} = {{{body}}},"
@

Let's verify, for each field and each character the issue and its
review name, that a written entry is parsed back by [[bibtexparser]] as
one entry with every field, each holding the value we meant to write,
and that the value is what the rules above say.
The parsing is the real reader of [[--cited-from]], not a check of our
own, and every test of the writer goes through it ([[_read]]).
For a DOI or URL the value read back, decoded, must be the value given.
The rows of \cref{tab:bibtex-verbatim-escapes} are pinned, and the
inversion is checked on a generated set: every code (and [[25]], in
both cases of its letters) after zero to three [[%25]], between pieces
that stand next to a code in the dangerous cases (a lone [[%]], a
brace, a backslash, a line break, the text of a code).
The paper's id is fixed with a DOI where the DOI is not the field under
test, so that the registry hands no other test's paper back.

