\paragraph{Numbers in the table}
\label{sec:audit-numbers}

The table goes into a document in its own language, so its numbers
must be written the way that language writes them: a confidence of
0.70 is \enquote{0,70} in a Swedish table, and scholar writes a count of
9744 hits as \enquote{9\,744} there and as \enquote{9,744} in English.
Both used to be printed the English way without grouping, which the
typesetter of a Swedish book had to correct by hand
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/262}).  The decimal
mark and the group separator are therefore entries of
[[AUDIT_STRINGS]], and [[audit_number]] is the one formatter for every
confidence in the reason column and every count in the caption: the
category counts, the extra counts, the \enquote{$k$ of $n$ queries} of
the databases sentence and the \enquote{$k$ of $n$ searches} of the
dates sentence.  Years, DOIs, paper ids and dates are identifiers,
not quantities, and never pass through it.

For Swedish we follow \emph{Myndigheternas skrivregler} (Ds 2009:38,
read at
\url{https://www.riksdagen.se/sv/dokument-och-lagar/dokument/departementsserien/myndigheternas-skrivregler_gxb438/html/}).
Its \S\,9.1.6 divides \enquote{mångsiffriga tal} into groups of three
digits counted from the right, marked with a fixed space, \enquote{inte
komma eller punkt}; its \S\,9.5.1 writes amounts as \enquote{1 234:50
kr}, grouping the integer digits three by three, and \S\,6.2 has
\enquote{2 250 kr} and asks for a fixed space so that a number is not
broken across lines; its \S\,9.1.7 makes the comma the decimal sign.
The text itself says only \enquote{mångsiffriga}; grouping every
number from four digits on is scholar's choice, which follows the
four-digit examples of \S\,9.5.1 and \S\,6.2.  The fixed space is
[[\,]], \LaTeX's thin space, which does not break across lines; the
thin rather than a full space is also scholar's choice.  For English
we opened no style source: grouping from four digits on with a comma
is scholar's choice, made to match the Swedish one.  The decimal comma
needs no braces, since the caption is text, not mathematics.

<<review report generation>>=
def audit_number(
    value: float, strings: dict, decimals: int | None = None
) -> str:
    """Write `value` with the decimal mark and digit groups of `strings`.

    Integers (`decimals` None) are grouped in threes from four digits on;
    with `decimals` the value is rounded to that many places.
    """
    if decimals is None:
        text = format(int(value), ",d")
    else:
        text = format(value, f",.{decimals}f")
    return "".join(
        strings["group_separator"] if char == ","
        else strings["decimal_mark"] if char == "."
        else char
        for char in text
    )
@

