\paragraph{When the text says what is cited.}
\label{sec:audit-cited-from}
The rule above reads \enquote{cited} off the screening: a keep is
cited when a human made or confirmed it.  That is the right reading of
a review a human screened, and the wrong one of a review screened by
an agent or a model and written up by a human
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/218}).  The appendix
chapters that issue is about say in their précis that the author has
not reviewed the screening, and once their agents' keeps carry the
agent source, every such table reads \enquote{0 cited} beside a chapter
that cites dozens of the listed works.  The only workaround was to
record the agents' keeps as [[human]], which forges a human decision.
Whether the text cites a work and whether a human screened its record
are two facts; the reason column needs the first, and the second is
already in it, as the confidence of a machine's row.

So [[sessions export --cited-from refs.bib]] hands the export the
works the text cites (\cref{sec:audit-cited-entries} says how a
bibliography entry finds its row), and [[audit_reason]] takes the
answer as [[cited]] for each copy of the work.  Without the option
[[cited]] is [[None]] and every rule above stands unchanged.  With it,
the text decides, whoever decided on the record:
\begin{description}
\item[A cited work that is kept or pending] is cited, order~0.  The
  label names the theme, else the category, and a pending record says
  it is unclassified, so a reader sees that the text cites a work the
  screening never judged.  A machine's row keeps its confidence, so
  who decided stays visible: the caption promises a confidence for
  every machine-classified row, and an agent's keep cited by the text
  is still the agent's classification.  A pending record has no
  classification, so it has no confidence to show either.
\item[A cited work that is discarded] keeps its place and its reason,
  and gets the note \enquote{cited in the text}, after its other notes.
  The text and the screening disagree there, and only the author can
  say which is wrong: making the row cited would hide the exclusion,
  and dropping the citation would hide what the text relies on.  The
  export also lists such works on standard error.
\item[A work the text does not cite] is not cited, even when a human
  kept it.  A kept record with a category falls to the category's
  order, as a machine's keep always did; any other keep, whoever made
  it, reads \enquote{kept, not cited in the text}, under its theme and
  with a machine's confidence, among the other rows
  ([[audit_uncited_keep]]), and the caption counts these rows on their
  own.  The two branches of the rule above that read a human keep as a
  citation are what [[by_screening]] turns off.
\end{description}

Without the option an unreviewed keep without a category is a
candidate, filed by its tags; with it, that reading would leave the
reason column saying less about an agent's keep than about a human's,
although the text treats both alike.  So the label is the same for
both, and the confidence shows who decided.  The rule is a predicate
of its own because the caption needs it as well: it counts the rows the
label is written on.

