\paragraph{Which copy represents the row}
\label{sec:audit-row-copy}

The category order alone let a machine's proposal speak over the
reviewer (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/204}).  An
unreviewed keep from the classifier without tags ranks as pending
(order~5); a human keep tagged only with a note, such as
[[recorded-not-cited]], or [[cited-in-report]] under [[--theme]],
ranks as \enquote{other} (order~6).  So the machine's copy represented
the work, and the row read \enquote{pending, unclassified
(cited-in-report)} for a work a human had kept.  The same happens to a
human keep with a weak category against a machine's stronger one: an
unreviewed \enquote{supports the claim} outranked a human
\enquote{adjacent subtopic}.  It happens to discards as well: an
unreviewed \enquote{supports the claim} keep outranked a human discard
that excluded the work although it qualifies the claim (order~6, see
\cref{sec:audit-table}), so with [[--bearing-only]] the appendix listed
as bearing a work the review had rejected, the contradiction of
issue~\#89 again.  The order says how strongly a copy bears on the
claim; it does not say whose judgement it is, and a proposal nobody
has looked at is not a judgement of the review.

So the confirmed decisions about the work---a human's, or a machine's
that a human confirmed ([[llm_reviewed]], [[agent_reviewed]]), that is,
a keep or a discard whose source is not [[is_unreviewed]]---form a first
tier that represents the row before any other copy, and the category
order decides only within each tier.  An unknown source counts as
unreviewed, as everywhere else (\cref{sec:review-source}), so its
copies are in the second tier, with the pending copies.

Not every confirmed discard is a verdict on the work, though.  A
discard noted as another version of a cited source
([[AUDIT_VERSION_NOTES]], such as [[duplicate-record-of-cited-source]])
says that this \emph{record} is redundant.  A discard under an
exclusion criterion ([[audit_exclusion_criterion]]) can judge the
work: a review's own criteria can be topical, such as
[[unrelated-term-match-other-field]] or [[not-literate-programming]],
and such a discard rejects the work whichever copy carries it.  A few
criteria say instead that the record could not be judged, and only
those are listed in [[AUDIT_RECORD_CRITERIA]]: [[no-abstract]] (the
record has no abstract to judge by) and [[insufficient-information]]
(the record says too little to judge).  They are the criteria in the
sessions at hand whose wording plainly says so; a bespoke tag that
mixes the two, such as one saying the abstract is missing \emph{and}
the work is off-topic, is a verdict on the work.  Neither a version
note nor a record criterion says anything against another copy of the
work, so neither may outrank it: such discards stay in the second
tier, ranked by the order as before, and an unreviewed keep of the same
work, or any other copy, can still represent it.

Two copies of equal order used to fall to the first in the session,
so a machine's unreviewed \enquote{other topic (0.97)} could give the
row where a human had discarded the work as off-topic too.  The tiers
settle that case, since the human's discard is a verdict on the work.
They leave the ties of the second tier: a confirmed discard of the
record and an unreviewed copy can share an order.  A third part of the
key puts the confirmed copy first there as well, so a human's and a
machine's [[no-abstract]] give the row the reviewer's criterion rather
than the machine's with its confidence.  The same part lets a record's
confirmed discard beat an unreviewed copy of the work on equal order:
a human's [[duplicate-record-of-cited-source]] discard (order~6) gives
the row, not a machine's unreviewed keep under a theme, \enquote{ml
(0.80)} (order~6).  That is acceptable because equal order means both
readings place the work alike---both are \enquote{other} rows, below
every row that bears on the claim, so the row's place and
[[--bearing-only]] do not change---and an unreviewed keep is a
candidate, never a citation (\cref{sec:audit-table}), so showing the
reviewer's note instead hides nothing the review cites.  The caption
then counts the row among the duplicates rather than the other hits,
which is what the reviewer recorded.

All of this is one sort key, [[audit_copy_rank]]: the tier, the
order, then whether the copy is unreviewed.  A sort key is a total
preorder, so the row does not depend on the order of the decisions
except between copies equal in all three parts, where the first wins,
as before.  The rank takes the theme names and the note vocabulary
because the exclusion criterion depends on them: under [[--theme]] a
named theme is skipped when the criterion is looked for.  So a discard
tagged [[no-abstract]] and then [[ml]] is a discard of the record when
[[ml]] is a named theme, its criterion being [[no-abstract]], and
without [[--theme]] a discard of the work under the criterion [[ml]],
its last tag.

