The English sentence used to read \enquote{Search date unknown for
\dots}.  The searches it counts are mostly records written before
dates were kept, and \enquote{not recorded} says that more plainly:
the date existed, the session did not keep it
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/275}).  The Swedish
\enquote{Sökdatum saknas} already said so.

\paragraph{Retired searches in the caption}
\label{sec:audit-retired}
The caption's statements about the searches are the coverage statement
of the appendix, and they must agree with the review's method text,
which describes the current searches only.  So the databases sentence,
its notes on failed, incomplete and capped queries and the dates
sentence are all built from the current records ([[current_records]],
\cref{sec:retired-searches}).  Leaving the retired ones out silently
would hide searches that were run and whose papers are in the table, so
one sentence counts them: \enquote{3 earlier searches that have since
been retired are not counted here.}

The rows are untouched: a paper that only a retired search found keeps
its row, its decision and its database code.  If no current search
reached that database, naming it among the databases searched would
claim a search the review no longer counts, and leaving the code
unexplained would leave the reader guessing.  So such a database
([[retired_only_codes]]: a code of the retired records that no current
record has) is explained in the retired sentence instead, code first,
since the code is what the reader looks up: \enquote{\dots; rows coded
WoS (Web of Science) were found only through them}
([[audit_coded_names]]).  A database known only from retired searches
and from no row is not named at all, as a database that answered
nothing is not.  Codes are compared as the shortfalls compare them
([[provider_short_names]]), so the parts of a merged record count each,
and a session without retired records gets the caption it got before.

A semantic search (\cref{sec:ranked-list} in the [[scholar]] module) is
a check on the keyword searches, not a database search, and its own
sentence says so; naming its code in the retired sentence, right after,
as a database the rows were found in would contradict that.  So a
retired semantic search is counted among the retired searches but never
named there.  Its rows keep their code, and the semantic sentence keeps
explaining it: the check was run, whether or not it still counts, and
the sentence's count (\enquote{the 50 works}) comes from every semantic
record, retired or not, since it describes how the rows marked OA-sem
were found.  The sentence appears, as before, when a row carries the
code or a current semantic search exists; a semantic check that is
retired and returned no row in the table is left to the retired count,
since there is then nothing in the table for the sentence to explain.

<<review report generation>>=
def retired_only_codes(
    current: list[SearchRecord], retired: list[SearchRecord]
) -> set[str]:
    """The provider codes of `retired` records that no `current` one has."""
    def codes(records: list[SearchRecord]) -> set[str]:
        return {
            short for record in records
            for short in provider_short_names(record.provider)
        }
    return codes(retired) - codes(current)


def audit_retired_count(
    count: int, strings: dict, databases: str = ""
) -> str:
    """Say that `count` retired searches are not counted, in `strings`.

    `databases` names the databases some rows were found in through
    them only; "" for none.
    """
    one = count == 1
    rows = ""
    if databases:
        rows = strings["retired_rows_one" if one else "retired_rows"].format(
            list=databases
        )
    return strings["retired_one" if one else "retired"].format(
        n=audit_number(count, strings), rows=rows
    )


def audit_retired_sentence(
    rows: list[AuditRow],
    strings: dict,
    current: list[SearchRecord],
    retired: list[SearchRecord],
) -> str:
    """Count the `retired` searches; "" if there are none.

    Databases of `rows` known only from the retired searches are named.
    """
    if not retired:
        return ""
    only_retired = retired_only_codes(current, retired)
    in_rows = [
        short for short in dict.fromkeys(
            provider for row in rows for provider in row.providers
        )
        if short in only_retired and short not in RANKED_SHORT_NAMES
    ]
    return audit_retired_count(
        len(retired), strings, audit_coded_names(in_rows)
    )


def audit_coded_names(shorts: list[str]) -> str:
    """The codes `shorts`, each with its database's full name if it
    differs, in the fixed order of the databases sentence."""
    names = []
    for key, short in PROVIDER_SHORT_NAMES.items():
        if short in shorts:
            full = PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES[key]
            names.append(f"{short} ({full})" if full != short else short)
    names += [
        escape_latex(short) for short in shorts
        if short not in PROVIDER_SHORT_NAMES.values()
    ]
    return ", ".join(dict.fromkeys(names))
@

