The table itself follows the conventions of the appendix it is meant for:
a [[longtable]] with a repeated header and a \enquote{continued} footer,
set in [[\footnotesize]] so four columns fit, with a caption that states
the counts per category so the reader gets the outcome before the rows.
Both the caption and the labels are localised; the label argument becomes
the [[\label]] the surrounding text refers to.

A topic-driven session can hold thousands of records, and a table of all
of them runs to dozens of pages that say \enquote{false hit} over and
over.  [[bearing_only]] therefore lists only the rows that bear on the
claim---cited, supporting, qualifying---and moves the adjacent,
off-topic and unclassified records into the caption as counts, so the
appendix still accounts for every record without printing it.

The \enquote{other} rows are counted by kind, each with a phrase of its
own, because each says something different about the review. The rows
noted as duplicates or other versions of cited sources are bookkeeping,
and the caption says so. The rows excluded under an exclusion criterion
are counted per criterion, most frequent first, under the criterion's
label. The rows excluded despite a bearing category are neither, and
calling them duplicates would misreport the review. What remains is
counted as other hits. The four counts are taken from the merged rows,
so a work is counted once, under the decision its row shows.

With [[cited_works]] (\cref{sec:audit-cited-from}) the counts follow
the text's citations: \enquote{$n$ cited} counts the rows the text
cites, and [[--bearing-only]] lists those with the supporting and
qualifying ones, as before.  The caption then says which rule it used,
in one localised sentence before the databases, because the same
words, \enquote{12 cited}, mean \enquote{a human kept 12} in one table
and \enquote{the text cites 12} in another, and a reader of the
appendix cannot tell them apart otherwise.  The other sentences hold
under either rule: \enquote{other versions of cited sources} are
versions of what the text cites, and an excluded row stays excluded.
The keeps the text does not cite get a count of their own, ahead of
the duplicates: their reason column leads with \enquote{kept, not
cited in the text}, and the caption counts a row by what that column
leads with, so such a keep is neither one of the other hits nor, when
a copy of it lends a version note, a duplicate.
A table exported without the option has no such sentence, and so is
byte for byte what it was.

\paragraph{The unlisted groups of [[--bearing-only]]}
The [[--bearing-only]] caption names what it leaves out as one list:
the adjacent rows, the false hits and whatever the extra counts add.
The extras used to be appended after the pair \enquote{the 3 adjacent
and 5 false hits}, which put the \enquote{and} in the middle of the
list as soon as an extra followed: \enquote{the 3 adjacent and 5 false
hits, 2 unclassified are not listed}
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/262}).  The groups are
now items, and [[audit_joined_list]] joins them with commas and the
language's \enquote{and} before the last, however many extras there
are.  Without extras the pair stays as it was, the two counts sharing
one noun.  With extras the pair cannot share it any more, since
\enquote{the 3 adjacent, 5 false hits and 2 unclassified} reads as if
\enquote{adjacent} were a group of its own; so each of the two gets its
own article and noun, \enquote{the 3 adjacent hits, the 5 false hits
and 2 unclassified} (in Swedish \enquote{de 3 angränsande träffarna,
de 5 felträffarna och 2 ej klassade}).  The extras are the phrases the
full caption uses, without an article.  The full caption's parenthesis
needs no such join: it lists every
count after a colon, separated by commas only, and an extra is one more
item of that list, so the extras keep their place there, each after a
comma.

<<review report generation>>=
def audit_joined_list(items: Sequence[str], strings: dict) -> str:
    """Join `items` with commas and the language's "and" before the last."""
    if len(items) < 2:
        return "".join(items)
    return f"{', '.join(items[:-1])} {strings['and']} {items[-1]}"
@

\paragraph{The confidence sentence}
\label{sec:audit-confidence-sentence}
The caption says whose confidence the reason column gives, and it can
only say it of the rows it lists.  [[audit_confidence_sentence]] looks
at the origins those rows carry ([[AuditRow.confidence_by]],
\cref{sec:audit-table}): only rows a model classified keep the
sentence about the model's confidence; only rows an agent decided get
one about the agent's; both get one sentence naming both; and a table
without any confidence, or a [[--bearing-only]] table whose listed
rows all lack one, gets none, so the sentence about the last column
ends after \enquote{exclusion} with nothing dangling.  The strings
start with the semicolon that joins them to that sentence for the
same reason.

<<review report generation>>=
def audit_confidence_sentence(rows: list[AuditRow], strings: dict) -> str:
    """Say whose confidence the listed `rows` show; "" if none shows one."""
    origins = {row.confidence_by for row in rows} - {None}
    if not origins:
        return ""
    if origins == {AUDIT_CONFIDENCE_LLM}:
        return strings["confidence_llm"]
    if origins == {AUDIT_CONFIDENCE_AGENT}:
        return strings["confidence_agent"]
    return strings["confidence_both"]
@

\paragraph{The double screen}
\label{sec:audit-double-screen}
When some records were screened a second time
(\cref{sec:screenings}), the caption says so after the sentence on the
last column, which the screen explains, and after the cited rule when
there is one, which belongs next to that sentence; then come the
databases.  It says how many hits were screened again, by what kind of
screener and how blind, each pair's agreement on kept/discarded with
kappa and its two indices, and the conflicts with how many a human
resolved.  The agreement module writes the sentences
([[audit_screening_sentence]], \cref{sec:agreement-reports} in that
module), from the numbers [[sessions show]] prints, and counts the
screened hits by the rows' works, which is why it gets the rows; the
import is local, since that module imports this one.  A session
without screenings gets the empty string and so the caption it got
before.  The rows are not touched: a second screener's verdict is no
citation and no reason (\cref{sec:agreement-reports} says why).

The words are entries of [[AUDIT_STRINGS]], like the rest of the
caption.  The screener's kind is named twice over: as who screened
(\enquote{by an agent}, with the number when a kind has several
screeners) and, with its name, as one side of a pair (\enquote{agent
a-2}).  The Swedish sentence puts the count first, \enquote{17 av 18
träffar granskades en andra gång (17 granskade av en agent, blint
enligt agentens egen uppgift)}, so that its two \enquote{av} do not
read alike, and every ground is an adverb that fits after
\enquote{granskade av en agent}; the declared ground names whose
declaration it is ([[screener_owner]], the genitive of the kind, and
[[screener_owners]] for several screeners).  How blind a screen was is
one phrase per ground the record keeps, so that a reader never takes a
declaration for a guarantee: blind by the prompt Scholar built, blind
in Scholar's review screens, blind by the screener's own declaration
(naming the kind, in both languages), not blind, or not
recorded.  A count of one takes the singular forms ([[hit_one]],
[[record_one]], [[screened_again_one]], [[screened_group_one]],
[[resolved_one]]).  The undefined-kappa reasons of the agreement
module are translated by their English text.

