The separator (\cref{sec:non-latin-text}) is checked the same way: a
title with Chinese written against Latin words, as the issue reported,
and a Cyrillic one written the same way.  Under pdf\LaTeX{} the
markers stand apart from \enquote{Mac OS X}; under Xe\LaTeX{} the
Cyrillic title comes out as written, with no space added.  The
Xe\LaTeX{} check uses the Cyrillic title, since DejaVu Serif has no
Chinese glyphs, so the Chinese row prints only its Latin words there.
A pdf\LaTeX{} host that defines [[\scholarnonlatin]] itself, with the
T2A definition the fragment header suggests, and the empty separator
beside it, prints the Cyrillic title as written too
(\cref{sec:non-latin-definition}).

The marker must also stay apart when an earlier piece of scholar output
in the same document has defined [[\scholarnonlatin]] already, which
is the normal case in a book: a first fragment whose non-Latin text
touches no letter, then a second whose text does; or a scholar
[[.bib]] whose [[@preamble]] biblatex reads at the start of the
document, before any fragment, both one written now and one of the
older form that defines only [[\scholarnonlatin]].  Each of these
documents is compiled and its text read back.

