\subsection{Works the text cites}
\label{sec:audit-cited-entries}

[[sessions export --cited-from refs.bib]] lets the text decide which
rows of the audit table are cited (\cref{sec:audit-cited-from}).  The
command reads each bibliography entry into a [[Paper]]
(\cref{sec:export-cited-from} in the CLI chapter); this subsection
finds the entry's work among the session's decisions and tells the
author where
the text and the screening disagree.

\paragraph{Finding an entry's work.}
A bibliography entry is a work returned from outside the session, the
same question the semantic check asks of a search
(\cref{sec:semantic-check}), so it is answered by the same lookup,
[[matching_entries]] over a [[session_match_index]] of the one
session.  An entry matches by DOI, the decisions' version-link DOIs
included, so a text citing the published version finds the preprint
the session adopted in its place; otherwise by title and first author,
[[audit_work_key]], and then only if [[plausibly_same_work]] lets the
match stand: two DOIs that differ without a version link, or years
more than one apart, are two works.  A third matcher would have been
easy to write and would have drifted from the check: the same entry
could then be \enquote{in the session} for one command and not for
the other.

A matched decision names its row by its [[audit_work_key]], the key
[[collect_audit_rows]] merges copies on, so every copy of the work is
told that the text cites it, and the row is the one the screening's
best copy gives (\cref{sec:audit-row-copy}): a human's discard of the
work still represents its row, and is then the disagreement listed
below.  One entry can name more than one row, when the session holds
copies of the work whose titles differ and that the audit table
therefore does not merge; each of them is cited.  [[matched_by]] says
how the entry was found, [[doi]] if any of its matches was by DOI,
since that is the stronger evidence, which is what the export's count
of DOI and title matches reports.

An entry may be read in more than one way, and then it matches what
any of its readings matches.  The case that needs it is a surname of
several words.  [[audit_work_key]] reads the surname of
[[Natacha Muriel Lopez Gallucci]] as its last word, [[Gallucci]], and
that of [[Lopez Gallucci, Natacha Muriel]] as what precedes the comma,
[[Lopez Gallucci]], so a session that stored one form does not match an
entry read in the other.  The command therefore passes the entry
given name first and, when that names another surname, surname first
(\cref{sec:export-cited-from} in the CLI chapter).  This was not
hypothetical: a bibliography exported from a real session missed two
of its own 1\,041 works on exactly this, both stored surname first.

