\paragraph{A second chance: editions and chapter numbers.}
Two shapes of record that providers produce defeat [[audit_work_key]]
although the bibliography and the session name the same work
(issue~\#272).  A chapter of an edited book may come back with its
number in front of an upper-case heading and no authors:
\enquote{7 THE QUIET LEDGER}, while the text cites
\enquote{The Quiet Ledger} by its author, with a DOI the record lacks.
And a book may come back with its edition in the title,
\enquote{Gardening with Lichens, 3rd edition}, while the bibliography
writes the title alone and puts the edition in its [[edition]] field.
The keys, [[7thequietledger]] against [[thequietledger]] and
[[gardeningwithlichens3rdedition]] against
[[gardeningwithlichens]], differ, so the text's citation went unseen:
the row read \enquote{kept, not cited in the text} and the entry was
reported as not held, the opposite of what the session records.

The repair is a second lookup on the titles with those two parts set
aside, tried for an entry that neither its DOI nor its work key found,
and for an entry whose edition the work key found only on another
edition (below).  It is not built into [[audit_work_key]], which also merges
the audit table's rows and finds the possible copies: there, the
second and third edition of a book are two works and must stay two
rows.  Nor does the sessions check (\cref{sec:semantic-check}) use
it, although it shares the first lookup.  The check's works are what
a search by meaning returned, near-namesakes by design, and a looser
title would count some of them as held, hiding the very gap the check
is run to find; a bibliography is the author's own list of works
known to be cited, so the risk there is the missed match, not the
false one.  The loosened match still has to pass
[[plausibly_same_work]] (years at most one apart, no two different
DOIs), and the authors are compared as in the first lookup: a session
record without authors matches by its title alone, records with
authors on both sides need the same first-author surname.

\paragraph{What is set aside.}
An edition is set aside only as a phrase at the very end of the title:
an ordinal, as digits with [[st]], [[nd]], [[rd]] or [[th]]
([[2nd]]) or as a word from \enquote{first} to \enquote{tenth},
optionally \enquote{revised} or \enquote{rev.}, then \enquote{edition}
or \enquote{ed.}, set off by a comma, a colon, white space or
parentheses: \enquote{, 2nd edition}, \enquote{(2nd ed.)},
\enquote{second edition}, \enquote{3rd rev.\ ed.}.  Requiring the
ordinal keeps titles such as \enquote{The Special Edition} whole, and
a phrase that would leave no title is not one.  Nor is a phrase after
\enquote{the}, \enquote{a} or \enquote{an}: there the edition is
what the title is about, \enquote{The first edition},
\enquote{Using the 2nd edition}, and setting it aside would leave
\enquote{The} or \enquote{Using the}.  Digit ordinals have no upper
bound (\enquote{21st edition} is one); the words stop at
\enquote{tenth}.  The edition the phrase names is kept for the
comparison below.

A chapter number is set aside only from a record without authors,
the shape the chapter records come in, and only before a title
without a single lower-case letter that has an upper-case one: a
number, a full stop or not, and white space before a heading in
capitals.  Older records with authors can be in capitals too, and
there a leading number is part of the title: \enquote{2 NEW MOSSES
FROM THE ANDES} by one author is not the paper \enquote{New Mosses
from the Andes} by the same author a year later, and
\enquote{404 NOT FOUND} is not \enquote{Not Found}.  A title with a
lower-case letter keeps its number, since there the number is part of the title: \enquote{2001: A Voyage},
\enquote{12 Angry Lighthouses}; so does a title that is only a number,
\enquote{1984}, which has no white space after it.  A heading
in a script without case keeps its number too; nothing tells a chapter
number there from a title's.  The edition goes first, so that a
heading such as \enquote{7 THE QUIET LEDGER, 2nd edition} loses both.
The rules are applied to both titles alike, the chapter rule by each
side's own authors, since a bibliography may copy either shape from
the record it was made from.

<<review report generation>>=
EDITION_ORDINALS = {
    "first": 1, "second": 2, "third": 3, "fourth": 4, "fifth": 5,
    "sixth": 6, "seventh": 7, "eighth": 8, "ninth": 9, "tenth": 10,
}
EDITION_PHRASE = re.compile(
    r"[\s,;:.(\[\u2013\u2014-]\s*[(\[]?\s*"
    r"(?P<ordinal>\d+\s*(?:st|nd|rd|th)|"
    + "|".join(EDITION_ORDINALS) +
    r")\s+(?:(?:revised|rev\.?)\s+)?(?:edition|ed\.?)\s*[)\]]?\s*\.?\s*$",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
EDITION_SEPARATORS = " ,;:.-\u2013\u2014"
EDITION_OBJECT_WORDS = {"the", "a", "an"}
CHAPTER_NUMBER = re.compile(r"^\s*\d+\.?\s+(?=\S)")


def edition_number(text: str | None) -> int | str | None:
    """The edition `text` names: an integer when it reads as one.

    `2`, `2nd`, `Second` and `second edition` all give 2; anything else
    is returned as its case-folded letters and digits, so that it is
    equal only to itself; None for no edition.
    """
    written = " ".join(str(text or "").split())
    if not written:
        return None
    digits = re.match(r"\d+", written)
    if digits:
        return int(digits.group())
    word = written.split()[0].casefold()
    if word in EDITION_ORDINALS:
        return EDITION_ORDINALS[word]
    return "".join(c for c in written.casefold() if c.isalnum()) or None


def loose_title(
    title: str | None, *, authorless: bool = False,
) -> tuple[str, int | str | None]:
    """`title` without a trailing edition phrase or a chapter number.

    The chapter number is set aside only for a record `authorless`.
    Returns the shortened title and the edition the phrase named (None
    without one).
    """
    text = clean_metadata_text(title or "").strip()
    edition = None
    phrase = EDITION_PHRASE.search(text)
    before = text[:phrase.start()] if phrase else ""
    words = re.findall(r"\w+", before.casefold())
    if phrase and words and words[-1] not in EDITION_OBJECT_WORDS:
        edition = edition_number(phrase.group("ordinal"))
        text = before.rstrip(EDITION_SEPARATORS)
    number = CHAPTER_NUMBER.match(text)
    if (authorless and number and not any(c.islower() for c in text)
            and any(c.isupper() for c in text)):
        text = text[number.end():]
    return text, edition
@

\paragraph{Matching on the loose title.}
The session's loose titles are indexed once, the first time an entry
needs them, so a bibliography whose entries all matched costs nothing
more.  Each decision is kept under its loose title's letters and
digits, with the edition its title named and its first author's
surname, empty for a record without authors.

An entry's edition is its [[edition]] field when it has one, else the
edition its own title names.  When both sides name an edition they
must be the same, else the works are two editions and not matched;
when only one side names one, they match, since that is the common
case: the bibliography has the field, the record the phrase.  The
field is read by [[edition_number]], so [[2]], [[2nd]] and
[[second]] are one edition; a field it cannot read as a number
(\enquote{Revised}) equals no phrase, which only ever names a number,
and the match is then refused rather than guessed.

If the loose title finds decisions of more than one work, the entry
is not matched: a heading and a book that both reduce to the entry's
title cannot both be the cited work, and choosing one would be a
guess.  Such an entry is listed as ambiguous, with the titles it would
have matched, so the author can settle it.  Here two decisions are one
work when they agree on the loose title, the edition their titles name
and the first author's surname (empty without authors), not when their
[[audit_work_key]]s are equal: \enquote{Gardening with Lichens, 3rd
edition} and \enquote{Gardening with lichens (3rd ed.)} by one author
are two spellings of one book, and the entry matches both, while a
heading without authors and a book with them, or the second and the
third edition, stay apart.

<<review report generation>>=
CHECK_BY_LOOSE_TITLE = "title without edition or chapter number"


def _loose_title_index(
    session: ReviewSession,
) -> dict[str, list[tuple[ReviewDecision, int | str | None, str]]]:
    """Index the session's decisions by their loose titles' letters."""
    index: dict[str, list] = {}
    for decision in session.decisions:
        authorless = _authorless(decision.paper)
        title, edition = loose_title(
            decision.paper.title, authorless=authorless
        )
        letters, _ = audit_work_key(Paper(title=title, authors=[]))
        if not letters:
            continue
        surname = "" if authorless else audit_work_key(decision.paper)[1]
        index.setdefault(letters, []).append((decision, edition, surname))
    return index


def _authorless(paper: Paper) -> bool:
    """Whether `paper` names no author."""
    return not any((author or "").strip() for author in paper.authors)


def entry_edition(
    readings: Sequence[Paper], edition: str | None,
) -> int | str | None:
    """The edition an entry names: its field, else its title's phrase."""
    field_edition = edition_number(edition)
    if field_edition is not None:
        return field_edition
    return loose_title(readings[0].title)[1]


def match_loose_title(
    key: str,
    readings: Sequence[Paper],
    edition: str | None,
    index: dict[str, list[tuple[ReviewDecision, int | str | None, str]]],
    *,
    same_edition: bool = False,
) -> CitedEntry:
    """Match one bibliography entry on its loose title (#272).

    The second chance of `match_cited_entries`, for an entry neither
    its DOI nor its work key found; with `same_edition`, only records
    whose titles name the edition the entry names.
    """
    wanted = entry_edition(readings, edition)
    matched: dict[int, tuple[ReviewDecision, tuple]] = {}
    for reading in readings:
        title, _ = loose_title(reading.title,
                               authorless=_authorless(reading))
        letters, _ = audit_work_key(Paper(title=title, authors=[]))
        surname = audit_work_key(reading)[1]
        for decision, held_edition, held_surname in index.get(letters, []):
            if held_surname and held_surname != surname:
                continue
            if same_edition and (wanted is None or held_edition != wanted):
                continue
            if None not in (wanted, held_edition) and wanted != held_edition:
                continue
            if plausibly_same_work(reading, decision.paper):
                matched[id(decision)] = (
                    decision, (held_edition, held_surname),
                )
    if wanted is not None and any(
        group[0] == wanted for _, group in matched.values()
    ):
        matched = {
            ident: match for ident, match in matched.items()
            if match[1][0] == wanted
        }
    works = list(dict.fromkeys(
        audit_work_key(decision.paper) for decision, _ in matched.values()
    ))
    titles = list(dict.fromkeys(
        decision.paper.title or "" for decision, _ in matched.values()
    ))
    title = readings[0].title or ""
    if len({group for _, group in matched.values()}) > 1:
        return CitedEntry(key, title, None, [], ambiguous=titles)
    if not works:
        return CitedEntry(key, title, None, [])
    return CitedEntry(key, title, CHECK_BY_LOOSE_TITLE, works,
                      loose_titles=titles)
