\paragraph{The edition the entry names comes first.}
A bibliography that gives an edition says which book it cites, and
that outranks the bare title.  A session may hold the first edition as
\enquote{Reading Tide Tables} (1999) and the second as
\enquote{Reading Tide Tables, 2nd edition} (2000); an entry for the
second edition, [[edition = {2}]], 2000, has the first edition's work
key, and the years are one apart, so the first lookup finds the first
edition and the second edition's row read \enquote{kept, not cited}.
So when the entry names an edition and the first lookup found it only
by title, on records whose titles name no edition or another one, the
records whose titles name exactly that edition are looked up on the
loose title, under the same guards, and if they are one work, they are
the match.  A DOI match is left alone, since the DOI is the identity,
and so is a title match on a record that names the edition itself; an
entry without an edition keeps the first lookup's answer.  The same
order holds inside the loose lookup ([[match_loose_title]] above):
when some of its candidates name the entry's edition, the candidates
that name none are dropped, so an entry for the third edition does not
become ambiguous between the third edition and a bare title.

