\subsection{Spreadsheet-safe cells}
\label{sec:spreadsheet-safe}

The CSV is the export people open in a spreadsheet program to sort and
filter a screen (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/285}),
and much of its text was written by someone else: titles,
abstracts, venues and author names come from the providers, reasons
from a classifier or an agent, tags from the screeners.  OWASP's page
on CSV injection
(\url{https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/CSV_Injection}, which
redirected to \url{https://community.owasp.org/attacks/CSV_Injection}
when it was read on 2026-11-20) says that when Microsoft Excel or
LibreOffice Calc opens a CSV, \enquote{any cells starting with = will
be interpreted by the software as a formula}, and it lists the
characters no cell should begin with: [[=]], [[+]], [[-]], [[@]], tab
(0x09), carriage return (0x0D), line feed (0x0A), and the full-width
(double-byte) variants of the first four (U+FF1D, U+FF0B, U+FF0D,
U+FF20), \enquote{which may be interpreted as formulas in some
locales}.  Those eleven characters are [[SPREADSHEET_FORMULA_STARTS]],
no more and no fewer.  The page names no case of white space or a
quote before one of them (it lists tab, carriage return and line feed
as such characters themselves), so a cell beginning with a space and
then [[=]] is left as it is.

The page gives two ways to neutralise a cell.  One prepends a single
quote, together with wrapping the cell in double quotes and doubling
the double quotes inside it, which [[csv.writer]] already does for a
cell that needs it.  The other, offered as an \enquote{Excel-resistant
mitigation}, puts a tab in front instead.  We take the single quote:
the same page lists the tab among the characters no cell should begin
with, and it says of the tab that it \enquote{remains part of the
underlying data}, which is as true of the quote.

The page applies its first remedy to \emph{each} field: wrap it in
double quotes and put the quote in front.  Scholar narrows that, and
the narrowing is its own choice, not the page's: only a field that
begins with one of the eleven characters (or with the quote) gets the
prefix, and the double quotes are left to [[csv.writer]]'s minimal
rule, which quotes a field only when it holds the delimiter, a double
quote or a line break.  Two reasons.  A prefix on every field would
change every cell, so a script reading the safe file would have to undo
all of them, and a field that never began like a formula would no
longer read as it is stored; with the narrow rule every other cell is
the stored text, and the undo rule below is exact.  And quoting every
field bought nothing in the one program tried: LibreOffice Calc
26.2.6.3 (2026-11-20, comma import) made the same formula of a cell
[[=1+2]] whether the file quoted only where needed or quoted every
field, so the quoting alone does not neutralise anything there, the
prefix does.

The page also states the limits.  Microsoft Excel \enquote{may remove quotes or escape
characters from CSV cells when a file is saved and re-opened}, so that
\enquote{previously escaped formulas may become active again}, and
\enquote{There is no universal CSV sanitization strategy that is safe
for all spreadsheet applications and all downstream consumers.}  It
also warns that a field separator or a quote inside a field can start
a new cell with a dangerous character in the middle of the input.
[[csv.writer]] quotes every field that holds a comma, a double quote or
a line break, so a reader that splits on the comma keeps the field
whole (the round trip below reads such fields back); a program that
splits the file on another character, such as the semicolon the page
names, can still begin a cell inside a field, and the option does not
guard against that.  It was tried: LibreOffice Calc read the comma file
with the semicolon as separator (as a spreadsheet set up for a
language that writes decimal commas may) and made a formula of the
text after the semicolon in a field holding [[a;=1+2]], exactly as from
the raw file.  The protection holds for a program that reads the file
with the separator it was written with, and the help of both commands
says so.

What a spreadsheet program then shows was tried once, with LibreOffice
Calc 26.2.6.3, converting a test file to its own format without a
window on 2026-11-20 (comma as separator, the import's other settings
as they came): the cell [[=1+2]] became a formula with the value~3 and
[[-2]] the number $-2$, while [['=1+2]], [['-2]] and [['@SUM(1;2)]]
stayed text, the quote shown as part of it.  The same program found
formulas in the cells of a test export that begin with [[=]], and none
when the same export was written with the option.  Microsoft Excel was
not tried.

The prefix changes the data: the cell holds [['=...]] where the session
holds [[=...]].  So it is an option, off by default, and a script
should read the default file, which holds the stored text unchanged.
For a script that reads the safe file all the same, the change can be
undone exactly: a cell that begins with the quote has had exactly one
quote put in front, so dropping the first character of every cell that
begins with [[']], in any column, gives back the stored text.  A cell
of a numeric column that is left alone never begins with a quote (it
is a number, below), so the rule needs no list of columns.  That rule
holds only because a cell that already begins with a quote gets one
more as well; otherwise a title stored as [['=x]], which needs no
prefix, would come back as [[=x]].  The quote is not added to the
characters that trigger the prefix for its own sake; it is escaped so
that the change stays reversible.

<<review report generation>>=
SPREADSHEET_FORMULA_STARTS = (
    "=", "+", "-", "@", "\t", "\r", "\n",
    "\uff1d", "\uff0b", "\uff0d", "\uff20",
)
SPREADSHEET_TEXT_PREFIX = "'"


def spreadsheet_safe_cell(value):
    """Return `value` as a cell a spreadsheet program reads as text.

    A string that begins with one of `SPREADSHEET_FORMULA_STARTS`, or
    with the prefix `'` itself, gets one `'` in front; any other value
    is returned unchanged. To undo it, drop the first character of
    every such cell that begins with `'`.
    """
    if isinstance(value, str) and value.startswith(
        (*SPREADSHEET_FORMULA_STARTS, SPREADSHEET_TEXT_PREFIX)
    ):
        return SPREADSHEET_TEXT_PREFIX + value
    return value
@

A number gets no prefix.  The [[year]] and [[llm_confidence]] columns
normally hold a number or nothing (the paper's year and the confidence
formatted with three decimals), and a prefix would turn a number into
text, which is not what a reader who sorts the column wants; a
negative confidence, which only a hand-edited file holds, would
otherwise get one, since it begins with [[-]].  But the column's name
does not make its cell a number: a session file can hold a year as
text, [["year": "=1+2"]], and [[Paper.from_dict]] keeps it as it is
(tried), so the export wrote [[=1+2]] into the year column, and
LibreOffice made a formula of such a cell in a test file.  So a cell of a numeric column is
left alone only when it is an [[int]] or a [[float]], or a string
[[float()]] reads and that equals its own [[strip()]]; anything else
goes through [[spreadsheet_safe_cell]] like a text cell.  The second
condition is there because [[float()]] ignores white space around the
number: it reads [["\t5"]] as 5, but that cell begins with a tab, which
is one of the eleven characters.  A string [[float()]] reads never begins with a
quote, which is what keeps the undo rule above exact.

<<review report generation>>=
def spreadsheet_safe_number(value):
    """Return `value` as a cell of a numeric column.

    An int or a float, or a string `float()` reads and that has no
    white space around it, is returned unchanged; anything else, such
    as a year stored as text, goes through `spreadsheet_safe_cell`.
    """
    if isinstance(value, (int, float)):
        return value
    try:
        float(value)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return spreadsheet_safe_cell(value)
    if value != value.strip():
        return spreadsheet_safe_cell(value)
    return value
@

The writer below learns where the numeric columns are from the header
row, by name, so every other column is text, including columns added
later.  It wraps a [[csv.writer]] rather than
replacing it, so [[generate_csv_report]] keeps building its rows as
before and only the line that makes the writer differs.

<<review report generation>>=
CSV_NUMERIC_COLUMNS = (
    "year", "best_rank", "providers_count", "llm_confidence",
)


class SpreadsheetSafeWriter:
    """A `csv.writer` whose text cells a spreadsheet reads as text.

    The first row written is the header: the cells of the columns it
    names in `numeric_columns` go through `spreadsheet_safe_number`,
    all other cells through `spreadsheet_safe_cell`.
    """

    def __init__(self, writer, numeric_columns):
        self.writer = writer
        self.numeric_columns = frozenset(numeric_columns)
        self.numeric_positions = None

    def writerow(self, row):
        """Write `row`, its text cells made safe."""
        if self.numeric_positions is None:
            self.numeric_positions = {
                position for position, name in enumerate(row)
                if name in self.numeric_columns
            }
        return self.writer.writerow([
            spreadsheet_safe_number(cell)
            if position in self.numeric_positions
            else spreadsheet_safe_cell(cell)
            for position, cell in enumerate(row)
        ])
@

Let's verify the rule on single cells first: each of the eleven
characters at the start gets the prefix, a cell that begins with the
quote gets a second one, and a cell with such a character later, with
a space before it, or that is empty or not a string, is left alone.

<<test functions>>=
class TestSpreadsheetSafeCell:
  TRIGGERS = ["=", "+", "-", "@", "\t", "\r", "\n",
              "\uff1d", "\uff0b", "\uff0d", "\uff20"]

  def test_each_trigger_gets_the_prefix(self):
    for start in self.TRIGGERS:
      assert spreadsheet_safe_cell(f"{start}1+2") == f"'{start}1+2"

  def test_the_prefix_itself_is_doubled(self):
    assert spreadsheet_safe_cell("'=1+2") == "''=1+2"
    assert spreadsheet_safe_cell("'quoted") == "''quoted"

  def test_other_cells_are_left_alone(self):
    for value in ["a=b", "x-y + z", " =1+2", "", "Plain", 2021, None]:
      assert spreadsheet_safe_cell(value) == value

  def test_a_numeric_cell_is_left_alone_only_when_it_is_a_number(self):
    for value in [2021, 0.25, -0.5, "2021", "0.250", "-0.500", "", None]:
      assert spreadsheet_safe_number(value) == value
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number("=1+2") == "'=1+2"
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number("-1+2") == "'-1+2"
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number("'1999") == "''1999"

  def test_a_number_with_white_space_around_it_is_text(self):
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number("\t5") == "'\t5"
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number("\n5") == "'\n5"
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number(" 5") == " 5"
    assert spreadsheet_safe_number("5\n") == "5\n"
@

Then the whole file.  One discarded record carries the same starting
character in every text column the review fills from outside (title,
first author, venue, abstract, tags, the classifier's and the agent's
reasons); the default file must hold the text as stored and the safe
file the same text behind one quote, while the year and the confidence
stay as they are in both, also a confidence with a minus sign, which a
hand-edited file can hold.  The per-decision CSV has no notes column,
so a note tag stands for the screeners' notes.  The data is invented.

<<test functions>>=
class TestSpreadsheetSafeCsv:
  TEXT_COLUMNS = ["title", "authors", "venue", "abstract", "tags",
                  "llm_reason", "agent_reason"]

  def _decision(self, start, title=None):
    return ReviewDecision(
      paper=Paper(
        title=title or f"{start}Sum of parts",
        authors=[f"{start}Ada Example", "Bo Sample"], year=2021,
        venue=f"{start}Journal of Examples",
        abstract=f"{start}An abstract that begins oddly.",
      ),
      provider="openalex", status=DecisionStatus.DISCARDED,
      tags=[f"{start}duplicate-record-of-cited-source"],
      source=ReviewSource.AGENT_UNREVIEWED, llm_confidence=0.25,
      llm_reason=f"{start}model reason", agent_reason=f"{start}agent reason",
    )

  def _rows(self, tmp_path, decisions, **options):
    import csv
    session = ReviewSession(
      query="q", providers=["openalex"], timestamp=datetime(2026, 11, 20),
    )
    session.decisions = decisions
    path = tmp_path / f"r{len(list(tmp_path.iterdir()))}.csv"
    generate_csv_report(session, path, **options)
    with path.open(newline="", encoding="utf-8") as f:
      return list(csv.reader(f)), path.read_bytes()

  def test_every_text_column_gets_the_prefix(self, tmp_path):
    for start in TestSpreadsheetSafeCell.TRIGGERS:
      decisions = [self._decision(start)]
      (raw_header, raw), _ = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions)
      (safe_header, safe), _ = self._rows(
        tmp_path, decisions, spreadsheet_safe=True)
      assert safe_header == raw_header
      raw, safe = dict(zip(raw_header, raw)), dict(zip(raw_header, safe))
      for column in self.TEXT_COLUMNS:
        assert raw[column].startswith(start), (start, column)
        assert safe[column] == "'" + raw[column], (start, column)
      assert safe["year"] == raw["year"] == "2021"
      assert safe["llm_confidence"] == raw["llm_confidence"] == "0.250"

  def test_numeric_columns_keep_a_leading_minus(self, tmp_path):
    decision = self._decision("")
    decision.llm_confidence = -0.5  # out of range, as a hand edit leaves it
    (header, raw), _ = self._rows(tmp_path, [decision])
    (_, safe), _ = self._rows(tmp_path, [decision], spreadsheet_safe=True)
    confidence = header.index("llm_confidence")
    assert safe[confidence] == raw[confidence] == "-0.500"

  def test_a_character_later_in_a_cell_is_left_alone(self, tmp_path):
    decision = self._decision("", title="Gains = losses - costs, + more")
    raw, _ = self._rows(tmp_path, [decision])
    safe, _ = self._rows(tmp_path, [decision], spreadsheet_safe=True)
    assert safe == raw

  def test_the_change_can_be_undone(self, tmp_path):
    decisions = [self._decision(start)
                 for start in TestSpreadsheetSafeCell.TRIGGERS]
    decisions.append(self._decision("", title="'=already quoted, once"))
    for year in ["=1+2", "'1999"]:
      decisions.append(self._decision("", title=f"Year {year} as text"))
      decisions[-1].paper.year = year
    raw, _ = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions)
    safe, _ = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions, spreadsheet_safe=True)
    undone = [[cell[1:] if cell.startswith("'") else cell for cell in row]
              for row in safe]
    assert undone == raw
    title = raw[0].index("title")
    assert [row[title] for row in safe if row[title].startswith("''")] == [
      "''=already quoted, once"]

  def test_a_text_year_gets_the_prefix(self, tmp_path):
    decisions = [self._decision("", title="One"),
                 self._decision("", title="Two")]
    decisions[0].paper.year, decisions[1].paper.year = "=1+2", "'1999"
    (header, *raw), _ = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions)
    (_, *safe), _ = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions, spreadsheet_safe=True)
    year = header.index("year")
    assert [row[year] for row in raw] == ["=1+2", "'1999"]
    assert [row[year] for row in safe] == ["'=1+2", "''1999"]

  def test_the_default_is_the_stored_text(self, tmp_path):
    decisions = [self._decision("=")]
    (_, raw), default_bytes = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions)
    _, off_bytes = self._rows(tmp_path, decisions, spreadsheet_safe=False)
    assert default_bytes == off_bytes
    assert "=Sum of parts" in raw
    assert b",=Sum of parts," in default_bytes
